# hwut-feature-doc.dat -- WHAT THE FEATURES ARE (spec 43, intend/43 T-c,
# T-g, T-p, T-s). One block per feature, headed '[[<id>]]' -- or
# '[[<id> to <audience>, ...]]', '[[<id> not <audience>, ...]]',
# '[[<id>:retired]]'; '[[CUSTOMERS]]' declares the audiences. A block: an
# optional 'documents:' line (paths from this file's directory, ','
# between), then the prose -- its first line the title, the rest the doc.

[[delivery]]
Delivery -- hwut reaches a user's machine: wheel, pipx, Debian package, image

[[demo]]
The demonstrations: each language port shows hwut's features from that language -- one owner per port, aggregating '+cover' (intend/43 T-j, T-k; intend/44).

[[adm]]
Administration Tools

[[auxiliary]]
Testing the auxiliary directory mutex: one live holder, holder identity,

[[bin]]
The Launcher Roof

[[bookkeeper]]
Bookkeeper -- Maintaining Results and Configuration Info

[[test_run_info]]
Test run info -- a book entry alive, and the member state

[[compare]]
Testing the comparison functionality

[[edit_operations]]
Testing the comparison functionality

[[compare-engine]]
Testing the comparison functionality

[[feeder]]
Feeders to UI and the like

[[reading]]
Testing the comparison functionality

[[potpourri]]
Equivalence check and association of potpourri lines

[[solver]]
Solving the constraint pairing problem

[[coverage]]
Testing the coverage component: the homogeneous record, its interval

[[operations]]
Testing test_run configuration, store, report, session, component hygiene.

[[consume]]
Testing the three operations: equivalence check, difference display, accept.

[[interaction]]
Testing the interaction seam: the feed session, the merge loop, the TUI tier and the service faces.

[[operations-run]]
Testing provision: the stages, the one answer shape, the planners, the build tool.

[[exploration]]
Exploration -- Reading Specifications, Selecting Test Cases

[[plan]]
Plan -- Determined, Printed, Executed

[[orchestrator-run]]
Run -- The Outer Face, the Report Stream

[[scheduler]]
Scheduler -- Admission, Dispatch, the Frame

[[procsitter]]
Testing the platform-independent procsitter: resource caps, attribution,

[[protocol]]
Protocol -- the event vocabulary, the line grammar, the event log

[[services]]
Services -- The Command Line Faces

[[config]]
The Configuration Faces

[[features]]
The Feature Faces -- the Central File of Features

[[labels]]
The Label Faces -- Sets of Test Runs

[[keyed]]
The Keyed Merge Session

[[hwut_pype]]
Testing the pype line-matching language: parser, pattern tokens, mode

[[test_writing_support-python]]
The HOCON Subset Parser given to Python Test Authors

[[one-version]]
documents: adm/version.py, adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
Wheel, Debian package and image carry the version of 'adm/version.py', and the same set of faces on PATH.
spec 24; 'adm/packaging/build.py'.

[[one-requirement-list]]
documents: adm/requirement-list.txt, adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
The wheel's dependencies and the package's Depends are the census requirements of 'adm/requirement-list.txt', and nothing else.
spec 24; 'services/lib/requirements.py'.

[[wheel-installs]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
'pip install hwut' into an empty virtual environment makes every face callable and 'hwut.help' answer.

[[pipx-installs]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
'pipx install hwut' does the same, isolated from the system.

[[deb-installs]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
'apt install ./hwut_<version>_all.deb' on a fresh Debian stable or Ubuntu LTS installs hwut system-wide, its requirements from the distribution.

[[deb-removes]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
'apt remove hwut' leaves no file of hwut behind.

[[image-runs]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
'podman run --rm -v $PWD:/work -w /work <image> hwut.help' answers with nothing of hwut installed on the host.

[[image-owner]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/24-installation-packages.txt
Files a run in the image writes into the mounted tree belong to the calling user.

[[help-names-missing]]
documents: services/help.py, adm/requirement-list.txt
On a machine that lacks a requirement, 'hwut.help' names it and how to install it, in every form.
'hwut.help --help', EXIT STATUS 1; 'services/lib/requirements.py'.

[[demo-green]]
documents: services/lib/support/core.py
In every form the demonstration tree runs green: six test cases, six ok.
'hwut.demo.python <dir>' writes it (spec 24, ruling 5).

[[bundle-self-applying]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
'bundle.sh --self' writes one shell script, the applier followed by the bundle unchanged, which applied at the receiving root lands every member intact -- deletions and modes included -- and which 'git apply' still reads.
Usage, '--self' and '--delete'.

[[bundle-classifies]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
The applier creates NEW members, skips IDENTICAL ones, refuses CHANGED ones unless '--force', touches nothing under '--check', and says 'already applied' on a rerun.
Usage, '--self'.

[[bundle-manifest-proved]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
Every member is proved against the bundle's sha256 manifest before the tree is touched; a bundle altered after it was written is refused.
Usage, '--self' ('off the # sha256 manifest').

[[bundle-wrong-root-refused]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
A directory where no member stands and no root marker is found is refused with or without '--force'; '--here' applies anyway.
Usage, '--self'.

[[bundle-self-limits]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
'--self' carries text members in one part: beside '--binary' or '--max-bytes' it is refused.
Usage, '--self' ('Text members only; one part').

[[bundle-selection]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
The members are selected from 'git ls-files', by 'find', as the files changed since a reference ('--since') or from a list ('--from-list'), less what '-x', '-X', '--exclude' and '-S' leave out.
Usage, 'Selection' and 'Exclusion'.

[[bundle-dependencies]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh, adm/dependency.py
'--deps' adds every module the selection imports, transitively, and '--app <name>' bundles one face whole: its module, launchers, tests, GOOD files and dependencies.
Usage, '--deps', '--app'; the docstring of 'dependency.py'.

[[bundle-documents-by-default]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
The README, RATIONALE, DISCUSSIONS and INTERNALS of every directory the bundle touches are included unless '--no-doc' stands.
Usage, '--no-doc'.

[[bundle-chmod-script]]
documents: adm/bundle.sh
'--chmod-script' writes beside the bundle a script restoring the executable bit of every executable member.
Usage, 'Modes'.

[[graph-depth]]
documents: adm/import_graph.py, adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
'import_graph.py' reports who imports whom at a stated depth: 1 the outer directories, 2 the inner ones, 0 the modules themselves.
The tool's docstring, 'THE DEPTH IS THE ZOOM'; DEVELOPMENT, 'THE IMPORT GRAPH, AND THE LAYERING'.

[[graph-formats]]
documents: adm/import_graph.py, adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
The graph is written as 'tree', 'edges', 'dot', 'mermaid' or as an 'svg' drawn without any other tool, editable in Inkscape, a mutual pair shown with a head at both ends.
The tool's docstring, 'FORMATS'; DEVELOPMENT, 'CONTRACT MATERIAL'.

[[layering-direction]]
documents: adm/import_graph.py, adm/LAYERING.txt, adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
'--check' reads a layering declaration: an import no '<from> -> <to>' rule permits is a violation naming both ends, answered with status 1; an obedient tree answers 0.
The tool's docstring, '--check'; the head of LAYERING.txt.

[[layering-sealed-and-door]]
documents: adm/import_graph.py, adm/LAYERING.txt
'SEALED <glob>' declares a component that imports nothing of the tree and 'DOOR <module>' one that nothing outside may reach past; both are judged at module depth whatever '--depth' is asked.
The tool's docstring, 'THE DECLARATION HAS TWO LINE SHAPES'; LAYERING.txt.

[[shared-names]]
documents: adm/import_graph.py, adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
'--shared[=<n>]' lists every name that <n> or more components import, with where it lives.
The tool's docstring, '--shared'; DEVELOPMENT, 'CONTRACT MATERIAL'.

[[unreadable-reported]]
A module that cannot be parsed is reported, never silently left out of the graph.

[[graph-refusals]]
An unknown format, an unknown option and a declaration line of no known shape are refused by name.

[[graph-of-this-tree]]
documents: adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt, adm/LAYERING.txt
The tool reads the whole of this tree, draws its outer picture and judges it against 'LAYERING.txt' without a refusal or a crash.
DEVELOPMENT, 'THE IMPORT GRAPH, AND THE LAYERING', last paragraph; LAYERING.txt, 'Run:'.

[[unread-modules]]
documents: adm/unread_modules.py
'unread_modules.py' names every '.py' file that no entry point reaches, module by module.
The tool's docstring.

[[static-analysis-report]]
documents: adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt, adm/ruff.toml
The static analysis runs 'ruff' under 'adm/ruff.toml' and states what the tree tolerates -- the rules ignored by name and each kind of finding that stands -- while its 'MUST STAND AT ZERO' block (F, B011, B020, S110) is empty.
DEVELOPMENT, 'STATIC ANALYSIS'; the ruling of 2026-10-09 in the test's header stands over 'THE RATCHET'.

[[static-analysis-needs-ruff]]
documents: adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
Without 'ruff' the static analysis states a missing precondition as its result, never a silent pass.
DEVELOPMENT, 'NO RUFF, NO REPORT'.

[[requirement-check]]
documents: adm/requirement-check.sh, adm/requirement-list.txt, adm/DEVELOPMENT.txt
'requirement-check.sh' says for every entry of 'requirement-list.txt' 'ok', 'MISSING' or 'OLD' (older than its '>=<version>'), and exits 1 where a requirement of the census is missing or old, or one of the test writing support ('support:<language>,...'), naming the languages that go red.
The script's head; DEVELOPMENT, 'THE DEVELOPMENT CONTAINER'.

[[stain-conversion]]
documents: adm/book_stain.py
'book_stain.py <directory>' says which books carry 'stain_repeat_n' and 'stain_when' and, only with '--apply', rewrites them with the one 'stain' column, every other line kept and a book in the new form left alone.
The tool's docstring.

[[good-audit]]
documents: adm/good_audit.py
'good_audit.py' reports for every GOOD file with a candidate from the last run the shape of their difference.
The tool's docstring.

[[rescue-goods]]
documents: adm/rescue_goods.py
'rescue_goods.py' reads a run's REFUSED block and prints a 'git mv' for every missing nominal that stands in another 'GOOD/' of the tree.
The tool's docstring.

[[fail-book]]
documents: adm/fail_book.py
'fail_book.py' prints, for every case of a '--fail' run that passed, what its directory's book says of it now.
The tool's docstring.

[[support-layout-holds]]
documents: adm/support_layout.py, support/test_writing/LANGUAGES.conf
'support_layout.py' lists every port of 'support/test_writing' with its home from 'LANGUAGES.conf' and finds the layout holding: Python the one reference, at home in hwut.
The tool's docstring; services RATIONALE E-216.

[[support-layout-bites]]
documents: adm/support_layout.py
A directory not listed, an entry with no directory, a home other than 'hwut' or 'support', not exactly one reference or a reference away from hwut, a '.link' beyond its port and the reference, and a file of hwut naming a 'home = support' port are each a fault; records and the feature registry are not counted.
The tool's docstring; services RATIONALE E-216.

[[one-live-holder]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt
A free directory is taken by one process through 'mkdir' of its lock sub directory; the holder is recorded as (pid, started, acquired), '.taken' says so, and the release removes every trace.
README sections 1 and 2.

[[foreign-holder-busy]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt
A lock held by another live process stands: 'acquire()' answers False, 'with' raises 'DirectoryBusy', and the holder's lock survives.
README section 2.

[[non-recursive]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt, auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
The live holder acquiring its own directory again raises 'DirectoryDeadlock', a 'DirectoryBusy'.
README section 2; RATIONALE D-2.

[[dead-holder-broken]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt, auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
A lock whose recorded holder no longer exists is broken and re-taken; liveness is asked from the system, never inferred from elapsed time.
README section 1; RATIONALE D-3.

[[wait-policy]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt, auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
With 'wait_sec' above 0 a contender polls a foreign live holder until the deadline and then reports busy; with 0 it reports busy at once.
README section 2, POLICIES; RATIONALE D-3.

[[hold-allowance]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt, auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
With 'max_hold_sec' set a live holder whose acquisition lies further back is treated as gone and its lock broken; within the allowance, or under the default None, it stands.
README section 2, POLICIES; RATIONALE D-3.

[[holder-age]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt, auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
'holder_age_sec()' answers the seconds since the acquisition, and None for a holder record without 'acquired'.
README sections 1 and 2; RATIONALE D-3.

[[ground-left-as-found]]
documents: auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
The release removes the lock's parent directory where the take made it and it is empty; a parent that stood before, or that holds anything, stays.
RATIONALE D-4.

[[ground-race-retried]]
documents: auxiliary/RATIONALE-directory_mutex.txt
A contender whose 'mkdir' meets the parent directory just taken away by another holder's release tries again.
RATIONALE D-4.

[[no-liveness-no-lock]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt
Where the platform reports no process start times no lock is taken, and '.taken' says so.
README section 1, last paragraph.

[[release-idempotent]]
documents: auxiliary/README-directory_mutex.txt
'release()' removes this process's lock and may be called again without fault.
README section 2.

[[launcher-per-face]]
documents: bin/README.txt
Every 'hwut.<name>' launcher runs the service face of its name and answers with that face's own voice and exit status, adding nothing of its own.
README, 'hwut.<name>' and the last two paragraphs.

[[bare-hwut-is-run]]
documents: bin/README.txt
'hwut' alone is 'hwut.run'.
README, 'THE DEFAULT FACE'.

[[path-forms-agree]]
documents: bin/README.txt
A launcher resolves its own location, so it answers the same when called through PATH as when called by path.
README, first paragraph and last.

[[bin-pype-shebang]]
documents: bin/README.txt
A script whose first line is '#! /usr/bin/env hwut.pype' runs when 'bin/' is on PATH.
README, 'hwut.pype'.

[[dash-for-underscore]]
documents: bin/README.txt
A launcher's name carries '-' where its module's name carries '_'.
README, 'THE DEFAULT FACE', second paragraph.

