[[record-naming]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
A key of test, choice and subject names the file that carries a record: the nominal under 'GOOD/', the candidate under 'TMP/store/'.
README section 1a, 'Beside them, not in them'.

[[one-row-per-choice]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
The book holds one row per test and choice, and a later write replaces the row.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-7.

[[decision-only-book]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
A book row carries the verdict, the report and the stain and no configuration, instant, host or duration; what the machine saw goes to the local database.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-6, B-25, B-27, B-11.

[[empty-name-as-above]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
In 'book.csv' an empty test cell means the test of the row above.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-8.

[[stain-cell]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
A choice's stain is one cell of '|'-separated keywords; the repetition instability and each named keyword are set and cleared independently.
README section 1a, column 'stain'; RATIONALE B-18.

[[known-before-member]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
An id issued for a test that has no row makes a row with the verdict 'aspirant'.
RATIONALE B-14.

[[retired-book-name]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
A book under a retired name ('result_db.csv', 'result_db.json') is read, and the first write lays down 'book.csv' and removes the old file.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-10.

[[protected-base]]
The book is write-protected after every write; a damaged book reads as empty and a later write recovers it.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent.

[[divergence-on-call]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
Handed what is declared, the book answers what is recorded but declared no longer, and edits nothing.
README section 1a names 'divergence()' as a door and says no more.

[[healed-through-the-door]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
'rename_test()' and 'rename_choice()' re-key an entry in place, and 'remove_test()' gives an entry up and returns it.
README section 1a, 'Healed through the door'.

[[adopt-entry]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
'adopt()' takes a removed entry in under a name that does not stand; a name that stands is refused and nothing is merged.
README section 1a, 'Healed through the door'.

[[one-locked-act]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
The register is written in the book's own act, and every act runs under the directory's lock, which a holder above takes once for a session.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-9.

[[lock-live-holder]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
A directory lock held by another live process refuses and is never broken, however long it is held.
README section 1a, 'DirectoryBusy'.

[[lock-dead-holder]]
A directory lock whose holder is gone is broken and re-taken; no expiry is waited out.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent.

[[promotion]]
A nominal comes to exist only by acceptance promoting a candidate; before that, reading the nominal reports absence.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent.

[[nominal-kinds]]
The kinds of nominal answer one interface; a stream, having one pass, refuses a second open.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent.

[[id-allocation]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
An id scope counts from 0 and issues one above the highest id it ever issued; the last id issued can be given back, and the id at 2**32 is refused by name.
README section 4; RATIONALE B-2, B-31.

[[id-spellings]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
An id and a scope's mark are written in fixed spellings -- decimal, base 64, the mark line -- and read back to the number they were written from.
README section 1, 'id_scope.py'; RATIONALE B-31.

[[register-allocation]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
An application or a choice receives its id on demand, in a scope of its own; asking without allocating answers nothing.
README section 4; RATIONALE B-2.

[[id-survives-rename]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
A rename within the directory keeps the id; a rename onto a live name and a rename of what was never registered are refused by name.
README section 4.

[[id-retired]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
Removal deletes the name and retires the number, which is never issued again, across a reload too.
README section 4; RATIONALE B-2.

[[aborted-accept-gives-back]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
An aborted accept hands its id back: the mark steps back where that id was the last issued, and stands otherwise.
RATIONALE B-4.

[[register-in-the-book]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/22-moving-and-renaming.txt
The register round-trips byte for byte through its file and through the book's '#' block and two id columns; ids without a block to bound them are refused by name.
RATIONALE B-9, B-14 (which cites a B-13 the file does not hold); intend 22. README sections 1 and 1a still name 'GOOD/test_ids.dat'.

[[vanished-named]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt
A registered application whose file is absent is named by 'vanished()', and its entry stands.
README section 4, last paragraph.

[[register-shown]]
'hwut.config.show --show-ids' prints the register, choices beneath their application, and names what vanished.
Stated in the test and in the face's help only: the component's documents are silent.

[[run-set-group]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
A set of runs becomes one group number, issued once per distinct set; group 0 is the empty set.
README section 1, 'group_table.py'; RATIONALE B-3.

[[group-table-file]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
The group table lives in one write-protected file 'GOOD/group_ids.dat', round-trips byte-stably and allocates onward from the mark it read; a fault in it is refused by name.
README section 1; RATIONALE B-3, B-31.

[[traces-table]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
'TMP/hwut-traces.csv' holds what a run cost, one sorted row per system, test, choice and operation, replaced where the key stands; an empty 'system' or 'test' cell is the one above.
README section 1a, 'Under TMP/'; RATIONALE B-11, B-12.

[[traces-machine-class]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
The system key of the traces table names a machine class, not a host.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-11.

[[traces-under-tmp]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
Every trace stands under 'TMP/'; one still standing beside the tests is read and is gone at the next write.
README section 1a; RATIONALE B-28.

[[bookkeeper-unaccepted-region]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
Only a '##! unaccepted' line opens an unaccepted region; 'hwut.accept' books a GOOD carrying one as 'aspirant' and any other as 'true'.
RATIONALE B-26; test_run_info RATIONALE B-16.

[[member-states]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
A case stands in one of three states -- unknown, aspirant, member -- and every event moves it from each state to a defined one.
README 'The member state is the state'; the event table under 'Nothing changes by assignment'; RATIONALE B-15.

[[aspirant-playable-not-runnable]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt
An aspirant is playable and never runnable; only a member is runnable.
README 'The member state is the state'.

[[no-output-moves-nothing]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt
A run that produced no output, or none ending in '<hwut-end>', leaves the state where it was.
README, last paragraph of 'The member state is the state'.

[[state-sealed]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
The member state moves by its events only: assignment and deletion are refused.
README 'Nothing changes by assignment'; RATIONALE B-15.

[[run-judges-members-only]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
'on_run_verdict' raises where the case is not a member, and a member that is judged stays a member.
README 'Nothing changes by assignment' and 'The verdict and the state are factually separate'; RATIONALE B-15.

[[verdict-apart-from-state]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt
A test run info keeps the last verdict apart from the member state: None where no run has judged, and a failed member is still a member.
README 'The verdict and the state are factually separate'.

[[state-of-evidence]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
'of_evidence' builds the state from the files, never from the book: whether a nominal stands and whether it carries the mark.
README 'The two witnesses may disagree'; RATIONALE B-15.

[[book-against-files]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/README.txt, engine/bookkeeper/test_run_info/RATIONALE.txt
'consistency_fault_tuple' names every disagreement between what the book says of a case and what the files show, and mends none.
README 'The two witnesses may disagree'; RATIONALE B-15.

[[platform-cell]]
The book's last column says which platform measured a verdict, empty for 'local': written by a run on a platform, cleared by one here, left by an acceptance; an older book reads as measured here and its first write adds the header's word alone.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 6; intend/42 rulings 7, 21.

[[verdict-and-diff-agree]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The verdict is 'equivalent' exactly where the association for the diff holds only equivalent pairs.
MANUAL section 1; SEMANTICS section 1 (THE LAW), D-4.

[[line-by-line-in-order]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Outside regions the output is compared line by line, in order; a line missing, extra or different makes the two not equivalent.
MANUAL section 2; README 'Line Sequences vs. Potpourri'.

[[association-for-display]]
documents: engine/compare/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Where the two differ, subject and nominal are aligned chunk by chunk and line by line for a side-by-side display.
README MODALITIES 2; ARCHITECTURE 'THE TWO PIPELINES'.

[[early-abort]]
documents: engine/compare/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The equivalence check stops reading at the first mismatch, however far one stream runs ahead of the other.
README MODALITIES 1 (Fast-Fail); ARCHITECTURE 'ASYNC AND CONCURRENCY MODEL'.

[[streamed-association]]
documents: engine/compare/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Association chunks are delivered while the input still arrives, whatever the rates of the two streams.
README 'Async I/O'; ARCHITECTURE 'ASYNC AND CONCURRENCY MODEL'.

[[session-isolation]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Concurrent comparisons share no analogy registry: each call sees its own.
ARCHITECTURE 'Flyweight FrozenAnalogyDb', last paragraph.

[[cross-check-mode]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
With VUT_COMPARE_CROSS_CHECK=1 every verdict is derived a second time through the association and a divergence raises CrossCheckError.
SEMANTICS section 1, D-4.

[[comments-and-blanks-insignificant]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A line that starts or ends with '##' and a blank line take no part: they may be added, removed or moved on either side.
MANUAL section 3; SEMANTICS section 2.

[[comment-marker-configurable]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
The comment marker is configurable and comments can be switched off, after which a '##' line is content.
MANUAL section 3, last line; RATIONALE C-4.

[[whitespace-runs-equal]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A single space and a run of spaces or tabs compare equal; leading and trailing whitespace of a line is stripped.
MANUAL section 4 WHITESPACE; SEMANTICS section 3 SEPARATOR.

[[numbers-within-ratio]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Two numbers are equal where the subject lies within the configured relative ratio of the nominal.
MANUAL section 4 NUMBERS; SEMANTICS section 3 NUMERIC, D-27.

[[number-always-number]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
A number is compared by value also at ratio 0 ('1.0' is '1'), its sign belongs to it, and a number glued to a word is text.
RATIONALE C-11, C-12.

[[analogy-consistent]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A '((...))' placeholder accepts any value as long as the same value stands for the same counterpart, one-to-one, throughout the text.
MANUAL section 5; SEMANTICS section 5, 5.1.

[[analogy-region-private]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A region has its own analogy bindings: placeholders inside neither see nor affect those outside.
MANUAL sections 5, 7; SEMANTICS section 5.2, D-13.

[[visible-nothing]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A configured visible-nothing word counts as absent: a line made only of such words equals no line at all.
MANUAL section 6; SEMANTICS sections 4, 6.

[[potpourri-counts-visible-nothing]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Inside a potpourri region a line of visible-nothing words is counted like any other line.
MANUAL section 6; SEMANTICS section 4 POTPOURRI EXCEPTION.

[[region-framing-must-match]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A region on one side with no region or another handler opposite is a mismatch, even where the region is empty.
MANUAL section 7; SEMANTICS section 7.2, D-17.

[[region-faults-loud]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A broken region framing or region parameter is refused with the line number and never read as content; a run of five or more '#' is a comment.
MANUAL sections 7, 14; SEMANTICS section 7.1.

[[malformed-data-asymmetry]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A defect in a region's data is a specification error naming the line where it stands in the GOOD file, and a plain mismatch where it stands in the output.
MANUAL section 14; SEMANTICS section 7.5 (last paragraphs of TABLE and POINT-CLOUD).

[[region-parameters-nominal]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A region's parameters are taken from the GOOD file; the subject need only open the same handler.
MANUAL section 7; SEMANTICS section 7.3 NOMINAL IS AUTHORITATIVE, D-21.

[[region-parameter-precedence]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A region parameter is taken from the shebang, else from the handler's configuration section, else from the handler's default.
SEMANTICS section 7.3.

[[regions-switchable]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
Region framing has a switch; with it off '##! ...' and '####' are no regions and read as comments.
RATIONALE C-4.

[[framing-fault-caught]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
On the reading road a broken region framing is answered with False and a REFUSED line naming the line, not raised.
RATIONALE C-5.

[[potpourri-any-order]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, ../README.md
The lines of a 'potpourri' region match in any order: each line needs a distinct equivalent partner, and a leftover line on either side fails.
MANUAL section 8; SEMANTICS section 7.5 POTPOURRI; top README 'Order, tamed'.

[[potpourri-subset-duplicates]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A potpourri may let the subject be a subset of the GOOD file's lines ('subset') and may collapse literally repeated lines ('duplicates'); the flags combine.
MANUAL section 8 Flags; SEMANTICS section 7.5, D-25.

[[verbatim-region]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A 'verbatim' region is compared without any tolerance, in order and with equal counts; comment and blank lines stay insignificant.
MANUAL section 9; SEMANTICS section 7.5 VERBATIM.

[[ignore-region]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The contents of an 'ignore' region are exempt from comparison; its framing is still compared.
MANUAL section 10; SEMANTICS section 7.5 IGNORE.

[[point-cloud-coverage]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A 'point-cloud' region with 'limit' passes where every point of either side has a neighbour on the other side no farther than the limit.
MANUAL section 11 limit; SEMANTICS section 7.5 POINT-CLOUD, D-22, D-23.

[[point-cloud-pair]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
With 'pair' a point-cloud region requires a one-to-one pairing of the points within the limit, counts equal.
MANUAL section 11 pair; SEMANTICS section 7.5.

[[point-cloud-distance]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The distance of a point-cloud region is 'euclidean', 'L1', 'L2', 'Linf' or an expression over the two points.
MANUAL section 11 dist; SEMANTICS section 7.5.

[[point-cloud-constraint]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A point-cloud 'constraint' is a property every point must satisfy; used alone the GOOD file is a specification, not a recording.
MANUAL section 11 constraint; SEMANTICS section 7.5.

[[table-rows]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A 'table' region compares rows by position, as a bag ('unordered'), or by the value of a key column ('key').
MANUAL section 12 key, unordered; SEMANTICS section 7.5 TABLE, D-24.

[[table-columns]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A table region skips ignored columns, compares stated columns within a relative numeric tolerance, and splits cells on a stated separator.
MANUAL section 12 ignore, numeric, sep; SEMANTICS section 7.5 TABLE.

[[compare-unaccepted-region]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
An 'unaccepted' region fails every comparison while either side holds a line in it; empty on both sides it is equivalent.
RATIONALE C-9.

[[constraint-violation-region]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
A 'constraint-violation' region is compared exactly as 'unaccepted': it fails while it holds a line.
RATIONALE C-21.

[[unaccepted-zip]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
An 'unaccepted' nominal chunk pairs with whatever subject chunk stands opposite, and a trailing '<hwut-end>' is a chunk of its own that pairs with itself.
RATIONALE C-10.

[[old-delimiter-ordinary-content]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The retired '||||' delimiter has no meaning: it is ordinary content.
MANUAL section 15; SEMANTICS section 7.6, D-19.

[[constraint-binding]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Two '((name: value))' bindings are textually equal by name alone; the value is free and is judged by the constraints of that name the moment it enters.
MANUAL section 13; SEMANTICS sections 10, 10.2.

[[constraint-fires-when-complete]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
A constraint is checked once every variable it names is bound, whichever is bound last, and again whenever one is bound anew.
RATIONALE C-19; SEMANTICS section 10.1; MANUAL section 13.

[[constraint-evaluation-failure]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A constraint that is false or cannot be evaluated (division by zero, type clash) makes the pair a mismatch, never an exception.
MANUAL section 13 'Failure is the quiet kind'; SEMANTICS section 10.3.

[[constraint-findings]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
What the constraints find is told in one sentence naming the side, the rule and the variable; a variable a rule names and never bound makes the streams not equivalent.
RATIONALE C-20; MANUAL section 13; SEMANTICS section 10.3.

[[constraint-good-violation]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A GOOD file whose own values break its own rules is not equivalent, and the GOOD is named as the side at fault.
RATIONALE C-20; MANUAL section 13; SEMANTICS section 10.3.

[[constraint-order-free-refused]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A binding in the GOOD file's potpourri, unordered table or keyed table is refused loudly; in an ordered table, verbatim or ignore region it is plain text.
MANUAL section 13; SEMANTICS section 10.4.

[[constraint-expression-language]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A constraint may use arithmetic, comparisons, boolean logic, the listed maths and general functions, constants, and string methods on a bound value.
README-constraint_namespace section 2; MANUAL section 13; SEMANTICS section 10.6.

[[constraint-glob]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt
'glob(value, pattern)' matches the way a shell does, case sensitive, on the value made a string.
README-constraint_namespace section 2.

[[constraint-spec-refused]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
A constraint that does not parse or reaches outside the namespace is refused as a broken specification before any line is read; there is no circularity test.
README-constraint_namespace section 3; SEMANTICS section 10.7; RATIONALE C-19.

[[constraint-reserved-names]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt
A name of the constraint namespace is never taken for a variable.
README-constraint_namespace section 4.

[[constraint-relation]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt, engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
Each constraint expression is related to every variable it mentions, and each variable to every expression mentioning it.
README-constraint_namespace section 5; RATIONALE C-19.

[[constraint-space-per-run]]
documents: engine/compare/engine/README-constraint_namespace.txt
The bindings of one run never reach another: the fixed names are copied, the bound variables belong to the run.
README-constraint_namespace section 1.

[[configuration-declared]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
Every configuration default stands in the declaration and is read without constructing anything.
RATIONALE C-4 ('declared'), C-8.

[[misspelt-field-refused]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
Assigning to a misspelt configuration field is refused.
RATIONALE C-8.

[[anchored-alignment]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/19-matching-separated.txt
Within one chunk pair, lines equivalent and unique on both sides are paired first, in order, and only the sections between them are searched.
RATIONALE C-15; intend 19.

[[search-budget-counted]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/19-matching-separated.txt
The association search is bounded by a budget counted in expanded nodes, so its cost is the same on every machine.
RATIONALE C-16; intend 19.

[[cheapest-line-alignment]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Two lines are aligned by the cheapest edit sequence that the analogies it requires allow.
ARCHITECTURE 'KEY ALGORITHMS', edit-distance search.

[[chunk-pair-description]]
A chunk pair delivered through the main API describes itself as readable text.

[[string-edit-distance]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The edit distance of two strings is the minimum number of character operations that turn one into the other.
ARCHITECTURE 'KEY ALGORITHMS': 'string.py' Levenshtein.

[[line-edit-operations]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Two lines are related by the cheapest sequence of edit operations over their line elements, with its accumulated cost.
ARCHITECTURE 'KEY ALGORITHMS', edit-distance search.

[[required-analogies]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The edit operations of two lines come with the analogies required for their equivalences to hold.
ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': Line, ChunkPair; SEMANTICS section 5.

[[edit-classes]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Every edit carries a class, for elements of every tolerance class meeting every other or nothing; only GOOD, GOOD_TOLERATED, GOOD_INSERT and GOOD_DELETE preserve equivalence.
SEMANTICS sections 3, 6, D-3.

[[visible-nothing-good-skip]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A visible-nothing element opposite nothing is a GOOD_INSERT or GOOD_DELETE, not a difference.
SEMANTICS sections 4, 6.

[[no-transposition-in-line]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
The element search reorders nothing: swapped elements read as delete plus insert.
RATIONALE C-13.

[[line-sequence-alignment]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Two line sequences are aligned by substitute, insert and delete only; lines are never reordered.
RATIONALE C-15 ('it reorders nothing'); README 'Line Sequences vs. Potpourri'.

[[anchored-first]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/19-matching-separated.txt
Lines equivalent and unique on both sides anchor the alignment; a line with an analogy, a tolerated number, an equivalence pattern or no content never anchors.
RATIONALE C-15; intend 19.

[[missing-lines-linear-cost]]
documents: adm/WORK/intend/19-matching-separated.txt
A run of k missing lines costs k times the price of one.
intend 19, o-2.

[[line-verdict]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Two lines are judged equivalent or not under a given analogy database.
ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': Line.is_equivalent.

[[line-edit-info]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The edit operations that turn one line into the other are provided for display.
ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': Line.edit_operations.

[[line-sequence-verdict]]
documents: engine/compare/README.txt
Two line sequences are judged equivalent or not, the order of the lines being imperative.
README 'Line Sequences vs. Potpourri'.

[[line-sequence-association]]
documents: engine/compare/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The line associations of two line sequences are provided for display.
README MODALITIES 2; ARCHITECTURE 'THE TWO PIPELINES'.

[[potpourri-verdict]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Two potpourris are judged equivalent or not, regardless of line order.
SEMANTICS section 7.5 POTPOURRI; README 'Line Sequences vs. Potpourri'.

[[potpourri-association]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The line associations of two potpourris are provided for display.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': potpourri associate, best_match.

[[line-pair-verdict]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A line pair made purely of insignificant lines is equivalent; a one-sided pair of content is not.
SEMANTICS sections 2, 6, 8.

[[line-pair-element-lists]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A line pair delivers how the elements of its subject and its nominal line were aligned, cell by cell.
ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': LinePair.

[[analogy-db-consistency]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
An analogy database is one-to-one in both directions; merging two that contradict each other is refused.
SEMANTICS section 5; ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': AnalogyDb / FrozenAnalogyDb.

[[analogy-db-hybrid]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A frozen analogy database works by bit mask where the ids allow and by a tuple of ids beyond, with the same results.
ARCHITECTURE 'Flyweight FrozenAnalogyDb'.

[[analogy-db-flyweight]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Equal frozen analogy databases are one object.
ARCHITECTURE 'Flyweight FrozenAnalogyDb'; README 'Flyweight Pattern'.

[[analogy-db-immutable]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A frozen analogy database cannot be changed after construction.
ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': FrozenAnalogyDb is immutable.

[[html-report]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A comparison of subject and nominal is written as one side-by-side HTML page.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': feeder/html_feeder.py.

[[ui-element-status]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Every line element type is serialised as a display instruction whose status follows from its edit class: good, tolerated or differing.
SEMANTICS section 6, last paragraph; ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': feeder/ui.py.

[[ui-sequence-structure]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Inserted and deleted lines of a line sequence are serialised as structural mismatches.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': feeder/ui.py; README NOTES.

[[ui-potpourri-structure]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
Missing and extra lines of a potpourri are serialised as structural mismatches.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': feeder/ui.py; README NOTES.

[[closing-token-section]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
The display names the section of a trailing '<hwut-end>' 'CLOSING-TOKEN' and sets it apart at the foot.
RATIONALE C-10.

[[protocol-signature]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
The UI feeder declares the signature of its protocol, by which a consumer tells whether it is compatible.
ARCHITECTURE 'EXTENSION POINTS': New output feeder.

[[chunking]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Lines are grouped into line sequences and into regions, opened by '##! <handler>' and closed by exactly '####'.
SEMANTICS sections 2, 7; ARCHITECTURE 'DATA TYPES': InputChunk.

[[framing-refused]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A missing '####', a stray '####' and a region inside a region raise a region syntax error that names the line.
SEMANTICS section 7.1.

[[comment-lines]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Comment and blank lines are no content of a chunk.
SEMANTICS section 2.

[[tolerance-table]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The pattern table is set up from the configuration, each pattern with its tolerance class.
SEMANTICS section 3.

[[pattern-order]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A constraint binding is recognised before an analogy, and the author's patterns before numbers and blanks.
RATIONALE C-14; SEMANTICS section 10.7.

[[slash-is-pattern]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
There is no slash switch: '/' and the backslash are interchangeable where an equivalence pattern says so.
RATIONALE C-18 (MANUAL section 4 SLASHES still names a switch).

[[lexing]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
A line of text is split into line elements by tolerance class; literal text between patterns survives as strings.
SEMANTICS section 3; README DESCRIPTION.

[[overlapping-patterns]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A lexeme matched by several equivalence patterns carries all of them; two such elements are equal where their pattern sets are the same.
SEMANTICS section 3 EQUIVALENCE_PATTERN.

[[number-dot]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
A dot belongs to a number only where digits or an exponent follow.
RATIONALE C-17.

[[element-comparison]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Each line element class compares itself with another: a verdict, the analogy required, and a relative edit distance.
SEMANTICS sections 3, 8; ARCHITECTURE 'VERDICT MODEL'.

[[equivalence-words]]
documents: engine/compare/MANUAL.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Words of one configured equivalence set match each other.
MANUAL section 6; SEMANTICS section 3 EQUIVALENCE_PATTERN.

[[number-band]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The numeric band is centred on the nominal with radius 'abs(nominal) * ratio'; the price of a number substitution stays within 0 and 1.
SEMANTICS section 3 NUMERIC, D-27.

[[visible-nothing-line-element]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A line whose text is insignificant lexes into one visible-nothing element covering the whole line.
SEMANTICS section 3, last paragraph.

[[exact-pairing]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
Equivalent lines of subject and nominal are paired completely, whichever partner a tolerant line has to take and under one consistent set of analogies.
SEMANTICS sections 5.3, 7.5 POTPOURRI; MANUAL section 8.

[[fail-quickly]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
A pairing that cannot be complete is refused without a detailed similarity analysis.
ARCHITECTURE 'Bipartite matching' (structural upper bound); SEMANTICS section 7.5 (counts must agree).

[[least-committal-tie]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
Among equally valid complete pairings the least committal is chosen: a symbol stands for itself where it can, then the lowest nominal line number.
SEMANTICS section 5.3 TIE RULE, D-6.

[[similar-pairing]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt, engine/compare/README.txt
For the display, lines without an equivalent partner are paired by similarity in edit distance; surplus lines are paired with nothing.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': best_match.py; README MODALITIES 2.

[[comparison-budget]]
documents: engine/compare/DOC/SEMANTICS.txt
The number of similarity comparisons is capped by 'max_comparisons', 128 by default.
SEMANTICS section 7.5 POTPOURRI parameters.

[[match-db-pairing]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A database of possible matches is turned into distinct pairs of subject and nominal lines.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': matching.py, potential_pair_db.py.

[[forced-matches]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Matches without an alternative are extracted from the database as couples.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': matching.py (ultimates).

[[analogy-interference]]
documents: engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
Matches inconsistent with a given analogy database are removed.
ARCHITECTURE 'MODULE MAP': potential_pair_db.py (analogy constraints).

[[maximum-matching]]
documents: engine/compare/region/potpourri/solver/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A maximum matching of a bipartite graph is found by augmenting paths; fewer pairs than required means no complete pairing exists.
solver README file 1 and USAGE GUIDE 1; ARCHITECTURE 'Bipartite matching'.

[[long-augmenting-paths]]
An augmenting path of any length is followed without recursion.

[[consistent-pairing]]
documents: engine/compare/region/potpourri/solver/README.txt, engine/compare/ARCHITECTURE.txt
A complete pairing whose analogies are mutually consistent is found by backtracking, most constrained subject first, or its absence is reported.
solver README file 2; ARCHITECTURE 'CSP backtracking with MRV'.

[[thousand-lines-in-fractions-of-a-second]]
documents: engine/compare/region/potpourri/solver/README.txt
The two solvers together find a mapping in fractions of a second for sets of 1000 lines or more.
solver README 'IMPORTANT'.

[[alternative-solvers]]
documents: engine/compare/region/potpourri/solver/README.txt
A chronological backtracking solver and an arc-consistency ('lilly pad lane') solver stand ready as alternatives.
solver README files 3, 4, 5 and USAGE GUIDE 3, 4.

[[diff-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
A unified diff is read into changed lines: an added line is itself, a removed line stands at its new-side position, and a file removed wholesale contributes nothing.
README section 4c.

[[diff-strip]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
'-p N' strips N leading path components from the paths of the diff.
README section 4c, synopsis only.

[[affected-answer]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
'hwut.affected' answers a change with what was asked, what was indexed and the runs that executed it.
README section 4c, 'default'.

[[affected-rebase]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
A record's paths, relative to its test directory, are rebased to the root before they are held against the diff.
README section 4c.

[[affected-limit-stated]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
Every answer of 'hwut.affected' states its limit: a suggestion, never a clearance.
README sections 4b 'The limit' and 4c.

[[affected-bare]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
'--bare' prints the runs alone, one per line, for piping; the limit goes to stderr.
README section 4c.

[[affected-exit-status]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
'hwut.affected' exits 0 with a selection, 3 where nothing executed the change, and 2 where the command line is refused by name.
README section 4c, 'exit'.

[[affected-help]]
'hwut.affected --help' prints the face's documentation and exits 0.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent.

[[measures-carried]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A source's output file carries the universe of every measure once and, per test run or group, the items it took; each run's measures read back as reported.
README section 5; RATIONALE D-43.

[[source-rebased]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A source named relative to its test directory stands under the run's root once gathered, its measures with it.
RATIONALE D-42; README section 4c states the rebasing for the face only.

[[gather-refused]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/README.txt
Runs that disagree on the size of one point, and a point that counts its items without naming them, are refused by name at gathering.
RATIONALE D-43; README section 6c, last paragraph of 'Both merge'.

[[interval-algebra]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
Lines fold into sorted half-open ranges with union and subtraction; the uncovered set and the ratio are derived, and a file without an executable line has no ratio.
README section 4.

[[delta-coding]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
Ranges are delta coded as 'd', 'd+L' or 'd+L*C', the delta counted from the previous range's end; encoding and decoding are inverse.
README section 4; FORMAT section 4.

[[record-text-roundtrip]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
A record survives its text spelling and back, byte-stably: provenance, the counts flag and every source block.
README section 4; FORMAT sections 1 to 6.

[[record-names-runs]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A record's header names its runs as a set of run ids: none ('-') fresh from a reader, one once seated, every contributing run once merged.
README section 4; RATIONALE D-18.

[[absent-and-empty-distinct]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
A source file not measured has no block; one measured and found without an executable line keeps a block with an empty 'EX'.
README section 4, 'Absent and empty do not collapse'.

[[record-merge]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/DISCUSSIONS.txt
Records merge by union of ranges, associatively, commutatively and idempotently; provenance that differs is joined, and records carrying hit counts refuse to merge.
README sections 4 and 6; DISCUSSIONS disc-5.

[[record-faults-named]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
A record text that cannot be read whole is refused by name ('RecordFault'), never half-read.
README section 6; FORMAT section 7.

[[tool-election]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The language is stated or derived from the extension, and the first candidate tool this machine has is elected; no derivation, no configured tool and no available candidate are refused.
README sections 3 and 6; RATIONALE D-2, D-26.

[[binary-same-record]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
The binary spelling, the one form on disk, packs and unpacks to the same record as the text.
README section 5 'harvest'; RATIONALE D-20; FORMAT section 8.

[[binary-faults-named]]
documents: engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A malformed binary record is refused by name.
FORMAT section 8; RATIONALE D-20.

[[gathered-same-file]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
The gathered coverage of one source file (record version 3) is one content in two spellings: binary on disk, text as presentation.
README section 5 'The output directory'; RATIONALE D-43; FORMAT section 9.

[[gathered-text]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
The text of a gathered file shows 'EX' and the universe of every measure once, then per test run or group 'CV@<reference>:' its lines and '<TAG>@<reference>:' its items.
README section 5; FORMAT sections 9 and 10.

[[run-measures]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
A gathered file answers what one run took: the items under every reference the run is in, OR'd per point.
RATIONALE D-43; FORMAT section 10.

[[gathered-faults-named]]
documents: engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
A malformed gathered file, binary or text, is refused by name.
FORMAT section 9.

[[measure-registration]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A measure is registered with a name and a record tag of its own; a second measure of one name or one tag is refused.
README sections 6c and 6; RATIONALE D-11.

[[branch-arms]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Branch coverage is, per decision line, a mask of the arms taken over their total, delta coded; a second decision on one line is refused.
README section 6c; RATIONALE D-43.

[[mcdc-conditions]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
MC/DC records which conditions demonstrated independence, and admits several decisions on one line.
README section 6c.

[[named-points]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Toggle and cover points carry their name beside their line and merge by it; one name with two totals is refused.
README section 6c, last paragraph; RATIONALE D-12.

[[measure-own-line]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/database/FORMAT.txt
Each measure rides in its own tagged record line; a file without such a measurement writes no such line, and a tag no measure claims is refused.
README sections 6c and 6; FORMAT section 5.

[[measure-union]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Masks of two runs merge by OR; a measure that cannot say which items it counted is refused by name at merge.
README section 6c 'Both merge'; RATIONALE D-43.

[[points-of-items]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Every registered measure converts to points of items -- line, name, total, mask -- and back; a count that names no item is refused.
RATIONALE D-43.

[[measure-faults-named]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
An ill-formed point of a measure is refused by name ('MeasureFault').
README section 6.

[[run-keys]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
An index is keyed by the run id the register issued, or by that id paired with its found directory ('Gathered') where the fold spans directories.
README section 4b; RATIONALE D-14, D-16.

[[origin-segments]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The index cuts the line axis where the set of executing runs changes and fuses it where it does not; a stretch nobody executed is a segment naming nobody, and a segment carries one group number.
README section 4b; RATIONALE D-9, D-15.

[[who-executed]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
Asked for a line, for ranges or for a change, the index answers the runs that executed them, sorted.
README section 4b.

[[group-ids-kept]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
Handed a directory's persisted group table, 'index_of' keeps its group ids across builds.
README section 4b.

[[index-not-merge]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The index keeps which run reached which line, which a merge of records folds away.
README section 4b; RATIONALE D-8.

[[empty-is-no-clearance]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
The index answers over the runs it was built from only; an empty answer names nobody and is no clearance.
README section 4b, 'The limit'.

[[walk-order]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'record.walk' visits the header first, then the files in sorted order, the line axis before the measures in registered order; a measure without a point is not visited.
RATIONALE D-29.

[[presenter-accounts-measures]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A presenter walked over a record renders every registered measure.
RATIONALE D-29.

[[presenter-vocabulary]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A presenter defines no visit outside the vocabulary of the walk.
RATIONALE D-28, D-29.

[[two-roles]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Every registered tool is a framework, which is invoked and stamps its name, over a format, which reads the artefact.
README section 2 'reader.py'; RATIONALE D-23.

[[tool-aliases]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
One reader serves every tool that writes its format, and the record still names the tool that ran.
README section 6b, 'An alias claims the format' and 'A format, not a tool'.

[[framework-calls]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A framework names its command line, filled from its call scheme with a word of an empty field dropped whole, and its second call; it makes neither, and names none where the tool has none.
README section 6b; RATIONALE D-10, D-39.

[[gcov-late-second-call]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
gcov's second call is named late, over the '.gcda' the run wrote, and is none where there are none.
README section 6b.

[[absent-is-not-empty]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
Where no artefact of its format stands, harvest answers absence, not a measurement of nothing.
README sections 4 and 6.

[[harvest-globs]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The gather set is two glob lists: 'include' bounds what is harvested, 'omit' subtracts, and an empty set takes whatever the tool took.
RATIONALE D-3.

[[python-json-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
coverage.py's json is read: 'executed' as covered, 'executed + missing' as executable, 'excluded' as neither, and no hit counts.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'.

[[lcov-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
The lcov tracefile is read from its 'DA' lines: every one executable, every non-zero one covered; summaries are not consulted.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'.

[[gcov-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
gcov's annotated source is read: '-' not executable, '#####' never run, the path from 'Source:', and the 'branch' lines under a line as its arms.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'.

[[gcov-serves-vhdl]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt
VHDL compiled by GHDL is read through the gcov reader, with no code of its own.
README section 6b, 'What that reaches'; OVERVIEW.

[[cobertura-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Cobertura XML is read: every '<line>' executable, every non-zero 'hits' covered, no branch point from 'condition-coverage', and a foreign root left alone.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'; RATIONALE D-43.

[[go-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
The Go cover profile is read as ranges, overlapping blocks unioned, the module name of 'go.mod' stripped from the path and the path left as it stands where there is none.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'.

[[luacov-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
The luacov report is read: a blank count field as not executable, '*0' as never run, the field width derived per section.
README section 6b, 'What each format says'.

[[jacoco-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt
JaCoCo XML is read: every '<line>' executable, 'ci > 0' covered, the path built from package and source file, no branch point, and a line with neither 'ci' nor 'mi' refused.
README section 6b, 'What each format says', and section 6.

[[verilator-export-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt
Verilog coverage is read from Verilator's lcov export by the tracefile reader.
README section 6b, 'What that reaches'; OVERVIEW.

[[verilator-native-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt, engine/coverage/README.txt
Verilator's native '.dat' is read with lines, branch arms, toggle bits and cover points, each on its own measure.
OVERVIEW 'Formats read since'; README section 6c, last paragraph.

[[psl-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt
GHDL's PSL report is read as named cover points at their lines; asserts are not read.
OVERVIEW 'Formats read since'.

[[simplecov-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt
SimpleCov's resultset is read and agrees with the cobertura export of the same run.
OVERVIEW 'Formats read since'.

[[ucis-read]]
documents: engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt, engine/coverage/DISCUSSIONS.txt
UCIS XML is read for line, branch and toggle coverage.
OVERVIEW 'Formats read since'; DISCUSSIONS disc-9.

[[readers-agree]]
documents: engine/coverage/OVERVIEW-coverage-roads.txt
Two formats describing one run yield the same executable and covered lines.
Stated in the test only; OVERVIEW says it for simplecov alone.

[[arms-read-and-merged]]
documents: engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The arms taken are read from the tools that name them -- gcov's branch lines, coverage.py's arcs -- and merge by OR across runs.
README sections 6b and 6c; RATIONALE D-43.

[[real-artefact-per-format]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/README.txt, engine/coverage/HOW-TO-INTEGRATE-A-COVERAGE.txt
Every registered format has a real artefact filed under it, reads it, and every file there is an artefact or named provenance.
RATIONALE D-30; README section 7; HOW-TO-INTEGRATE.

[[flow-line]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt, engine/display/README.txt
Every event of a run becomes one flow line: a badge, the application, the choice in a column of its own, and dots to the verdict tag.
RATIONALE D-1, D-9, D-20; README 'The line format' (older form).

[[flow-elision]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A flow line elides what repeats: the badge of the line above, and ':' for the application where a choice identifies the line.
RATIONALE D-9, D-15.

[[two-modes]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt, engine/display/README.txt
A run reads as a 'START' and an 'END' line; under '--brief' as one 'DONE' line; a node that never ran reads 'SKIP'.
RATIONALE D-15; README 'The line format'.

[[parallel-badge]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Every flow line carries '||n', the count of parallel executions when the line is printed, a closing line not counting itself.
RATIONALE D-14, D-18.

[[reason-word]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A failure that is not about equivalence says one short word of its reason before '[FAIL]'; a plain difference says none.
RATIONALE D-31.

[[no-good-file-fails]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A case with no GOOD file reads 'no GOOD file [FAIL]' and fails.
RATIONALE D-32.

[[setup-lines-silent]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Session and build lines are silent unless '--show-details', the VERBOSE tier or a failure asks for them, and '--show-timing' restores the seconds column.
RATIONALE D-9.

[[directories-roll-call]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
After the flow the DIRECTORIES block calls every directory with its verdict and its ok count, drawn as a tree where directories nest.
README 'The closing blocks'; RATIONALE D-11.

[[hints-by-category]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt, engine/display/README.txt
The HINTS block groups the failures by category, each directory once and each application once, with the full phrase and the cause, and points to 'hwut.help'.
RATIONALE D-35, D-34; README 'The closing blocks' (as FAILURES).

[[here-name]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A run asked inside a TEST directory names that directory from the root conf where it would say '.'.
RATIONALE D-47.

[[tiers]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
VERBOSE shows every event, PLAIN the flow and the closing blocks, QUIET the closing blocks without the flow, SILENT nothing on stdout and the faults on stderr.
README 'The tiers'; RATIONALE D-4, D-5.

[[faults-shown]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt
A fault at the tree or in a directory stands in the flow as an 'ERROR' line and is counted.
README 'The tiers', 'The closing blocks'.

[[empty-selection-noted]]
A wish that selects nothing is reported with a note and a roll-call of zeros.
Stated in the test only: the documents are silent (README names the NOTE line and no more).

[[stream-stability]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A token the phrase table does not carry prints with its hyphens opened, an unknown event kind is ignored, and a misfit event becomes a fault line while the stream walks on.
README 'The one English door', the stability promise; RATIONALE D-6.

[[phrase-table]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Every wire token becomes its phrase through one table.
README 'The one English door'; RATIONALE D-6.

[[failure-table]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Every reason a case fails has one entry: its category, the word before '[FAIL]', its phrase, and the explanation 'hwut.help' prints.
README 'failure.py'; RATIONALE D-34, D-36.

[[display-report-width]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A piped report is 78 columns wide; a terminal is asked for its width, 'COLUMNS' first, and the line is held between 40 and 120.
RATIONALE D-9, D-19, D-20.

[[colour-decision]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Colour is decided once: '--colour' enforces it whatever 'NO_COLOR' says, '--no-colour' refuses it, and otherwise piped output is plain.
README 'Colour gates'; RATIONALE D-2.

[[tree-ink]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
The DIRECTORIES tree and the verdict tags are inked in fixed colours, the tag on a ground of its own.
RATIONALE D-12, D-9.

[[display-exit-status]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.run' exits 0 where all is green, 1 where a test failed or a fault was met, 2 where it refuses, 3 where the wish selects nothing.
RATIONALE D-3 / E-1.

[[rendering-refused]]
documents: engine/display/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
Rendering words that cannot want anything are refused by name before anything runs, the usage block printed once and only where it is the subject.
README 'Three kinds of word'; RATIONALE D-17.

[[strategy-homogeneity]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A tree of directories yields the same digest of runs, verdicts and roll-call under every strategy and job count.
RATIONALE D-10, D-13.

[[deterministic-flow]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
'--deterministic' fixes the order of the flow and '--jobs=1' its shape, so a raw flow of several runs compares byte for byte.
RATIONALE D-16.

[[strategy-refused]]
An unknown '--strategy' is refused, the known ones named.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent; an execution word of the face.

[[jobs-budget]]
'--jobs' is host-global: never more pieces of work stand at once than the budget, across all directories.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent; an execution word of the face.

[[busy-directory-fault]]
A directory held by a live process is reported as a fault while its siblings run to their end.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent.

[[store-and-timing-knobs]]
'--no-store' leaves no candidate behind and '--timing' leaves a cadence sidecar beside the candidate.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent; execution words of the face.

[[short-form]]
Bare words are targets: the first names files, each further one a choice, globbing allowed in both.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent; a selection word of the face.

[[label-silence]]
A bare run silences what the standard label marks; naming a label or a literal target lifts the silence, and an unknown label is refused by name.
Stated in the test only: display's documents are silent; a selection word of the face.

[[goal-selects-operation]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A caller hands 'run_test' a configuration and a request, and the request's goal selects the operation that runs.
README 2.8.

[[unservable-configuration-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A configuration that can serve no goal is refused where it is handed in, before anything runs, naming the first fault.
README 2.6, 2.8 (verify).

[[configuration-frozen]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A configuration is read-only once handed in: no run can change the design it runs under.
README 2.7, ONE WRITER, MANY READERS.

[[configuration-aggregates-verbatim]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The configuration holds the structs of procsitter, build, store and compare verbatim, as members.
README 2.7, AGGREGATION, NOT RESTATEMENT.

[[none-choice]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A test without choices has the single choice key None and is called once without a choice argument; named choices and None never mix.
README 3, THE 'None' CHOICE.

[[three-places-derived]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The run directory, the build directory 'BUILD/<source stem>' and the output directory 'OUT/' follow from the source file and the test directory.
README 3, THE PLACE.

[[store-place-said-once]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Artifacts are written where the configuration's store names a directory, in the test's own directory otherwise, and nowhere without a store.
README 2.8; 6, StoreConfig.

[[display-target-selects-driver]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A display target named in the request is turned into its driver in one place; a target without a driver is refused.
README 2.6, 2.8, 11.4.

[[busy-directory-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A run of a test whose directory a live process holds is refused as busy -- not queued, not forced -- and books nothing.
README 2.8 (lock); 6, THE DIRECTORY IS THE LOCK.

[[lock-released-however-ended]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The directory lock is released however the run ended, a fault included.
README 2.8 (unlock); 6.

[[judged-execution-recorded]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The execution that was judged is stored as the candidate a later loaded read reads; a replay records nothing.
README 2.8 (record); 4.

[[raw-and-cadence-sidecars]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Where asked, the raw stream and the per-line cadence are kept beside the canonicalised record, which stays byte-exact.
README 4, RAW, TIMING.

[[outcome-booked-overwriting]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
What happened is entered in the book as one entry per choice, overwritten and never appended.
README 2.8 (book); 6, THE ENTRY IS OVERWRITTEN.

[[book-entry-holds-no-configuration]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/README.txt
The book entry holds the verdict and the report and neither the canonicaliser nor the compare setup the run was judged under.
bookkeeper RATIONALE B-6, B-25; README 6 still says both are recorded.

[[request-stop-event]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A caller ends a running test through the stop event of its request.
README 2.8, Request.stop_event.

[[subject-channel-decides]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Subject provision executes where no current recording stands and loads one that does; a caller that only reads, or holds no configuration, is never made to execute, and only an execution is recorded.
README PART II, THE ONE CHANNEL.

[[build-without-tool-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The build command line follows from the declared build system; a build system that names no tool is refused, not guessed.
README 2.6.

[[clean-exit-not-a-built-target]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A build runs in its own directory and accounts its declared targets after exit: a tool that exits clean and built nothing has failed.
README 2.6; 3, THE PLACE; 13 (TARGET_NOT_BUILT).

[[build-report-names-reason]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A build report names what went wrong, a tool reason before a target reason, and tells a build ended by a cap from one that failed.
README 13 gives the families only; 4 (the silence cap protects builds).

[[failed-build-is-failed-test]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A failed build is a failed test: the verdict is false, the report is the build's, and no comparison exists.
README 8.

[[empty-comparison-not-a-pass]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A comparison that judged nothing is not a pass.
README 2.6, ABSENT AND EMPTY NEVER COLLAPSE.

[[first-reason-by-precedence]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Where several reasons could speak the report is the first by precedence -- source and build before run before subject and nominal -- and every failure token has a rank.
README 8, 13.

[[products-kept-apart]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A result carries what was provided and, only where a comparison happened, what was compared; no comparison is an absence, not an empty field.
README 8.

[[missing-source-and-display-named]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A source file that is not there and a display target that cannot be reached are reported by their own tokens.
README 13 (SOURCE_NOT_FOUND, DISPLAY_TARGET_UNREACHABLE).

[[observer-never-reaches-verdict]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An observer is notified of every step, a missing method is no fault, and one that raises changes no verdict.
README 12.

[[console-observer]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The console observer writes one line per notification, a build's outcome included.
README 12, Shipped.

[[empty-answer-explained]]
documents: engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
A run that answered nothing is explained from facts already held -- its argv, containment, exit, duration and stderr tail; where nothing ran nothing is said.
RATIONALE O-35; DISCUSSIONS todo-21.

[[resolution-chain-shown]]
documents: engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
The diagnosis shows how the called program resolves on PATH, symbolic links followed, and says where it does not resolve.
RATIONALE O-35 (1); DISCUSSIONS todo-21.

[[wrong-channel-hint]]
documents: engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
An empty stdout beside a speaking stderr draws a hint that the output is on the wrong channel -- a hint, never a verdict.
DISCUSSIONS todo-21.

[[coverage-run-harvested]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A coverage run executes the application under the elected tool's command line, harvests the record and stores it with the run id; nothing is compared and nothing is booked.
coverage RATIONALE D-38, D-39.

[[coverage-absence-answered]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt
A coverage run that left nothing, did not testify, or has no coverage target is answered with its cause and stores no record.
coverage RATIONALE D-38; operations RATIONALE R-70, CITED BY.

[[verdict-from-comparison]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Each subject is held against its nominal: a match passes, a difference fails and the report names the mismatch.
README 8.

[[unreadable-nominal-is-fault]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A nominal that cannot be read is a fault of its own name, never a pass.
README 2.6; 13 (NOMINAL_FILE_NOT_FOUND).

[[named-subject-absent-fails]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A subject that is named and was not produced fails by the name of the missing output, while the subjects that were produced are judged.
README 13 (OUTPUT_FILE_NOT_FOUND).

[[unnamed-subject-unjudged]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A subject absent from the subjects-to-nominals map is not judged.
README 7.

[[deterministic-fast-fail]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Subjects are compared in name order, so a fast-fail reports the same first difference on every run.
README 8, fast_fail.

[[failed-provision-no-comparison]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A failed provision is a failed test with no comparison, and a difference display shows nothing for it.
README 8; PART II (provision failure is test failure).

[[provenance-blind]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A check gives the same verdict whether its subjects were executed or read back, and does not learn which.
README 2.4, 2.5.

[[watching-changes-nothing]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Observing a check or displaying a difference changes no verdict.
README 12; 9.

[[nominal-without-token-fails]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt
A stdout nominal that does not end in '<hwut-end>' fails the case as 'nominal-without-hwut-end', whatever the subject says.
README THE TERMINAL TOKEN; RATIONALE R-70.

[[cut-short-subject-named]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt
A stdout subject without the token against a nominal that carries it draws 'terminated-without-hwut-end', never a line difference.
README THE TERMINAL TOKEN; RATIONALE R-70.

[[file-subjects-exempt-from-token]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt
A file subject needs no terminal token.
README THE TERMINAL TOKEN; RATIONALE R-70.

[[undeclared-stderr-fails]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A word on stderr of a test that does not declare it ignored fails as 'unexpected-stderr'; whitespace is silence, and declared stderr is never read.
README STDERR (S-1).

[[accept-refuses-undeclared-stderr]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Where a run wrote on undeclared stderr the acceptance refuses and writes nothing at all; stderr is never stored as a nominal.
README STDERR (S-1), THE SECOND QUESTION.

[[stderr-stain-cleared]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An acceptance that stands takes the 'stderr-untolerated' stain away; a refused one neither sets nor clears it.
README STDERR (S-1), THE STAIN.

[[down-is-compares-stream]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The difference display carries compare's own item stream to the target and interprets none of it.
README 9; 11.2.

[[only-differing-carried]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A matching subject is not carried out at all unless asked otherwise.
README 9, only_differing.

[[every-named-subject-shown]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
Every named subject that differs is shown, never only the first.
Stated in no document; README 9 is silent.

[[feed-session-order]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A display driver sees open, each item, close, in that order, and close runs however the session ended.
README 11.4.

[[foreign-signature-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A stream whose protocol signature the hub cannot parse is refused before anything is parsed.
README 11.2, THE SIGNATURE; 2.6.

[[dump-replaces-nominal]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A handed dump replaces the nominal wholesale, and every later comparison reads it.
README 10, TAKE_DUMP; 6.

[[initiate-stores-session-stream]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An INITIATE acceptance stores the final plain stream of its merge session, and without a session stores nothing -- never the current output.
README 10, INITIATE.

[[accept-all-or-nothing]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An acceptance that cannot read one of its dumps writes nothing, not even the subjects whose dumps were readable.
Stated in no document for dumps; README STDERR says it of stderr only.

[[accept-pulls-provision]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An acceptance whose step names no dump pulls provision, once for all its subjects.
README 2.5; 10, groundwork.

[[acceptance-booked-undated]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/README.txt
An acceptance writes the verdict and the report into the book and no instant of acceptance.
bookkeeper RATIONALE B-25; README 11.5 (the nominal, the register, the book).

[[session-sequence]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A merge session runs open, present, resolve, close in that order: open and close once, resolve inside the open session.
README 11.4, 11.6a.

[[target-builds-driver]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A caller names a display target and the matching driver is built for it; a target with no driver is refused.
README 11.4; 2.6.

[[basic-mergetool-tier]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
Any editor launched on files serves as merge tool: the bytes it leaves become the nominal, and a tool that fails or writes nothing leaves the nominal untouched.
README 11.5, BASIC tier; DISCUSSIONS todo-17, THE CHEAP PATH.

[[commit-becomes-nominal]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The merge tool is handed the plain subject and the plain nominal, and the plain stream it commits becomes the nominal.
README 11.3, 11.6.

[[cancel-leaves-nominal]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A cancelled merge stores nothing; the nominal is unchanged.
README 11.6.

[[empty-commit-is-cancel]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A commit that carries no artifact is downgraded to a cancel and stores nothing.
README 11.3, 11.6a; 2.6.

[[display-only-driver]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A driver without 'resolve' renders once and cancels; half-duplex is no fault.
README 11.3.

[[edit-is-realigned]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
An edit of the working nominal is answered with a fresh alignment from compare, round after round, until a commit or a cancel.
README 11.6; DISCUSSIONS todo-16.

[[no-progress-guard]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A realign that carries no artifact or one identical to the round before ends the session as a cancel at once.
README 11.6a.

[[round-cap]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A session that neither commits nor cancels is ended as a cancel at 'max_round_n' rounds.
README 11.6a.

[[envelope-signed]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The answer envelope is signed and carries the plain artifact; a foreign signature is refused before anything is read.
README 11.3.

[[unknown-intent-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An intent the hub does not know is refused, never guessed.
README 2.6.

[[rich-client-over-pipe]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
A client in another process and any language drives display and merge over a pipe: items down as JSON lines, one envelope up.
README 11.5, RICH tier; DISCUSSIONS todo-16, todo-17.

[[down-carried-uninterpreted]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The hub carries whatever compare emits downwards -- a new kind, new fields -- without an edit and without interpreting it.
README 11.2.

[[tui-renders-down]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
The terminal tier renders each generation as text -- setup banner, section boundaries, verdict-marked spans, every region kind -- and shows only meta the stream carries.
README 11.5, TUI; DISCUSSIONS todo-20.

[[tui-reading-view]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The reading view feeds one stream against itself and marks what can vary, by tolerance kind, with a legend line.
README 11.5, TWO MARKING VIEWS.

[[tui-edit-through-editor]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
On [e]dit the terminal tier hands the nominal to '$EDITOR' and shows the fresh alignment; [c]ommit stores, [q]uit cancels.
README 11.5, TUI.

[[tui-unchanged-edit-reprompted]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An edit that changed nothing is asked again locally; only decisions leave the terminal tier.
README 11.5, TUI.

[[argv-from-source-kind]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The source kind fixes the command line -- the source itself, the interpreter before it, or the built application -- and the choice name is the argument that selects the scenario.
README 3.

[[channels-and-declared-files-are-subjects]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
Stdout, stderr and every declared output file are subjects; a declared file is read and removed.
README 1, 7; DISCUSSIONS todo-23.

[[canonicalised-before-compare]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A declared canonicaliser rewrites its subject before anyone compares it; a subject without one is compared raw.
README 3, CANONICALISATION.

[[failing-canonicaliser-ends-provision]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt, engine/operations/README.txt
A failing canonicaliser ends provision: the report names the canonicaliser, and no stream is handed over.
orchestrator/run RATIONALE O-18; README PART II.

[[absent-application-is-launch-failure]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An application that is not there is reported as the launch's failure, and no subject is provided.
README PART II (no stage raises); 13 (TEST_APP_*).

[[cap-named-in-report]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A cap that ended the run is named in the provision report by its own token; silence reads 'test-app-stalled'.
README 4, THE SILENCE CAP; 13; orchestrator/run RATIONALE O-19.

[[partial-streams-delivered]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A run ended by a cap still delivers what it produced, with the failure token beside it.
README PART II, EVERY STAGE IS A SUPPLIER.

[[loaded-same-shape]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
A stored run is read back in the same shape as an executed one, without execution and without attribution records.
README 2.5, 5.

[[absent-recording-reported]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
An absent recording is reported as such, never provided as an empty subject.
README 2.6; 13 (RECORDING_MISSING).

[[no-terminal-word-set]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt, engine/operations/RATIONALE.txt
Where a stdout canonicaliser stands the application is launched with 'HWUT_NO_TERMINAL' in its environment.
README THE TERMINAL TOKEN; RATIONALE R-70.

[[wrong-slot-refused]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
A provider in the wrong slot of a provision is refused at construction, by name.
README-provider 2.

[[local-and-proxy-alike]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
A provider that is no local stage fills a slot through the interface alone, and a queue consumes plain providers and a multi's proxies alike.
README-provider 2, 5.

[[cadence-in-delivery]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
The cadence rides in the execute delivery: a dict where the provider measures, None where it cannot.
README-provider 1.

[[raw-stream-live]]
documents: engine/operations/README.txt
The raw stream is handed on as it is produced.
Stated in no document; README PART II names only the fan-out.

[[one-call-many-choices]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
One supervised '--interactive' call serves many choices, each with its own token-terminated delivery, behind the ordinary execute slot.
README-provider 3; DISCUSSIONS todo-23.

[[session-record-on-every-choice]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
Every choice of a session carries the one attribution record of the process that produced it, a refused choice included.
README-provider 3; DISCUSSIONS todo-23.

[[non-zero-status-delivered]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
In a session a zero or positive status delivers the choice's streams with report OK.
README-provider 3, Status.

[[negative-status-contained]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
In a session a negative status delivers with 'test-app-contained'.
README-provider 3, Status.

[[unknown-choice-survived]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
A choice the application answers with 'fail' delivers nothing and is named, and the session serves the next choice.
README-provider 3, Status; DISCUSSIONS todo-23.

[[session-needs-registered-capability]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt, engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt
A session is refused for a configuration that does not register 'interactive', and for a choice name the blank-separated wire cannot carry.
README-provider 3, Capability; DISCUSSIONS todo-23 (the blank is stated nowhere).

[[session-file-subjects]]
documents: engine/operations/DISCUSSIONS.txt, engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
In a session a choice's declared files are read after its 'done' and removed; a declared file that is missing fails that choice.
DISCUSSIONS todo-23, BUILT; README-provider 3 still says channel subjects only.

[[wave-build]]
documents: engine/operations/run/README-provider.txt
A multi build invokes the tool once per wave, parallel within a wave and ordered between; a failed wave fails its targets and every later wave answers 'target-not-built' without an invocation.
README-provider 4.

[[session-clock-at-first-choice]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A session an orchestrator holds is launched at its first choice: its wall clock starts with the test run, never while its choices wait; a choice a cap left unserved names the cap.
services RATIONALE E-196.

[[candidate-files]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The candidates of a test directory are its regular files, one directory deep, less the default ignore globs, the directory's own 'ignore' globs and the framework's own files.
README section 0 gate 1, section 10; RATIONALE X-CONSIDERED.

[[one-carrier]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A specification speaks for a file on exactly one carrier, its header or an 'apps' entry of 'hwut.conf'; a file with both, or an 'apps' entry naming no file, is a test directory error and exploration completes.
README sections 0 gate 2, 3; RATIONALE R-7.

[[untriggered-file-silent]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A file neither carrier speaks for is no test application: no fault, and nothing is asked of it.
README section 0 gate 3; RATIONALE X-SILENT (supersedes R-44).

[[title-stands-in]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A specification lacking 'title' is not refused: the title stands in as '<source file> <title unspecified>'.
README section 0 gate 4; RATIONALE X-TITLE.

[[marker-in-head]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The marker '@hwut {' makes a file a test application only within its first eight lines; the word alone, a marker inside prose or a string, or one past the head is none.
README section 4; RATIONALE X-HEAD, R-72.

[[decoration-any-language]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
Whatever precedes the marker on its line, up to two tokens of at most eight characters without a digit, is decoration and is stripped from every header line, so a header reads in any language's comment.
README section 4; RATIONALE X-DECORATION, R-9.

[[region-unwrapped]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The region ends at the matching brace, a brace inside a string not counting; the common leader of its lines is stripped and a trailing comment closer tolerated, with no table of comment syntaxes.
README section 8; RATIONALE R-9.

[[positions-file-relative]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
Every unwrapped line carries its file line and its stripped column, so every position reported is relative to the file and never to the region.
README section 8; RATIONALE R-13.

[[specification-shape]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A specification states general parameters at the root and specific ones per choice; 'choices' as a list means the map with empty bodies, and no 'choices' means the single choice-less call.
README sections 1, 10; RATIONALE R-5, R-6.

[[unknown-key-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A key outside the vocabulary is refused by name at its position, and validation completes.
README section 8; RATIONALE R-4.

[[value-shape-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A value of the wrong type or range is refused by name at its position: a ratio beyond [0..1], a cap that is no positive number, an unknown cap, a marker pair of three.
README section 2 (caps: fixed vocabulary); RATIONALE R-47, R-24.

[[absence-is-not-off]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
An unstated key takes its owner's default; 'analogy', 'comment' and 'constraints' are switched off by '[]', 'false', 'no', 'null' or an empty value, a marker pair set to 'true' is refused, and a key bound to nothing where a value is required is refused.
README section 2.1; RATIONALE R-24, R-64.

[[root-only-parameters]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'same' and 'interactive' stand at the root and reach every choice; inside a choice each is refused by name.
README section 2; RATIONALE R-45, R-46.

[[empty-choices-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A header stating 'choices = []' is refused by name at its position.
RATIONALE X-EMPTY-CHOICES.

[[execute-stated]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'execute' states the call verbatim at the root or in a choice with '$file', '$filestem' and '$choice'; an unknown '$word' is refused at validation and 'execute' beside 'interactive' at resolution.
RATIONALE R-68, R-82.

[[output-subjects]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'output' names the subjects in order; a declared file is read after the run has terminated, recorded beside stdout and removed.
README section 2 'output', laws (2); RATIONALE R-71.

[[output-file-missing]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A declared output file the run did not leave is the verdict 'output-file-not-found'.
README section 2 'output', law (3); RATIONALE R-71.

[[stderr-never-subject]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'<stderr>' in 'output' is refused by name at validation.
README section 2 'output', law (1); RATIONALE R-71.

[[choice-overwrites-root]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The root is the default and a choice's own value overwrites it; a scope merges field by field at every depth, and resolution happens once, at exploration.
README sections 2.1, 10; RATIONALE R-5, R-26.

[[faults-collected]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A file whose specification carries a fault yields no application; every fault of the directory is reported at once and the healthy files still explore.
README sections 7.1, 8; RATIONALE R-30.

[[directory-keys]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.conf' carries the directory's keys -- 'on_entry', 'on_exit', 'ignore', 'collision' and 'dependency' written in targets -- and 'apps' entries that use the header vocabulary unchanged.
README sections 5, 5.2; RATIONALE R-28, R-31..R-34.

[[conf-root-no-test-parameter]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A test parameter at the root of 'hwut.conf' is refused with the sentence that says where it belongs.
README section 6.

[[key-places-enforced]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
Every key the framework knows is read or refused by name in each place it can stand -- root conf, directory conf, 'app_defaults', 'apps' entry, header, choice; none is silently dropped.
README section 6 (exclusivity table); RATIONALE R-73, R-83.

[[dependency-graph]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A 'collision' or 'dependency' target the directory does not offer and a dependency cycle are directory failures, and every case whose dependencies cannot be met carries '[MISDEP]'.
README section 5; RATIONALE R-32..R-36.

[[app-defaults-yield]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'app_defaults' gives every application of the directory its parameters without overwriting what the application or the choice states.
README section 5.1; RATIONALE R-52.

[[tree-walked]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'explore_tree' finds every test directory below a root in a fixed order, the root itself included, does not descend into one, and refuses a start with no root conf above; stream and snapshot walk alike.
RATIONALE R-69, R-70.

[[marker-name-inherited]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'test_directory' renames the test directory marker for everything below the level stating it; 'TEST' holds elsewhere.
RATIONALE R-69.

[[configuration-inherited]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/00-configuration.txt
'app_defaults' flows down the tree and merges parameter by parameter: a nearer level's word wins, the test directory's own 'hwut.conf' last.
RATIONALE R-69; intend/00-configuration.

[[local-keys-do-not-flow]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'on_entry', 'on_exit', 'collision' and 'dependency' stated at a tree level draw a fault and flow nowhere.
RATIONALE R-69.

[[climb-ends-at-tmp]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The climb for the root conf ends at a 'TMP' standing beside a 'GOOD/': a fixture built there does not read the enclosing project's 'hwut-root.conf'.
README section 6; RATIONALE R-76.

[[language-found]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A file's language is its 'language' word, else the 'language-setup' entry whose 'extensions' claims its extension, else none; an extension two entries claim is refused at the root.
README section 7; RATIONALE R-73, R-10.

[[variant-groups-declared]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'variant_group' declares groups of alternatives in one namespace; a group or an alternative that is no scope, an unknown key inside one and one name in two groups are refused at the declaration.
README section 5.1a; RATIONALE E-9.

[[variant-selected]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--variant' merges one alternative per group over the base, stating differences only and in any order; an unknown name and two alternatives of one group are refused before anything merges.
README section 5.1a; RATIONALE E-9.

[[defaults-related-to-owner]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/00-configuration.txt
One relation table covers the parameter vocabulary exactly and names, per parameter, the member of the owning component that declares its default; a renamed member or a forgotten default fails at import, naming parameter, class and member.
README section 9; RATIONALE R-23, R-38..R-42, R-57.

[[unrelated-parameter-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A parameter name the relation table does not carry is refused, not defaulted.
README section 9.

[[complete-configuration]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/00-configuration.txt
Instantiation yields one complete configuration per owning class: stated values written, everything else at the declared default, 'None' only where the owner declared 'None'.
README sections 9, 10; RATIONALE R-37, R-43.

[[record-keeps-chosen]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/00-configuration.txt
The record keeps 'None' for what the author did not state, and a value stated off is a statement no default replaces.
README sections 8, 9, 10; RATIONALE R-17.

[[cases-selected]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'get_test_cases' turns the set into a flat ordered sequence of (file, choice) cases with their records -- every choice of every application, or the named files and choices; the choice-less application yields its 'None' case.
README sections 11, 12; RATIONALE R-19, R-20.

[[selection-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A wish naming an application or a choice the set does not carry is refused at the door, by name.
README section 11.

[[configuration-printed]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The configuration as read is printed in the specification language, every parameter included, for a file or a whole directory, and what is printed reads back as written.
README section 7.1; RATIONALE R-29, R-50, X-PRINTED, R-89.

[[provenance-in-comment]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A printed value not this choice's own word carries its provenance in a comment: 'app', 'hwut.conf:<line>', '--hwut-info' or 'default'.
README section 7.1; RATIONALE R-51, R-53.

[[no-default-drops-unstated]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--no-default' drops every value nobody stated, a scope with no stated leaf whole.
README section 7.1; RATIONALE R-51.

[[places-named]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--provenance' names file and line of every stated value; '--gnu' puts 'file:line:column:' at the beginning of every line, in a padded column, a line without a place of its own taking that of what encloses it.
README section 7.1; RATIONALE R-67.

[[faulty-file-prints-faults]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A file whose specification carries a fault prints no tree and its faults instead.
README section 7.1.

[[interview-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
An injected runner's answer to 'app --hwut-info' is read as a specification -- title, 'CHOICES:', 'HAPPY:', 'SAME;', 'INTERACTIVE;' -- under caps; an answer that is no block, an empty one or none yields no specification and no fault.
README section 3 'INTERVIEW'; RATIONALE R-44, X-SILENT.

[[interview-only-where-silent]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
Where a runner is given, only a file neither carrier speaks for is asked; a file carrying a header or named under 'apps' is never run to be read.
README section 3; RATIONALE R-44 NOTE, X-SILENT COST.

[[info-dat-not-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut-info.dat' is not read; a directory's title is the 'title' of its 'hwut.conf', validated like any key.
RATIONALE X-INFO-DAT.

[[header-amended]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A scope or an entry is set, added, extended or removed in an author's header of any comment style or in 'hwut.conf' by text surgery: every other byte stays.
RATIONALE X-AMEND.

[[feature-statements-read:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
A TEST directory's 'hwut-features.conf' is read as stated -- the features under 'childs' (name = key, title, doc, documents, keys) and the keys of the whole file.
README section 13; RATIONALE R-83; spec/23-features.

[[features-linked-in-block]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
'features' in a test's block links at the root for every choice; a choice's list replaces the root's and '[]' links the choice to nothing; a name twice, an empty name or a non-string is refused.
README section 13; RATIONALE R-83; HOWTO-FEATURES section 1.

[[statement-faults-named:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
What a TEST directory's feature file states that is not read is named with its place -- an aggregate's words, a directory reference and the vocabulary of the past among it.
RATIONALE R-83; HOWTO-FEATURES section 2 ('statement, not read').

[[feature-states:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
Over a tree every TEST directory carries its features, the runs linked to each with the verdict of 'GOOD/book.csv', the runs linking nothing and the links to names not defined; a component file of the past is read by nothing.
README section 13; RATIONALE R-83; HOWTO-FEATURES section 2.

[[features-follow-moved-test:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The features a moved test links to are carried into the target TEST directory's feature file under their names, the file written where none stands, and the central file follows: the leaf repointed to the target's directory id (registered where it has none), retired where the target defines the feature alike.
README section 13; RATIONALE R-91.

[[removal-names-unproven]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
The features a removed test run leaves without a linked run are named.
README section 13 ('unproven_by_removal').

[[registry-read:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut-root-features.conf' is read lazily as stated -- 'directory_map' (id -> path), the 'tree' of aggregates and leaves (external id = internal id '<directory id>:<name>') and 'retired' -- and every id stands once: what stands twice, is no identifier (UAX #31, NFC), or was retired and bound again is a fault named with its line.
spec 23 VERSION 10 sections 3, 4; RATIONALE R-96 ('feature_registry.py').

[[registry-states:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A claim is IMPLEMENTED, OPEN or RETIRED with a verdict beside it: a leaf from its linked runs, an aggregate from the claims below it; OPEN is always fail and RETIRED has no verdict.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 6 (o-14); RATIONALE R-96.

[[detector-judges:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
ONE detector judges the central file against the tree -- directories without an id or gone, twins, names defined and in no line, names retired and defined again, the vocabulary of the past -- for 'register' and for the sanitizer alike; it detects and never writes, retires or guesses.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 7; RATIONALE R-96 ('feature_detector.py').

[[detector-pairs:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A gone and a new are paired only where it is certain -- a directory by the fingerprint of its feature names, mutually best, unique and at least half; a leaf the only one gone and new in its directory, or of an equal name elsewhere -- and every pairing is named on its proposal line; else 'unpaired' lists the candidates.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 7; RATIONALE R-96.

[[platform-list-read]]
The outermost 'hwut-root.conf' says 'platform = [ ... ]': entries { name, kind, tools, ... }, 'local' with 'tools' alone, read in order as (name, spec) pairs, 'tools' a tuple; 'requires = [ ... ]' read in a root and a 'hwut.conf', added down the hwut.conf files, replaced at an inner root.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, sections 1, 10; intend/26 o-50, o-54, o-56, o-57.

[[platform-faults-named]]
A 'platform' list that does not read is refused by name -- not a list, empty, a bare name other than "local", a name missing or twice, 'local' with keys, a kind missing or unknown, and the kind's own keys checked by the kind -- and its bubble runs nothing.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 1; intend/42 rulings 14, 15, 16.

[[platform-root-only]]
'platform' stands in 'hwut-root.conf' alone: in a tree level's or a test directory's 'hwut.conf' it is refused with the reason.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 1; intend/42 rulings 3, 10.

[[platform-bubble-decides-alone]]
A bubble's platforms reach its own test directories and stop at an inner bubble's root, which says its own or none.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 3; intend/42 rulings 10, 19.

[[plan-determined]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
Determination turns a wish and an explored directory into a plan: BUILD nodes from the build interview, one SESSION node per interactive file, TEST nodes in selection order, ordering links out of 'dependency' and the collision group as an exclusion set.
README section 7; RATIONALE P-3, P-5.

[[dependency-closure]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A case a wished case depends on enters the plan by implication, to a fixed point through a chain, marked with the target that required it.
README sections 1, 7; RATIONALE P-7.

[[lone-exclusion-dropped]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt
A collision group is pruned to the members the plan holds and dropped where fewer than two remain.
README section 7.

[[empty-selection-reported]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A wish matching nothing yields an empty plan and a report, not a refusal.
README section 7; RATIONALE P-9.

[[misdep-marked]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A selected case whose dependencies cannot be met stands in the plan marked '[MISDEP]', its dependants likewise.
README section 1; RATIONALE P-6.

[[deterministic-plan]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
The same wish on the same directory determines the same plan, byte for byte in print.
RATIONALE P-4 (COST), P-12; README section 4.

[[node-kinds]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A plan node is a TEST, a BUILD or a SESSION with a name unique in the plan; a TEST node carries '[MISDEP]', its provenance and the target that implied it.
README section 1; RATIONALE P-5, P-7.

[[derived-tables]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt
A plan derives at construction who runs before whom, what each build or session supports, and who supports each test.
README section 4.

[[provision-stage]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
Every node answers its rung of the provision ladder, derived from its kind: BUILD for a build, EXECUTE for a session and a test.
README section 9; RATIONALE P-18.

[[construction-laws]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A plan violating a construction law -- a doubled name, a dangling or mistyped link, a foreign session, an ordering cycle, a link leaving a '[MISDEP]' node for a healthy one, an exclusion member covering nothing -- is refused by name.
README section 4; RATIONALE P-6.

[[canonical-print]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A plan prints as one canonical text in construction order: nodes with their annotations, ordering and supports links, exclusion sets, '(none)' for an empty section.
README section 5; RATIONALE P-4, P-12.

[[tree-plan]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A wish over a tree yields one plan per test directory in walk order, each printed under its directory heading with the reports determination made there.
README section 8; RATIONALE P-17.

[[tree-plan-faults-printed]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt
The printed tree plan states the walk's faults first and each directory's exploration faults under its heading.
README section 8.

[[frame-outside-plan]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A tree plan entry carries its directory's 'on_entry' and 'on_exit'; the scripts appear in no printed plan.
README introduction and section 8; RATIONALE P-10.

[[wish-keywords]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
The selection keywords '--fail', '--pass', '--since=', '--until=' and '--glob' are read off a command line and written back as one; arguments that are none of them are handed back untouched.
README section 6; RATIONALE P-8, P-15.

[[time-anchors]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A point in time is a span back from now or an anchor -- today, yesterday, last-week, last-month, a weekday, a month -- naming one instant at 00:00 UTC.
README section 6; RATIONALE P-15.

[[wish-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
An unreadable point, a keyword without its value, '--fail' beside '--pass' and an empty '--since'/'--until' window are refused, naming the argument.
README section 6; RATIONALE P-15.

[[base-needs-bookkeeper]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A wish asking the book of verdicts is refused where no Bookkeeper is handed in.
README sections 6, 8; RATIONALE P-8, P-17.

[[cases-by-glob]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A glob selects over file and choice with '*', '?' and '[ ]', a bare file glob taking every choice; several globs are OR'ed and an empty match is legal.
README section 6; RATIONALE P-8, P-9.

[[cases-by-base]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
'--fail', '--pass', '--since=' and '--until=' select by the last recorded run, questions of different kinds AND'ed; only '--until=' wants a case never run.
README section 6; RATIONALE P-15.

[[required-caps]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
In force for a case are the caps carrying a default, always, and an unfielded cap only where it is stated to confine, the choice's word standing over the application's.
exploration RATIONALE R-80 (2).

[[unenforceable-cap-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A case requiring a cap the platform cannot enforce is refused at the plan's gate, the reason naming the cap, the platform and the way out.
exploration RATIONALE R-48, R-80.

[[cap-lack-acknowledged]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
An acknowledgement of the platform's lack in 'hwut-root.conf' lifts the refusal for that cap on that platform alone.
exploration RATIONALE R-80.

[[wish-taken-by-face]]
A face names the wish words it takes; the others are not read and come back as typed, in order, for the face's own reader to refuse by name; a short spelling is taken by its long one; where the first bare word ends the reading, nothing after it is read.
services RATIONALE E-219; engine/orchestrator/plan/wish.py 'parse_wish'.

[[wish-one-reader]]
Every face that selects cases reads its command line through 'parse_wish'; no face builds its own wish, and every one that does is listed.
services RATIONALE E-219.

[[wish-platform-words]]
'-p <platform>' adds a platform to those specified, 'local' among them unless '--exclude-local' takes it out; read where the face runs, each name once and in order, spelt back; refused by name elsewhere; '--exclude-local' with no '-p' refused; a '-p' no bubble requires is a NOTE.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 2; intend/42 rulings 9, 11, 12.

[[wish-far-argv]]
For a bubble handed to a platform the wish becomes the far run's words: targets and directory rules re-rooted at the bubble, one outside dropped, a bare name kept, nothing wanted there -- not handed; every other word as given, no '-p', and '--as-platform=' naming the platform its 'local' is.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 THE RULE.

[[report-stream]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A run reports itself as a queue of events -- 'tree-begun' with the directories in walk order, then per directory its begin, frame, runs and end, 'tree-done', one closing 'None' -- with root-relative directories and a stated clock.
README introduction, sections 1, 2; RATIONALE O-1, O-2.

[[wish-refused-at-call]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Determination happens before the first event: a refused wish raises at the call and no queue is answered.
README introduction; RATIONALE O-1.

[[faults-in-stream]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt
The walk's faults and each directory's exploration faults and determination reports travel the stream as 'fault' and 'report' events.
README section 2.

[[failures-as-verdicts]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Whatever keeps a test from running is reported as that test's 'run-ended' with a verdict and its cause: a broken build, the tests it leaves unsupported, a '[MISDEP]' case -- also where no 'run-begun' went before.
README section 1; RATIONALE O-3.

[[finer-report-word]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
'run-ended' keeps the coarse verdict and carries the operation's own word as 'report' where one is known.
README section 5; RATIONALE O-6.

[[launch-failed-lifted]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A test whose report is 'test-app-launch-failed' carries the verdict 'launch-failed'.
RATIONALE O-6.

[[frame-per-directory]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A failing 'on_entry' dispatches nothing in its directory, 'on_exit' still runs, and the other directories are unaffected.
README section 2; RATIONALE O-11.

[[raising-unit-contained]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A directory unit that raises -- a held lock, a raising dispatcher -- becomes a 'fault' and a 'dir-done good=false'; the other units run on.
README section 2; RATIONALE O-13.

[[empty-wish-run]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
A wish matching nothing anywhere runs to a good end: 'report' events, empty plans, 'tree-done good=true'.
plan RATIONALE P-9; README section 2.

[[warning-event]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A finding of determination that decides nothing travels as a tree-level 'warning' event before the first run.
RATIONALE O-26.

[[summary-fold]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
The summary of a run is folded from the stream alone: verdicts and causes per node, frames, faults, reports, and the goodness of each directory and of the tree.
README section 3; RATIONALE O-4.

[[vocabulary-format]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
The format description states the promise -- fields only added, kinds never removed -- and every kind with its fields for an outside consumer.
README section 1; RATIONALE O-2.

[[emitter-refuses]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
An event of unknown kind, or with a missing, mistyped or unknown field, is refused at emission.
README section 1; RATIONALE O-2.

[[receiver-dispatch]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A receiver gets each event on its named handler; a newer stream's extra field is dropped there yet visible to 'on_any', and an unknown kind reaches 'on_unknown'.
README section 4; RATIONALE O-5.

[[receiver-misfit]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A known kind missing a required field or carrying a wrong type, and a thing that is no event, reach 'on_misfit'.
README section 4; RATIONALE O-5.

[[parameters-reach-owners]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Every parameter an author states reaches the component that owns it -- the tolerances compare's configuration, 'same' the bookkeeper's naming law, 'execute' the call, a pype the canonicaliser -- and a member reaching nothing is named.
README section 6; RATIONALE O-9.

[[constraints-enforced]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A page's constraints reach compare compiled, and compare judges by them.
RATIONALE O-34.

[[caps-inherited]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Caps reach the procsitter by inheritance: the choice's word over the application's over the procsitter's own default, an unstated cap never a zero and a sibling untouched.
README section 6; RATIONALE O-9, O-20, O-21.

[[translation-refused]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A language no interpreter is declared for and a marker pair that is no pair are refused by name, never guessed into a default.
README sections 5, 6; RATIONALE O-9.

[[real-processes]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
The real dispatcher runs a directory's builds, tests and interactive sessions as supervised processes: a real make, a pype filter canonicalising stdout, one standing session serving its choices.
README section 5; RATIONALE O-7.

[[directory-lock]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
The real dispatcher holds one directory lock for its lifetime; a directory already held is refused at the door.
README section 5; RATIONALE O-7.

[[store-knob]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
With recording off a run stores no candidate; by default every subject is stored beside its freshness sidecar.
README section 5 ('record', n-1); RATIONALE O-8.

[[directory-strategy]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
The strategy decides when a directory's unit starts: 'linear' one after another, 'successor' the next beside the current, 'parallel' all at once.
README section 2a; RATIONALE O-15.

[[bundled-strategy]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/20-bundled-strategy.txt
'bundled' opens a further directory only where the open ones cannot fill the budget, and wakes when a slot comes free.
RATIONALE O-29, O-31; intend 20 sections 1, 3.

[[strategy-contract]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A strategy without 'may_start' is refused; results come back in walk order whatever the ending order, and an empty unit list ends at once.
README section 2a; RATIONALE O-15.

[[strategy-words]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
One '--strategy=' value names a directory strategy and a selection order in any order, long or short; a word of neither, an empty word and two words of one kind are refused.
RATIONALE O-27, O-31.

[[strategy-defaults]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Where the value names no strategy it is 'successor'; where it names no selection order it is longest-first.
RATIONALE O-27, O-29, O-31.

[[choices-together]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Under longest-first the slowest application goes first, weighed as the sum of its choices, its choices together and the slowest of them first.
RATIONALE O-32.

[[unmeasured-last]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A node or an application nobody has measured goes after every measured one.
RATIONALE O-30, O-32.

[[directories-ordered]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Directories are started in the selection order too, a directory weighing its longest measured case.
RATIONALE O-28, O-33.

[[bubble-decided]]
Before the first event every bubble the walk meets is decided from the tools it requires, the platforms the outermost root defines and the command: the first platform defined that announces them all and is specified; none -- not run, 'available'; 'local' -- run here; another -- not run, 'reachable', where it cannot be reached. A directory below requiring a tool its platform does not announce is not entered, said.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, sections 3, 10; intend/26 o-54, o-57, o-58.

[[bubble-refused]]
Confs that cannot be decided are refused at the call, before anything is explored, each named: a 'platform' in an inner root, a tool 'hwut-tools.rst' does not explain.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 10; intend/26 o-52, o-53.

[[bubble-inner-decided-alone]]
A bubble not run hides none of its inner bubbles: each is decided on its own, with the same specified platforms.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 3; intend/42 ruling 19.

[[bubble-not-run-untouched]]
A bubble not run is not explored: nothing of it interviewed, no book, no 'TMP/', no 'OUT/' written; it stands in the stream as one 'not-run' event, counted nowhere.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, sections 3, 6; intend/42 ruling 21.

[[bubble-handed-hides-inner]]
A bubble handed to a platform is not walked here; its inner bubbles are decided over there.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 3; intend/42 rulings 3, 19.

[[bubble-handed-run-there]]
A bubble handed to a platform is run there by one far 'hwut.run', its 'local' that platform: its events reach the near stream as they happen, each below the bubble and carrying 'platform', its verdicts counted as local ones -- the same verdict per case as the bubble run alone.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, sections 4, 5; intend/42 rulings 3, 5, 20, THE RULE.

[[bubble-handed-ended]]
A near run ended by a signal while a bubble runs on its platform ends the far run with it.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 ruling 14; services E-55.

[[bubble-play-decided]]
'hwut.play' decides its directory's bubble as 'hwut.run' does: refused where it meets two specified platforms, not played where none, played there -- its lines relayed, its recording on the same disk -- where it is handed, played here as ever else.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 3; intend/42 ruling 7; ruled 2026-11-03 'yes to play'.

[[bubble-book-says-where]]
A verdict measured on a platform stands in its book with that platform's name, written there by the far run; 'hwut.report' reads it without '-p' and says it beside the case, and in JSON.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 6; intend/42 rulings 7, 21.

[[bubble-far-empty-no-fault]]
A bubble handed to a platform whose far wish selects nothing ends EMPTY: no fault of the platform's, no verdict, nothing counted -- the near run answers EMPTY as it does for a wish of its own that selects nothing.
engine/platforms '_status.py'; services RATIONALE E-221.

[[concurrent-dispatch]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt
Nodes nothing stands between start together, and the scheduler acts on the first ending rather than waiting for a wave.
README section 2; RATIONALE S-1, S-3.

[[readiness-admission]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A node may start only when its ordering sources are terminal, its supporters ended good and no member of its exclusion sets runs; starting a node that is not ready is refused.
README section 1.

[[ordering-link]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt
A node waits for its predecessor to reach a terminal state; a predecessor ending bad releases it all the same.
README section 1; RATIONALE S-2.

[[exclusion-set]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt
Members of an exclusion set never run at the same time, while a node outside the set runs beside them.
README section 1; RATIONALE S-3.

[[supports-carry-verdict]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A build ending bad leaves the tests it supports UNSUPPORTED and undispatched; a build ending good admits them.
README section 1.

[[session-lifecycle]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
An interactive session is opened once and closed when every test it supports is terminal; a session that does not launch fails its tests undispatched and is never closed.
README sections 1, 2.

[[misdep-never-dispatched]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A '[MISDEP]' node is terminal before anything runs, is never dispatched and stands as a failure.
README section 1.

[[frame-scripts]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
'on_entry' runs before any dispatch and its failing stops the run; 'on_exit' runs after all work in either case.
README section 2.

[[worker-bound]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A worker bound limits how much stands at once without changing the work done; a bound below one is refused.
README section 2.

[[shared-budget]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt
Schedulers sharing one budget never hold more slots together than it has, frame scripts taking a slot like any work.
README section 2; RATIONALE S-4.

[[run-report]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A run answers a report: the frame's outcome, the state of every node and what was dispatched in start order, good only where the frame stood and no node failed.
README section 2 (CRunReport).

[[ready-count]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/20-bundled-strategy.txt
A scheduler answers how many nodes could start now, asked of the plan state anew and never cached.
intend 20 section 3 (1); run RATIONALE O-31.

[[node-selection-order]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Among the admissible nodes the scheduler starts the one the selection order puts first, plan order breaking every tie.
run RATIONALE O-27, O-28, O-30.

[[disk-room-before-start]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/disk_guard.py, engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt
A case starts only where its disk has room for it above the floor; it waits while a case running on that disk may make room, and is refused with its numbers where none can.
disk_guard.py; RATIONALE S-5 (D-24).

[[capability-board]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
Before a call the board says, cap by cap, whether this platform watches it and by which utility; 'network', 'file_handle_max_n' and 'write_directory_list' are watched nowhere.
MANUAL 4, THE CAPABILITY BOARD.

[[unenforced-caps-reported]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A cap the platform cannot enforce is reported in the result's '.unenforced'; the other caps still hold.
MANUAL 1, 4, 8.

[[cap-hit-ends-call-and-is-named]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A call that exceeds its wall clock, CPU time, memory, process count, file size or disk usage, or leaves too little free disk, is ended alone and the result names that cap.
MANUAL 4; 6, Containment Overview.

[[silence-cap]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A call silent on both production ports for the output gap is ended as stalled; a call that flushes is left alone, and without the cap silence is no fault.
MANUAL 4, max_output_gap_sec.

[[clean-under-caps]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A workload that stays under every cap completes as OK_COMPLETED.
MANUAL 6 gives the token; that no cap fires falsely is stated nowhere.

[[exit-classes]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
Exit zero is OK_COMPLETED; a non-zero exit and a signal death are FAIL_COMPLETED with the exit code kept.
MANUAL 6, Containment Overview.

[[missing-program-is-a-result]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A program that cannot be spawned is the result FAIL_LAUNCH, not an exception; the command is an argv sequence run without a shell.
MANUAL 5; 6. That a bare string is rejected is stated nowhere.

[[stderr-tail-kept]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
Without a stderr handler the result holds the last 100 lines of stderr.
MANUAL 6. The bound on a line's length is stated nowhere.

[[created-files-listed]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
The result lists the files and directories the call made and left under its work directory and its scratch ground -- not what it removed, not what stood before, not 'TMP/'.
MANUAL 6, .created_tuple, Scratch ground.

[[no-child-left-behind]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
No process of a supervised call survives it: the whole group is killed and escaped descendants are swept.
MANUAL 5, KILL LADDER, straggler rule.

[[interrupted-run-leaks-nothing]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A run interrupted from outside kills its process group at once and leaves no child.
MANUAL 5, NOTE.

[[stop-event-stops-call]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A set stop event ends the call as FAIL_STOPPED.
MANUAL 5; 6.

[[handler-fault-surfaces]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
An exception in a caller's handler kills the process, leaks nothing, and then surfaces.
MANUAL 5, last paragraph.

[[environment-overlay]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A given 'env' is merged over the parent's environment at spawn.
MANUAL, ENVIRONMENT OVERLAY.

[[tee-fans-out]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A tee hands a production to every consumer, complete and byte for byte.
MANUAL 7.2.

[[chain-feeds-through]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A chain feeds its first stage from a link and each stage from the one before, with one record per stage.
MANUAL 7.3.

[[chain-stops-upstream]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A chain stage that ends while an upstream stage still runs stops the upstream, which reads FAIL_STOPPED.
MANUAL 7.3, rule 2.

[[chain-listener]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A stdout handler given to a chain stage listens beside the chain edge and does not replace it; the edge is fed first.
MANUAL 7.4.

[[chain-owned-key-refused]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A chain stage that names 'stdin_reader' or 'stop_event' in its extra arguments is refused at construction.
MANUAL 7.5.

[[man-in-the-middle]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
A second supervised call answers a subject's prompts on its stdin while a consumer keeps listening, each call with its own attribution record.
MANUAL 7.6.

[[kill-names-its-cap-on-every-road]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Through 'hwut.run' every cap violation reaches the hints by the cap's name, on the plain and on the interactive road; a choice that never began says the process had already died.
orchestrator/run RATIONALE O-21, O-19.

[[disk-cap-counts-the-call-own]]
documents: engine/procsitter/MANUAL.txt
The disk cap counts what the call holds: its scratch ground and the files it created under the work directory -- not what stood before, not a sibling's scratch.
MANUAL 4, max_disk_mb.

[[line-round-trip]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
Every event kind of the vocabulary, with every field it can carry, is written as one line '<t>:<kind>:<content>' and read back as the same event.
orchestrator/run RATIONALE O-24 (COST) names the line language; the claim itself stands in 'line.py' only.

[[content-forms]]
The content is a string message, a CSV record or a JSON object by the event's kind, quoted so that it reads back unambiguously, the time first with two decimals.
Stated in no document; 'line.py' only.

[[malformed-line-refused]]
A line that is not of the grammar is refused and the fault named.
Stated in no document; 'line.py' only.

[[face-exit-status-law]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
Every face ends with one of four statuses: 0 nothing refused or failed, 1 a fault or a failed test, 2 the command line cannot be read, 3 it reads and asks for nothing.
README 'EXIT STATUS'; RATIONALE E-1; README.md 'The commands'.

[[face-signal-ends-quietly]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A terminal signal ends a face with the one line '<face> ended forcefully.' on stderr, the shell's own 128+n status and no traceback.
README '_exit.py'; RATIONALE E-55.

[[face-sigpipe-quiet]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A face whose reader hung up goes quiet and ends with status 141.
README 'EXIT STATUS' (141 SIGPIPE); RATIONALE E-1.

[[face-refusal-by-name]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A command line a face cannot read -- an unknown option, a surplus word, a directory that does not exist -- is refused at the door by name, with the usage line and status 2.
README 'EXIT STATUS' (2 REFUSED); RATIONALE E-1.

[[face-empty-said]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A command line that reads and selects nothing -- a wish matching no case, a tree without a test directory -- is answered EMPTY with status 3 and is no fault.
README 'EXIT STATUS' (3 EMPTY); RATIONALE E-1, E-2.

[[face-fault-keeps-output]]
documents: services/README.txt
A fault does not withhold the output: a directory with one broken header prints the fault, then the plan of what stands, and answers 1.
README, the paragraph after the list of faces; 'hwut.plan --help'.

[[face-help-page]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
Every face answers '--help' on stdout with status 0 as a manual page -- NAME, USAGE, DESCRIPTION -- and each example the page states is accepted by the face itself.
README first paragraph; RATIONALE E-32; README.md 'The commands'.

[[face-directory-option]]
documents: services/README.txt, ../README.md
Every face takes '--directory=<path>' and reads the current directory where none is given.
README first paragraph; README.md 'The commands'.

[[face-did-you-mean]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A mistyped option or word is answered with the nearest known spellings, at most three.
RATIONALE E-73, E-81.

[[face-retired-word-named]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--yes' is refused by name wherever it once stood, with the word that replaced it.
RATIONALE E-66, E-68.

[[face-completion-table]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Each face prints its options as a completion table read off its own parser.
RATIONALE E-84, E-85.

[[complete-tab-fast]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A TAB is answered by 'hwut --command-line-complete <shell> <line>' importing nothing of the tree but the completer and the table setup wrote.
RATIONALE E-151; adm/WORK/gathered/0s.

[[complete-static]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A TAB completes the face names and each face's options from the table setup wrote; a face without a parser completes nothing.
RATIONALE E-151 (STATIC).

[[complete-cheap]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A TAB completes an option's words, the directories and test applications here, and -- after an application -- the choices its header declares.
RATIONALE E-151 (CHEAP); gathered/0s open (b).

[[complete-dear]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A TAB completes the labels of the tree's 'hwut-root.labels' and the targets the current directory's 'hwut.conf' binds.
RATIONALE E-151 (DEAR).

[[complete-silent]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A completion never fails loudly: whatever goes wrong, no candidate, nothing on stderr, status 0.
RATIONALE E-151 (THE LAWS).

[[complete-shells]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
One completer answers in three renderings: bash, zsh and PowerShell.
RATIONALE E-151.

[[complete-setup]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt
'hwut --command-line-setup' writes the option table of every face from its parser as one byte-compiled module; a face without a parser enters by name.
RATIONALE E-151; doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt.

[[complete-bash-script]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt
Sourcing 'services/shell/hwut.bash' registers every hwut face on PATH for TAB completion in bash.
doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt.

[[complete-zsh-script]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt
Sourcing 'services/shell/hwut.zsh' after 'compinit' registers every hwut face on PATH for TAB completion in zsh.
doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt.

[[complete-powershell-script]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt
Dot-sourcing 'services/shell/hwut.ps1' registers every hwut face on PATH for TAB completion in PowerShell.
doc/HOWTO-COMPLETION.txt.

[[face-choice-roster]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Where the words name one application that exists, a missing or unknown choice is answered with the choices that application reports, in header order.
RATIONALE E-72.

[[face-as-library]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A face answers a caller that is no command line: 'main(argv, write)' takes a writer, and 'do(Request)' returns a plain Result or streams its events to a sink, without output and without exit.
README first paragraph; RATIONALE E-101 .. E-105.

[[tree-boundary-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A tree without 'hwut-root.conf' is refused by every face in one line that names the way out, 'hwut.sanitize root'.
RATIONALE E-25; 'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (no option).

[[sanitize-root]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.sanitize root <dir>' writes 'hwut-root.conf' with its language table; without <dir> it asks with a menu of the repository roots, the current directory and 'q', and refuses where nobody can answer.
RATIONALE E-25; 'hwut.sanitize --help' (root).

[[preferences-person-overlay]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, bin/README.txt
A person's '~/.hwut.conf' overlays the installation's 'bin/.hwut.conf' key by key; a faulty file stops nothing and is noted once.
RATIONALE E-78, E-82; bin/README '.hwut.conf'.

[[preferences-not-project]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, bin/README.txt
A project's 'hwut.conf' may not state a preference: it is refused by name.
RATIONALE E-78; bin/README.

[[path-word-enters]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A bare word whose directory part is literal -- 'a/TEST/keep.sh', a path with '..', an absolute path -- enters that directory and performs the test there, on every face that takes test words; a relative path is read against '--directory'.
README '_target.py'; RATIONALE E-45, E-47.

[[path-word-two-directories-refused]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Two words naming two directories, and an absolute path outside '--directory', are refused.
README '_target.py'; RATIONALE E-47.

[[wish-short-form]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Bare words are a wish in short form: the first names files, each further one a choice -- the same selection as '--glob'.
README 'THE CONSOLIDATION' (short form); RATIONALE E-21.

[[wish-glob]]
documents: services/README.txt
'--glob <target>' selects by file name and choice name with fnmatch wildcards; several are a union, and keywords of different kinds narrow each other.
README 'hwut.plan'; '--help' SELECTION.

[[wish-fail-pass]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--fail' and '--pass' select by the verdict the book last recorded; a case never run is wanted by neither.
README 'hwut.plan'; '--help' SELECTION; RATIONALE E-35.

[[wish-since-until]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--since=<point>' and '--until=<point>' select by when the last run was recorded, a point being a span ('2h') or an anchor ('yesterday') in UTC; '--until' alone also wants a case never run.
README 'hwut.plan'; RATIONALE E-80.

[[wish-faster-than]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--faster-than=<seconds>' selects the runs this machine last saw finish faster than that; a run it never saw is not wanted.
'--help' SELECTION; RATIONALE E-36.

[[wish-fail-beside-pass-refused]]
documents: services/README.txt
'--fail' beside '--pass' is refused.
'hwut.plan --help'.

[[wish-exclude]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--exclude <target>' outranks every include: a case it names is not wanted, whatever else selects it.
RATIONALE E-15.

[[wish-dir-rules]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--dir' and '--exclude-dir' are read in command-line order and the last one matching a directory decides; where none matches, the directory is wanted unless the first of them is a '--dir'.
RATIONALE E-15.

[[wish-wishlist-file]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--wishlist <file>' takes the file's lines as targets, '#' lines and blanks dropped, './' meaning the file's own directory, so what 'hwut.report.list' prints reads back and 'hwut.run --wishlist' runs exactly the listed cases.
RATIONALE E-13; 'hwut.report.list --help'.

[[wish-wishlist-elided]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A sorted wishlist may elide: ':/' repeats the previous line's directory and ':/:' its file; a ditto without a predecessor is refused by name.
RATIONALE E-30, E-96 mention the marks; the format itself is stated in no document of services.

[[wish-label-unknown-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'-l <expr>' selects the runs a label expression names; a label the tree does not know is refused by name with status 2 at every face that takes a wish.
RATIONALE E-19, E-20, E-112.

[[wish-language-unknown-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--language=<name>' selects by the language of the test application; a language the tree does not know is refused by name at every face that takes a wish.
RATIONALE E-26.

[[wish-label-silence]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Without a label expression the standard label 'meta' is silent: a glob whose every match it silences earns a warning naming the remedy, and a literally named target overrides the silence.
RATIONALE E-20, E-21; '--help' SELECTION (-l).

[[wish-unaccepted]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--unaccepted' selects every case for which no nominal stands, at every face that takes a wish.
RATIONALE E-58.

[[run-gate-nominal]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.run' runs a case only where its nominal stands: a case without one is not run and fails 'no GOOD file', case by case, and an accepted test the register lacks is entered and said.
RATIONALE E-41.

[[run-backup-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A backup-shaped copy of a test is ignored: named nowhere, not run, not booked; 'admit' in hwut.conf makes it a candidate again.
RATIONALE E-41.

[[run-unaccepted-fails]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A GOOD carrying an 'unaccepted' region fails its case as 'unaccepted' at the next run: a decision is still owed.
README 'ACCEPT IS PARTIAL BY DEFAULT'; RATIONALE E-60.

[[run-stderr-fails]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A test that writes to stderr fails 'unexpected stderr' unless its own block declares 'tolerance { stderr_ignored = true }'.
RATIONALE E-136.

[[run-stained-not-run]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A stained choice is not run: it fails 'UNSTABLE -- not run' and its directory fails with it.
RATIONALE E-11.

[[run-constraint-verdict]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A nominal or an output that breaks the test's constraints fails the case by the name 'constraint'; the finding is written after the line that caused it, and a nominal at fault stains the book 'constraint'.
RATIONALE E-123.

[[run-constraint-stain-cleared]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A 'constraint' stain is taken away by the first run that passes.
RATIONALE E-123, E-136 (4).

[[run-cap-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A test stating a cap the platform cannot enforce fails 'uncapped', not run, counted, named in HINTS with the 'hwut-root.conf' entry that acknowledges it; the run exits 1.
RATIONALE E-135.

[[run-cap-refused-every-door]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.plan' names the same case 'UNCAPPED [FAIL]', and the faces that run without a plan -- 'hwut.accept', 'hwut.play', 'hwut.merge', 'hwut.report.details' -- refuse to run it, by the same reason.
RATIONALE E-135 names 'hwut.run' and 'hwut.cov.run' only; exploration R-80.

[[run-no-coverage-option]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.run' takes no '--coverage': the coverage run is 'hwut.cov.run'.
RATIONALE E-132, E-133.

[[run-points-to-help]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.run' closes its HINTS with a pointer to 'hwut.help' where a failure stands that is no plain deviation from GOOD, and with none otherwise.
README 'hwut.help'.

[[run-ignores-features]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.run' does nothing with features: a test run that links to no feature runs, is judged, and the run says nothing of it.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 9; spec 23 section 6.

[[run-display-tiers]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
'--verbose', '--plain', '--quiet' and '--silent' each show a subset of the tier above; two together are refused, and '--silent' prints nothing on stdout while a fault still goes to stderr.
README 'hwut.run'; 'hwut.run --help' RENDERING; display D-4, D-5.

[[run-strategy]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
'--strategy' orders a run: 'longest-first' starts what this machine last measured longest, 'shortest-first' the other end, 'name-sorted' the tree's own order; 'bundled' is a word of the option and not the default.
'hwut.run --help' EXECUTION; run O-31, O-33.

[[run-timing]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.run --timing' keeps the run's cadence beside each candidate.
RATIONALE E-10; 'hwut.run --help'.

[[run-no-store]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.run --no-store' records no subject, and the verdict still enters the book.
README 'hwut.run'; 'hwut.run --help' EXECUTION.

[[run-jobs]]
documents: services/README.txt, ../README.md
'hwut.run --jobs=<n>' bounds the work standing at once.
README 'hwut.run'; README.md 'Run'.

[[run-pype-own-python]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A test's pype runs under hwut's own Python whatever its she-bang or file mode.
RATIONALE E-28.

[[run-pype-defect-fails]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/run/RATIONALE.txt
A pype that is missing or does not parse is a defect of the test and fails it by the word 'pype'; nothing is compared.
run O-18.

[[run-execute-expansion]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'execute' expands '$file', '$filestem' and '$choice' in the call; a placed '$choice' is not appended again and an unplaced one is the last argument.
exploration R-68.

[[run-same-nominal]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
Under 'same = true' one nominal stands for every choice of a test, and accept, run, report.details, rename and remove treat it so.
exploration R-45; bookkeeper B-30.

[[run-directory-frame]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'on_entry' and 'on_exit' frame a directory's run, 'collision' keeps tests apart, and 'dependency' orders them without asking for success.
exploration R-31 .. R-36.

[[run-dependency-cycle]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A dependency cycle fails its members and their dependants as 'no-dep', by name, and the rest of the directory runs.
exploration R-33, R-35; 'hwut.plan --help' ([MISDEP], [CYCLE]).

[[run-pattern-before-number]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
An 'eq_pattern' claims its text before a number inside it is compared as a number.
compare C-14.

[[run-table-separator]]
The separator of a table region is the one the GOOD file states, whatever the output says.
No document of services; found by the defect report of 2026-10-10.

[[run-test-directory-name]]
A tree whose test directories carry another name ('test_directory') gives the same result from above the directory and from inside it.
No document of services; found by the defect report of 2026-10-10.

[[run-book-as-left]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
'GOOD/book.csv' is read as a person may have left it, a book under an old name is carried to the current one at the first write, and a refused acceptance takes no id.
bookkeeper B-4, B-8, B-10, B-27.

[[walk-no-entry]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
No walk enters a directory that carries 'hwut.no-entry-here.marker' -- a coverage output directory among them -- nor a test directory's 'TMP' and 'OUT'.
exploration R-85.

[[spec-word-fault]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, services/README.txt
A word a test's '@hwut' block or 'hwut.conf' may not state is a fault by name with file, line and column, and no traceback.
exploration R-4 .. R-7, R-22, R-47; README 'hwut.config.show' (syntax checked).

[[spec-block-found]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
The '@hwut {' block is found in any comment syntax and deep in a file; a bare 'hwut {' in a source file is none.
exploration R-9, R-72.

[[spec-one-carrier]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
One file has one carrier of its specification: a header and an 'apps' entry together are the directory's error, and 'app_defaults' reach every application without overwriting.
exploration R-7, R-52, R-53.

[[spec-tolerance-scope]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
Every lexical tolerance stands in the one scope 'tolerance { }'; the old root keys are unknown words, not aliases.
RATIONALE E-42; exploration R-77.

[[plan-page]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.plan' prints the plan the framework intends -- nodes, links, exclusion sets -- with the cases a selected case requires marked as implied.
README 'hwut.plan'; 'hwut.plan --help'.

[[plan-wish-narrows]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The wish 'hwut.run' takes narrows the plan; a wish matching nothing yields an empty plan and a report.
README 'hwut.plan'; RATIONALE E-2.

[[plan-session-node]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
'interactive = true' gives one SESSION node that supports the choices of its test, each still a TEST node.
'hwut.plan --help' (nodes); plan P-5.

[[plan-agrees-with-run]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.plan' refuses and fails exactly the cases 'hwut.run' does not run, by the same reason.
RATIONALE E-41.

[[plan-misdep-cycle-tags]]
documents: services/README.txt
In the plan a case whose dependencies cannot be met carries '[MISDEP]' and a link inside a dependency cycle carries '[CYCLE]'.
'hwut.plan --help'.

[[config-show-resolved]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.config.show' prints what the framework read -- syntax checked, defaults resolved -- for one file, or for the whole directory with 'hwut.conf' first.
README 'hwut.config.show'.

[[config-show-no-default]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--no-default' drops every value nobody stated.
README 'hwut.config.show'; exploration R-51.

[[config-show-provenance]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--provenance' names the file and line of every stated value, '--gnu' as 'file:line:column'; a conf above the test directory is named by its path from there.
README 'hwut.config.show'; exploration R-67.

[[config-show-reads-back]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
What 'hwut.config.show' prints is the specification language itself and can be read back.
README 'hwut.config.show' (R-50).

[[config-show-faults]]
documents: services/README.txt
A specification carrying a fault yields its faults and no tree, with status 1.
'hwut.config.show --help'.

[[config-show-directory-fault]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.config.show' of a directory with one broken header prints the fault, then the tree of what stands, and answers 1.
README, the paragraph after the list of faces.

[[config-show-labels]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.config.show' names in a LABELS section the label entries that concern what is shown, the silencing ones marked.
RATIONALE E-19 (DECISION, 'hwut.show answers section 6').

[[config-show-ids]]
'hwut.config.show --show-ids' prints the test register, a registered application whose file is absent marked VANISHED.
'hwut.config.show --help'.

[[config-show-verbose]]
'hwut.config.show --verbose' writes the null parameters too.
'hwut.config.show --help'.

[[accept-whole]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--force', '--whole' and '--as-is' are one act: the candidate becomes the nominal as it stands, nobody is asked, and the candidate is provisioned so that nothing need run first.
README 'hwut.accept' ('ARE ONE ACT'); RATIONALE E-109.

[[accept-book-and-good]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
An acceptance is a row in 'book.csv' and a file in 'GOOD/', never one without the other.
README 'WHAT ACCEPTANCE MEANS'; RATIONALE E-41.

[[accept-standing-left-alone]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Off a terminal a standing nominal that differs is named and left alone -- 'merge required', status 1 -- and '--force' overwrites it out loud.
README 'A CHANGE IS MERGED HERE', 'PROMOTION'; RATIONALE E-4, E-57.

[[accept-asks-with-reading]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Before a standing nominal is replaced the question shows the candidate, and anything but 'y' leaves the pole alone.
RATIONALE E-57.

[[accept-dont-ask]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--dont-ask' suppresses the question and decides nothing about what is done; '--force' implies it, never the reverse.
RATIONALE E-68, E-70; 'hwut.accept --help' PROMOTION.

[[accept-merge-at-terminal]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
At a terminal a differing case is merged through the engine 'hwut.merge' runs: saving writes the nominal, leaving writes nothing, and the report says which -- 'saved [FAIL]' where what was saved still differs.
README 'A CHANGE IS MERGED HERE', 'the merge engine'; RATIONALE E-59, E-88, E-89.

[[accept-stderr-refused]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A candidate whose stderr spoke is refused until the test's own block declares 'tolerance { stderr_ignored = true }'; stderr is never promoted and no switch tolerates it.
README 'STDERR IS NEVER SUBJECT TO TESTING'; RATIONALE E-5, E-110, E-136.

[[accept-incomplete-refused]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
A candidate whose stdout does not end in '<hwut-end>' is refused, and no flag bypasses it.
README 'PROMOTION'; RATIONALE E-6; README.md.

[[accept-constraint-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A candidate that breaks the test's constraints is refused by name, with '--force' and at the merge's commit alike.
RATIONALE E-124.

[[accept-stained-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.accept' refuses a stained choice by name, with the way out.
RATIONALE E-11.

[[accept-refusal-atomic]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A refusal spans the whole selection: where one selected candidate is refused, nothing is promoted.
README 'hwut.accept' (the atomic refusal); RATIONALE E-4.

[[accept-refresh]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Every face that works on a recording -- 'hwut.accept', 'hwut.merge', 'hwut.diff' -- runs nothing: no build, no run, in any circumstance; 'hwut.run' and 'hwut.play' alone make a recording.
RATIONALE E-165 (was E-40, E-122: refresh on necessity).

[[accept-force-run]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.accept --force-run' runs every selected case first, whether or not its recording is current.
README 'It RUNS ON NECESSITY'; 'hwut.accept --help'.

[[accept-walks-tree]]
documents: services/README.txt
A bare 'hwut.accept' walks the tree as 'hwut.run' does; '--directory=<path>' reads that one directory.
README 'A BARE hwut.accept WALKS THE TREE'.

[[accept-propose]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.accept.propose -o <file> [<n>]' blesses and runs nothing: it writes each differing case as replacement blocks with their own line numbers and the target under them, and names a case over the bound in one line on stdout without a target.
README 'hwut.accept.propose'; RATIONALE E-44.

[[accept-propose-only-divergence]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Only a divergence is proposed: a case without candidate, without nominal, equivalent under its tolerances, or whose run broke, is not.
README 'hwut.accept.propose' (ONLY A DIVERGENCE IS PROPOSED); RATIONALE E-44.

[[accept-apply]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.accept.apply <file>' blesses the targets the file still names and nothing else, asks nothing, and closes on 'Accepted <n>/<m>'.
README 'hwut.accept.apply'; RATIONALE E-44.

[[accept-apply-answers-every-line]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.accept.apply' answers every line of the file, a target that matched nothing included.
README 'hwut.accept.apply'.

[[interactive-session]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.merge' holds one session per differing case: take the subject whole, edit, or quit; a commit is the acceptance, and the candidate stays as the run left it.
README 'hwut.merge'; RATIONALE E-51.

[[interactive-first-nominal]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A case with no nominal is 'hwut.merge's too: the checklist offers it and the session opens on the mirror.
README 'hwut.merge' (E-60).

[[interactive-checklist]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Where more than one case differs a checklist comes first: a number works that case, '<enter>' the first unhandled, 'q' quits, '[X]' marks the handled; '--all' skips it.
README 'hwut.diff' (the checklist); RATIONALE E-50, E-65, E-67.

[[interactive-force]]
'hwut.merge --force' takes the subject whole for every differing case, without a checklist.
'hwut.merge --help'.

[[interactive-keep-tolerance]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A merge that ends with a changed tolerance asks where to keep it: [1] a TMP file, [2] the test's own header, <enter> nowhere -- after a cancel only [1] or <enter>.
README 'the keyed session' (At the end ...); RATIONALE E-119.

[[diff-two-files]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.diff SUBJECT NOMINAL' renders the verdict view on stdout and exits by the diff convention: 0 equivalent, 1 differing, 2 unusable.
README 'hwut.diff'; RATIONALE E-9, E-50.

[[diff-setup-options]]
'--numeric', '--pattern' and '--nothing' state the tolerance a two-file comparison is read under.
'hwut.diff --help'.

[[diff-reading-view]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.diff FILE' displays how compare reads one file, marked by tolerance kind.
README 'hwut.diff'; RATIONALE E-9, E-56.

[[diff-side-by-side]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'-y' renders two columns at '--width', subject left and nominal right, and a line without a partner is said on both sides of its row.
README 'hwut.diff'; RATIONALE E-49, E-142.

[[diff-store-form]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
With a wish 'hwut.diff' shows every selected case whose stdout candidate is not equivalent to its nominal, judged now under the choice's own setup, one banner each.
README 'hwut.diff' (THE STORE FORM); RATIONALE E-50.

[[diff-nothing-selected]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.diff' exits 3 where the wish selects no case.
README 'hwut.diff' ('3 no case selected').

[[diff-stains-said]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Every stain of the book is said by the viewing faces: an equivalent and stained case as a 'STAIN:' line with exit 1, a differing one as a note before its view.
RATIONALE E-141, E-136.

[[diff-fault-side]]
A broken region frame is one FAULT line naming the side at fault, output or nominal, with file and line, and no traceback.
No document of services; found by the defect report of 2026-10-10.

[[diff-constraint-finding]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The view shows a constraint finding where it stands in the candidate, after the line that caused it.
RATIONALE E-123.

[[diff-search-budget]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
A pair whose alignment spends the search budget shows as the line gone and the line come, whole.
compare C-16.

[[play-reading]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
'hwut.play' runs one choice and shows the reading of what it printed under the test's own tolerance; it judges nothing and records nothing.
README 'play.py'; RATIONALE E-22, E-74; README.md 'Run'.

[[play-views]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--raw' shows what the application produced and '--pyped' what the pype made of it, each as a named region beside the subject.
'hwut.play --help' (THE THREE VIEWS); RATIONALE E-22.

[[play-stderr-view]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--stderr' shows the error stream, marked as never tested.
RATIONALE E-5, E-22.

[[play-save]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.play' records what it ran as the case's recording, without verdict and without book entry: a new test's way to its first acceptance (E-165).
README 'hwut.accept' (the two readings of a first acceptance); RATIONALE E-40.

[[play-one-choice]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
An application offering one choice is played unasked; one offering several and given none is refused.
'hwut.play --help' (EXIT STATUS 2); RATIONALE E-72.

[[play-build-failure]]
A failing build is shown with its log as the reason there is nothing to play, with status 1.
'hwut.play --help' (EXIT STATUS 1).

[[play-empty-explained]]
A play that produced nothing to read answers EMPTY and says why: the call, what PATH resolved, the exit, the channels.
'hwut.play --help' (EXIT STATUS 3) names EMPTY; the explanation is stated in no document of services.

[[stability-verdict]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.run.stability' runs a wish '--repeat' times and faults a case whose verdict differs between repeats: the one finding that moves the exit status.
README 'stability.py'; RATIONALE E-10, E-111.

[[stability-stain]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A case convicted of instability is stained in the book; a later stability run that finds it steady takes the stain away.
RATIONALE E-11.

[[stability-bytes]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Bytes that differ under an equal verdict are a warning, and said only under '--byte-pedantic'.
RATIONALE E-113.

[[stability-length]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Cadences of differing line count are a warning, and the timing of that case is not attempted.
RATIONALE E-10; 'hwut.run.stability --help'.

[[stability-cadence]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Under '--cadence' a line whose time differs grossly between repeats is named in a warning that never stains and never moves the exit status.
RATIONALE E-10; 'hwut.run.stability --help'.

[[stability-counts-not-numbers]]
The findings are counts and names; no number the machine chose is printed unless '--verbose' asks.
'hwut.run.stability --help' (WHAT IS NEVER PRINTED).

[[report-traditional-page]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
'hwut.report' reads the books, not a run, and renders the traditional page: a block per directory, a summary with a prefix-elided directory column, each failure said once, the total.
'hwut.report --help'; RATIONALE E-14, E-17, E-70; README.md 'Report'.

[[services-report-width]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
The page is as wide as it is told by '--width', else as wide as the run on the same screen, the verdicts right-aligned.
'hwut.report --help' (THE WIDTH IS THE RUN'S); display D-33.

[[report-colour]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Only the traditional page is coloured, and only on a terminal or under '--color'; piped, under '--plain' or '--out' it is plain.
RATIONALE E-69; 'hwut.report --help'.

[[report-machine-formats]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--format=junit|tap|json' renders the same results as JUnit XML, TAP 13 and JSON, and '--out=<path>' writes them to a file.
RATIONALE E-17; 'hwut.report --help'.

[[report-stain-fails]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A stained case is a failure in every format, never 'skipped', also where its last repeat passed.
RATIONALE E-17 (amended 2026-10-08).

[[report-no-good-file]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A case whose GOOD file does not stand is reported as the failure 'no GOOD file' in every format.
'hwut.report --help'; display D-32.

[[report-not-in-book]]
documents: engine/display/RATIONALE.txt
A case with a GOOD file the book has never seen is reported as the failure 'not in book', with the pointer to 'hwut.help'.
'hwut.report --help'; display D-37.

[[report-agrees-with-run]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Every failure 'hwut.run' counts 'hwut.report' shows, with its reason word: a header that does not parse, a record the tree no longer declares, a dependency that cannot be met.
RATIONALE E-128.

[[report-difference-shape]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The report says how a differing output differs -- GREW, SHRANK, DIVERGED -- taken from the files afterwards, and the machine formats carry it as the reason.
RATIONALE E-29, E-31.

[[report-list-print]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.report.list' prints every case the wish selects as one wishlist line './<path>/<test> [<choice>]' in walk order, a choice-less test as its file alone, and nothing else.
README 'list.py'; RATIONALE E-13, E-137.

[[report-details-pack]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.report.details' packs a test whole on stdout -- metadata, source, GOOD, OUT with the cadence inside, stderr where it spoke -- and says what is not there.
README 'hwut.report.details'; RATIONALE E-14, E-23, E-54.

[[report-details-raw]]
documents: services/README.txt
'-r' gives the files verbatim, the sidecars as sections of their own.
README 'hwut.report.details'.

[[report-details-coverage]]
documents: services/README.txt
The pack carries the coverage of the run where an output directory is found or named by '--cov-dir'; '--no-coverage' leaves it out.
README 'hwut.report.details'; 'hwut.report.details --help'.

[[report-details-wish]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The words are the wish's: every selected case is packed in turn in walk order, and the face exits 3 where nothing is selected.
README 'hwut.report.details'; RATIONALE E-52.

[[report-timings-traditional]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.report.timings' reads the traces back as one line per case, the build time in brackets, and a tail that counts the cases and sums the run times only.
RATIONALE E-114; 'hwut.report.timings --help'.

[[report-timings-table]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--table' prints the traces as CSV, and '-o <file>' writes the report there instead of stdout.
RATIONALE E-114; 'hwut.report.timings --help'.

[[report-timings-own-machine]]
documents: engine/bookkeeper/RATIONALE.txt
Only this machine's rows are read: another machine's are passed over, and a directory it never measured is EMPTY.
'hwut.report.timings --help'; bookkeeper B-11.

[[report-timings-recursive]]
'hwut.report.timings -r' walks the tree below the directory.
'hwut.report.timings --help'.

[[report-features-tree]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.report.features' prints the tree of 'hwut-root-features.conf' below a directory -- aggregate, leaf with its state IMPLEMENTED ok, IMPLEMENTED fail or OPEN, the linked runs with their verdicts, RETIRED -- and runs nothing; inside a TEST directory its leaves are the whole page.
HOWTO-FEATURES sections 1, 2; RATIONALE E-139; spec 23 section 5.

[[report-features-outside]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Below the tree the page names what stands outside the relation: a link to a feature the directory does not define, a document that does not stand, a statement that cannot be read, a feature not registered, what the central file and the tree disagree on.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 2; spec 23 section 5.

[[report-features-nonsense]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
A test run that links to no feature, in a TEST directory that states features, is named 'NONSENSE?' and is no failure; a directory without a feature file is named nowhere.
RATIONALE E-145; HOWTO-FEATURES section 2.

[[report-features-documents]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A feature's 'documents' are printed under it as 'see <path>' and are links in the feature document.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 1; RATIONALE E-148.

[[report-features-matrix]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--matrix' prints one table per TEST directory that carries a feature file: test runs down, features across, the verdict where a run links, the empty row and the empty column marked.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 6; RATIONALE E-143.

[[report-features-code]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--code' lists under each feature the lines only its runs executed, the shared lines as a count and the linked runs not measured, from the coverage output '--cov-dir' names or the nearest 'hwut.coverage/'; where none is found a NOTE stands and the page has no code.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 7; RATIONALE E-143.

[[report-features-document]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--html=<file>' and '--ods=<file>' write the feature document with the tabs Matrix, Tree, Features and Tests, linked among each other; the same tree yields the same bytes.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 8; RATIONALE E-146.

[[report-features-document-tree]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The document's tree folds and unfolds, filters by state and by word, and stands in the spreadsheet as row groups.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 8; RATIONALE E-147, E-148.

[[report-features-document-coverage]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
With '--cov-html=<dir>' each source in the 'Features' tab is a relative link to its coverage page at the first of the feature's own lines.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 8 (THE WAY TO THE CODE); RATIONALE E-147.

[[report-features-test-doc]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
A test's 'doc', written between three double quotes, stands in the 'Tests' tab with the common indentation removed.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 8; spec 23 section 5.

[[report-features-unnumbered]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
A feature written without a number is read and reported as '#-'.
spec 23 section 9, o-6.

[[help-explains-failures]]
documents: services/README.txt, ../README.md
'hwut.help' reads the books and explains each kind of failure that is no plain deviation from GOOD, once: its word, how many cases, how it is resolved, the cases concerned; with nothing to explain it exits 3.
README 'hwut.help'; README.md 'Report'.

[[help-explains-nonsense]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
'hwut.help' explains 'NONSENSE?' and names the test runs that link to no feature; a tree that states no features says nothing.
RATIONALE E-145; HOWTO-FEATURES section 2.

[[help-explains-coverage]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.help' names every selected case the last coverage run left without a record, read from the local trace of each directory.
'hwut.help --help'; coverage D-41.

[[help-explains-cap-refusal]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.help' says what the next run fails 'uncapped', for want of an enforceable cap: the caps, the utility that is missing and how it is installed, the 'hwut-root.conf' entry that acknowledges.
RATIONALE E-135.

[[sanitize-propose-acts-on-nothing]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.sanitize.propose' acts on nothing and runs nothing; '-o <file>' writes what stdout would carry.
README 'hwut.sanitize.propose'; RATIONALE E-18, E-125.

[[sanitize-session]]
documents: services/README.txt
A 'TMP/session/' that outlived its run is proposed for 'remove'.
README 'hwut.sanitize.propose'; 'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (--session).

[[sanitize-lock]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A lock whose holder is gone, or which cannot name one, is proposed for 'remove'; a live lock is never proposed and 'remove' refuses it by name.
RATIONALE E-18; 'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (--lock).

[[sanitize-out]]
documents: services/README.txt
An 'OUT/' directory holding files is proposed for 'remove', unless a live run holds its directory, which is said on stderr.
'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (--out).

[[sanitize-transient]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The transient roots 'OUT/' and 'TMP/' are proposed whole only under '--transient', never in a bare proposal.
RATIONALE E-24; 'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (--transient).

[[sanitize-orphans]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Records of an application or a choice the configuration no longer offers are proposed for 'forget'; a record whose application still stands and cannot be explored is unreachable, never proposed, and 'forget' refuses it.
README 'hwut.remove.propose' (the judgement is sanitize's); RATIONALE E-18, E-125.

[[sanitize-move]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
An orphan whose application stands in one other directory with nothing recorded of it there is proposed for 'move'; where it is recorded there already, or stands in several places, the 'forget' line carries a note.
RATIONALE E-130, E-131.

[[sanitize-books]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'--books' proposes a 'book' for a nominal the book lacks and for a book entry called aspirant while a nominal stands; an aspirant without a nominal is no disagreement, and applying moves nothing in 'GOOD/'.
README 'WHAT ACCEPTANCE MEANS'; RATIONALE E-41, E-125.

[[sanitize-constraints]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'--constraints' proposes a 'remark' for each nominal that contradicts its own constraints; applying writes the finding into the nominal and stains the book, and a finding already written is not proposed again.
RATIONALE E-123, E-125.

[[sanitize-target]]
documents: services/README.txt
'--target <name>' proposes one 'run' line for the project's own verb; a target no directory binds is refused by name.
README 'hwut.sanitize.propose'; 'hwut.sanitize.propose --help' (--target).

[[sanitize-notes-on-stderr]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
What cannot be proposed -- a directory a live run holds, a case not judged because exploration faulted or the wish hides cases -- goes to stderr as 'NOTE:', so the proposal holds commands and comments only.
README 'hwut.sanitize.propose'; RATIONALE E-129.

[[sanitize-apply]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.sanitize.apply <file>' does every command the file still holds, in order; a line under '#' is not done, a line that spells no command refuses the whole file, and the report closes on 'Done <n>/<m>'.
README 'hwut.sanitize.apply'; RATIONALE E-125.

[[sanitize-command]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.sanitize <command> <entity>' does one line of a proposal: done, nothing to do, or refused by the tree.
README 'hwut.sanitize'; RATIONALE E-125.

[[sanitize-judges-again]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Every command judges again before it acts: what the tree no longer finds insane -- a lock that came alive since the proposal -- is refused.
README 'hwut.sanitize.apply'; RATIONALE E-125.

[[sanitize-rerun-trigger]]
A command that acted touches every test application of its directory, so every refreshing face runs it next time.
'hwut.sanitize --help' (THE RE-RUN TRIGGER).

[[remove-test]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.remove <test>' forgets a test whole -- nominals, candidates with every sidecar, the book entry, the register id -- every choice its records name included.
README 'hwut.remove'; RATIONALE E-12, E-53, E-127.

[[remove-choice]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.remove <test> <choice>' forgets that one choice and no more; a third word is refused.
README 'hwut.remove'; RATIONALE E-53.

[[remove-author-untouched]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Removal never touches the test application, its 'hwut.conf' or anything under 'OUT/'.
README 'hwut.remove'; RATIONALE E-12.

[[remove-asks]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Without '--dont-ask' the whole list is shown, and anything but yes removes nothing.
README 'hwut.remove'; RATIONALE E-68.

[[remove-unknown-no-error]]
documents: services/README.txt
A test that is not in the book is no error: there is nothing to forget, and the face says so.
README 'hwut.remove'.

[[remove-takes-stain]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The stain goes with the book entry: a stained test removed runs again.
'hwut.remove --help' (WHAT REMOVAL IS FOR); RATIONALE E-12.

[[remove-names-features]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.remove' names the features left without a linked test run.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 5; RATIONALE E-140.

[[remove-propose]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.remove.propose -o <file>' removes nothing and writes every test run whose record lost its ground -- 'app absent', 'choice not offered', 'possibly moved to <dir>' -- and never an aspirant.
README 'hwut.remove.propose'; RATIONALE E-45, E-126.

[[remove-apply]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.remove.apply <file>' forgets what the file still names, one case per call, a line under '#' kept, and closes on 'Forgotten <n>/<m>'.
README 'hwut.remove.apply'; RATIONALE E-126.

[[rename-follows-name]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.rename <app> -to <app'>' renames a test whole and '<app> <choice> -to <choice'>' one choice: nominals, candidates, the book entry and the register entry follow the name.
README 'hwut.rename'; RATIONALE E-16, E-46.

[[rename-across-directories]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A fresh name carrying a path carries the test into that directory: the source register retires the id, the target issues a fresh one, the book entry is adopted by the target.
README 'hwut.rename'; RATIONALE E-46.

[[move-is-rename]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/intend/22-moving-and-renaming.txt
'hwut.move <app> <app'>' is 'hwut.rename <app> -to <app'>', word for word.
README 'hwut.move'; RATIONALE E-46; intend 22, 6.1.

[[rename-collision-refused]]
documents: services/README.txt
A fresh name that already stands in the target's book or register is refused before anything moves.
README 'hwut.rename'.

[[rename-says-never-edits]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The application file, its '@hwut' block and any 'hwut.conf' apps section are never edited: what disagrees is said as NOTE lines before 'Proceed?'; '--no-warning' drops the notes, '--silent' all but a refusal, and a file under both names is refused.
README 'hwut.rename'; RATIONALE E-48.

[[rename-stain-follows]]
A rename is no fresh start: the book entry moves with its recorded runs and any stain.
'hwut.rename --help' (A RENAME IS NOT A FRESH START).

[[labels-follow]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Label entries follow a test or a choice that is renamed, carried into another directory, or removed.
README '_follow.py'; RATIONALE E-20.

[[move-features-follow:retired]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.move' into another TEST directory carries the features the test links to into the target's feature file, and their leaves in 'hwut-root-features.conf' follow to the target's directory id.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 5; RATIONALE E-140; spec 23 section 7.

[[rename-stages-in-vcs]]
documents: adm/WORK/intend/22-moving-and-renaming.txt
Where hwut knows the version control system, a move stages the touched paths and asks about committing, or commits under '--commit'.
intend 22, 6.2 (ruled 2026-09-12, stated there as NOT BUILT).

[[rename-copies-without-vcs]]
documents: adm/WORK/intend/22-moving-and-renaming.txt
Where the version control system is not known, hwut copies instead of moving, provides a remove script and leaves a note in the book's header.
intend 22, 6.3 (ruled 2026-09-12, stated there as NOT BUILT).

[[labels-query-here]]
'hwut.labels.query' prints its targets relative to where the caller stands, so its pipe into '--wishlist' selects the same runs from the root and from a test directory.
'hwut.labels.query --help'; ruled 2026-10-09 (f-7), entered in no RATIONALE of services.

[[execute-target]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.execute <target>' runs the script each directory's 'hwut.conf' binds to the target, in every directory that binds targets, and passes everything after the target name to the script untouched.
README 'hwut.execute'; RATIONALE E-7.

[[execute-phase-one]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Phase one settles the plan and a refusal there costs no execution: a directory is refused where the file is absent, not executable, or its she-bang's interpreter is; '-i' skips it, '--default=<script>' fills absence, '+x' sets the executable bit.
README 'hwut.execute' (TWO PHASES); RATIONALE E-7.

[[execute-exit-codes-inform]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
Targets are not tests: the scripts' exit codes are reported and decide nothing.
README 'hwut.execute'; RATIONALE E-7.

[[execute-locked-directory]]
documents: services/README.txt
Each directory is executed under its lock: where another live run holds it, the script does not run, and the face says so and fails.
README 'hwut.execute' (Phase two).

[[execute-quiet]]
documents: services/README.txt
'-q' drops the per-directory output and leaves the summary.
README 'hwut.execute'.

[[execute-nothing-bound]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.execute' answers 3 where no directory binds any target.
README 'hwut.execute'.

[[execute-standard-target-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A standard target such as 'on_entry' inside 'target { }' is refused by name, where it stands.
RATIONALE E-7.

[[execute-command]]
'hwut.execute -c "<commands>"' runs the commands by 'sh -c' in every test directory in walk order, narrowed by '--dir'; '--default' and '+x' are refused beside it and no word may follow.
'hwut.execute --help'.

[[cov-run-records]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.cov.run' runs the wish's cases under their coverage tool and says per case '[REC]' or why no record stands; no verdict is determined and the book does not move.
README 'hwut.cov.run'; RATIONALE E-133; coverage D-38.

[[cov-run-output]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
The output directory holds the test run ids, the groups of runs and one file per source with every covered range annotated by who executed it; it is emptied at every start, a directory of somebody else's files is refused, and without '-o' and nobody to ask the run is refused.
'hwut.cov.run --help' (THE OUTPUT DIRECTORY); coverage D-42.

[[cov-run-wish]]
documents: services/README.txt
A wish narrows the coverage run, and a wish that asks for nothing is EMPTY.
README 'hwut.cov.run' ('the wish's cases').

[[cov-run-trace]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
A coverage run leaves a local trace per case under 'TMP/'; a later run replaces the rows of the cases it selects and keeps the others.
'hwut.cov.run --help'; coverage D-41.

[[cov-run-serial]]
documents: engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Under 'hwut.cov.run' one test runs at a time in a directory, whatever '--jobs' says.
coverage D-22.

[[cov-run-cap-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.cov.run' exits 1 where a case failed 'uncapped', for want of an enforceable cap.
RATIONALE E-135.

[[cov-to-humans]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.cov.conv.to_humans' shows a coverage file in its text spelling and '--to binary' turns it back, byte for byte; a record that cannot be read is refused by name.
README 'hwut.cov.conv.to_humans'.

[[cov-to-lcov]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.cov.conv.to_lcov' renders the output directory as one lcov tracefile -- DA, FN/FNDA, BRDA -- and says in the tracefile what lcov cannot carry.
README 'hwut.cov.conv.to_lcov'; coverage D-43.

[[cov-to-html]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.cov.conv.to_html' renders the output directory as pages: an index over sources and measures, and per source every line coloured with its points and the test runs that executed it.
README 'hwut.cov.conv.to_html'; coverage D-44.

[[cov-to-xml-json]]
documents: services/README.txt
'to_cobertura' and 'to_jacoco' render the output directory as XML reports, 'to_json' as lossless JSON.
README 'hwut.cov.conv.to_cobertura, to_jacoco, to_json, ...'.

[[cov-to-tex-pdf]]
documents: services/README.txt
'to_tex' renders the output directory as a LaTeX document of the package's macros -- '--style' loads a reader's package, '--body-only' writes the body alone -- and 'to_pdf' compiles it.
README 'hwut.cov.conv.to_cobertura, to_jacoco, to_json, to_tex, to_pdf'.

[[cov-measures-accounted]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
Every converter renders each registered measure or names it as unrendered in its output.
README (EVERY CONVERTER IS A VISITOR); coverage D-29, D-44.

[[cov-formats]]
documents: services/README.txt, engine/coverage/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.cov.formats' prints the table of coverage tools and formats this build reads, generated from the registry.
README 'hwut.cov.formats'; coverage D-13.

[[affected-runs]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.affected' takes a unified diff and names the test runs that executed a changed line as wishlist lines 'hwut.run --wishlist' takes; a directory that is no coverage output is refused by name.
README 'hwut.affected'; 'hwut.affected --help'.

[[about-page]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.about' prints the version, the licence and the home, then where this installation stands, its Python and its platform; it needs no tree.
README 'hwut.about'; RATIONALE E-138.

[[about-version]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.about --version' prints the version alone, one bare line.
README 'hwut.about'; RATIONALE E-138.

[[pype-filter]]
documents: services/README.txt
'hwut.pype SCRIPT' filters stdin, or named input files read as one stream, and the interpreter's stdout passes through untouched.
README 'hwut.pype'; 'hwut.pype --help'.

[[services-pype-shebang]]
documents: services/README.txt, bin/README.txt
A '#! /usr/bin/env hwut.pype' she-bang line reaches the face by PATH.
README 'hwut.pype'; bin/README 'hwut.pype'.

[[pype-door]]
documents: services/README.txt
No argument naming an existing script, or a dangling '--pype-dir', is refused with the usage and status 2; a script that does not parse is the interpreter's own line and status 1.
README 'hwut.pype'.

[[pype-exit-passes]]
documents: services/README.txt
'sys.exit(n)' inside a python block leaves the face with the script's own number.
README 'hwut.pype'.

[[dev-container-build]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.dev.podman.build' builds the image where none stands and anew under '--rebuild', at the tree's root; a failing build is a fault.
README 'hwut.dev.podman.build'; RATIONALE E-134.

[[dev-container-enter]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.dev.podman.enter' opens a shell or runs one command inside the container, the root mounted read-write at its own path, the working directory and the user kept; a missing image names the build face.
README 'hwut.dev.podman.enter'; RATIONALE E-134.

[[dev-container-no-engine]]
Where neither podman nor docker stands, the container faces fault by name.
'hwut.dev.podman.build --help'.

[[renovate-report]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.renovate' says how hwut 1.0's words would be carried into 2.0's -- the title and '--not' lines of 'hwut-info.dat', 'numeric', 'whitespace_eqv', 'slash_eqv' -- and writes nothing.
RATIONALE E-99; 'hwut.renovate --help'.

[[renovate-apply]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.renovate --apply' writes the translation, every other key, comment and line of a file staying as it was.
RATIONALE E-99; 'hwut.renovate --help'.

[[renovate-keeps-stated]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A key the directory already states is never overwritten: the relic's value is reported as 'kept'.
'hwut.renovate --help' (WHAT IT LEAVES).

[[renovate-slash]]
documents: engine/compare/RATIONALE.txt
The retired 'slash = true' in any 'tolerance { }' becomes an 'eq_pattern', and 'slash = false' is removed.
'hwut.renovate --help'; compare C-18.

[[ignore-named-paths]]
documents: services/lib/config/ignore.py, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.config.ignore <path>...' writes each file's name under 'ignore' in the 'hwut.conf' of its own directory, once, touching no other key, comment or line, and writes a conf holding only that block where none stands.
The face's help; X-SILENT (COST); services E-48.

[[ignore-from-wishlist]]
documents: services/lib/config/ignore.py, engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--wishlist <file>' ignores every path of a list file, '#' lines and blanks dropped and './' read as the list's own directory, from wherever the face is called.
The face's help; exploration README section on wallflowers; X-SILENT.

[[settle-wallflowers]]
documents: services/lib/config/ignore.py, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'--wallflowers' ignores the files of every wallflower list below the caller and removes each list once its files are settled.
The face's help; X-SILENT.

[[refused-file-is-wallflower]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A backup-shaped file is ignored and is no wallflower: not in the list, nothing to settle.
X-SILENT, AMENDED 2026-10-04.

[[report-feeds-ignore]]
documents: services/lib/report/wallflowers.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.report.wallflowers' prints the wallflowers one path per line, relative to the caller, as valid input to 'hwut.config.ignore'; once they are ignored it finds none.
The help of 'hwut.report.wallflowers'; services E-116.

[[ignore-asks-first]]
documents: services/lib/config/ignore.py, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.config.ignore' shows what it would write and asks before writing, unless '--dont-ask' stands.
The face's help ('--dont-ask'); X-SILENT (COST).

[[ignore-refusals]]
documents: services/lib/config/ignore.py
A path naming a directory or a file that does not exist, an unknown option, '--wishlist' without a file and a list that cannot be read are refused by name, and nothing is written.
The face's help, last paragraph.

[[show-defaults-merged]]
documents: services/lib/config/show.py, engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
'hwut.config.show' shows a directory's 'app_defaults' merged leaf by leaf over the root's; a directory without a conf of its own shows the root's alone.
The help says 'defaults resolved'; the 'app_defaults' scope is stated in the exploration README.

[[show-provenance]]
documents: services/lib/config/show.py, services/README.txt
'--provenance' names, for every stated value, the file and the line it stands on.
The face's help; services README, 'hwut.config.show'.

[[stray-parameter-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, services/lib/config/show.py
A test parameter written at the root of a conf, outside 'app_defaults' and 'apps', is reported as a fault by name with its position, not silently dropped.
Services E-99 ('a root tolerance is refused'); the help: a specification carrying a fault yields its faults.

[[init-writes]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.init' writes 'hwut-root-features.conf' beside 'hwut-root.conf' -- a header of comments, an id per directory whose TEST directory defines features, every feature an unplaced leaf under its first external id -- and refuses where the file stands or files of the past stand.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 8; RATIONALE R-96.

[[register-adds:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.register' adds below a directory what has no id or no line, prints every line it adds, names every disagreement with the line that would heal it, acts on none of them and never retires; its exit status says whether everything agrees.
spec 23 VERSION 10 sections 7, 8; RATIONALE R-96.

[[tree-prints]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.tree' prints the aggregates and their leaves, below a base where one is named; '--list' one '<tree path>/<external id>' per leaf, '-b' the aggregates, '-e' the external ids; retired claims are not printed.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 8; RATIONALE R-96.

[[retire-moves]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.retire' moves a leaf, or an aggregate with everything below it, into 'retired' as it was -- its line and the definition from its TEST directory -- and a retired id is never bound again.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 3; RATIONALE R-96.

[[move-follows:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.move' renames a directory and rewrites every 'directory_map' line at or below it in one step; the ids, and every external id, stay.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 8; RATIONALE R-96.

[[keys-edited:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.features.key.add' and 'key.remove' change the keys a claim states itself -- a leaf in its TEST directory, an aggregate in the central file -- and '--all' takes a key off every claim stating it.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 9; RATIONALE R-96.

[[sanitize-proposes:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Where 'hwut-root-features.conf' stands, 'hwut.sanitize.propose --relations' proposes what the one detector pairs as lines of 'hwut.sanitize' -- 'register', 'redirect', 'repoint', 'fold', the old value in the note -- and says what it cannot pair as a note, never as a line.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 7; RATIONALE R-96.

[[sanitize-applies:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'hwut.sanitize.apply' and 'hwut.sanitize <verb>' do 'register', 'redirect', 'repoint' and 'fold' only where the detector proposes that line now; a redirect that is half of a swap takes its mate along; a line proposed no more is NOTHING.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 7; RATIONALE R-96.

[[run-hints-registration]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Where 'hwut-root-features.conf' stands and features defined below the run's directory have no line in it, 'hwut.run' ends with one line counting them and naming 'hwut.features.register', its exit status untouched; 'hwut.help' names each of them.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 7 (o-5 amended); RATIONALE R-96.

[[report-reads-registry:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Where 'hwut-root-features.conf' stands, 'hwut.report.features' prints its tree -- aggregates with their state and counts, leaves IMPLEMENTED ok / IMPLEMENTED fail / OPEN with internal id, effective keys and runs -- the unplaced leaves, the retired claims, and outside the relation what is defined and not registered and what the detector finds; asked below the top, only what stands below.
spec 23 VERSION 10 sections 5, 6; RATIONALE R-96.

[[document-reads-registry:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Where 'hwut-root-features.conf' stands, the feature document ('--html', '--ods') holds a row per leaf under its external id, its aggregate and internal id in the columns of component and published id, its state on two axes, the tree of aggregates with their state and counts and the unplaced, and the state boxes of those states; a document of the files of the past keeps its bytes.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 5; RATIONALE R-96.

[[wish-reads-registry:retired]]
documents: adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Where 'hwut-root-features.conf' stands, '-F' selects by tree path (an aggregate brings its subtree; '*', '**', '{a,b}', a bare word anywhere, the lines of 'hwut.features.tree --list') or by internal id, '-e/--feature-ext' by external id, one union, '-k' over the effective keys narrowing it; a retired id and an external id naming nothing are refused by name.
spec 23 VERSION 10 section 11; RATIONALE R-96.

[[registry-spans-bubbles:retired]]
A bubble without a 'hwut-root-features.conf' of its own belongs to the nearest registry above it: registered, viewed and selected there, from above and from inside; a bubble with one of its own is not walked, and named.
services RATIONALE E-220.

[[registry-begun-in-a-bubble:retired]]
'hwut.features.init' inside a bubble begins a registry there, the bubble's own; the registry above then names that bubble's directories gone.
services RATIONALE E-220.

[[create-snapshot]]
documents: services/lib/labels/create.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.labels.create <label> <wish>' makes a new label from the runs the wish selects at that moment, names each run in its report, and writes them to 'hwut-root.labels' as literal targets, sorted and elided, never as a glob.
The help of 'hwut.labels.create'; services E-19.

[[create-refuses-standing]]
documents: services/lib/labels/create.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
Create refuses a label that already stands and names 'hwut.labels.add' as the verb for growing it.
The help of 'hwut.labels.create'; services E-19.

[[reserved-names-refused]]
documents: services/lib/labels/create.py, services/lib/labels/add.py
A grammar keyword, the universe 'all' and the standard label 'meta' are refused as the name of a label to create; 'all' is refused as a label to add to.
The helps' EXIT STATUS: 'is reserved', 'is no label'.

[[stateless-wish-refused]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, services/lib/labels/create.py, services/lib/labels/remove.py
Create, add and remove refuse a wish that states nothing: everything must be asked for in words, '--label all' or '--label <label>'.
Services E-19; the three helps.

[[label-in-wish-union]]
documents: services/lib/labels/create.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
A '--label <expr>' inside the wish of a label face selects the union of the named labels, narrowed by the rest of the wish.
The helps' SELECTION, '-l, --label'; services E-19.

[[empty-selection-no-label]]
documents: services/lib/labels/create.py
A glob that met nothing is warned of by name, and a wish that selects no run creates no label and answers EMPTY (3).
The help of 'hwut.labels.create', EXIT STATUS 3.

[[add-grows]]
documents: services/lib/labels/add.py
'hwut.labels.add' enrols the selected runs in a standing label, reports '+' for each newly enrolled and '=' for each already carrying it, and writes nothing where nothing was new.
The help of 'hwut.labels.add'.

[[add-refuses-absent]]
documents: services/lib/labels/add.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
Add refuses a label that does not stand and names 'hwut.labels.create'.
The help of 'hwut.labels.add'; services E-19.

[[standard-meta-stands]]
documents: services/lib/labels/add.py
The standard label 'meta' always stands: it is added to without a create.
The help of 'hwut.labels.add'.

[[labels-accumulate]]
The labels of an application's entry and of a choice's own entry in the labels file unite on that run.

[[remove-takes-off]]
documents: services/lib/labels/remove.py
'hwut.labels.remove' takes a label off the selected runs, reports '-' for each that carried it and '=' for each that did not; removing what is not there is no fault.
The help of 'hwut.labels.remove'.

[[empty-label-deleted]]
documents: services/lib/labels/remove.py
A label left without members is deleted and reported so, and a labels file left without entries is removed.
The help of 'hwut.labels.remove'.

[[remove-refuses-absent]]
documents: services/lib/labels/remove.py
Remove refuses a label that does not stand.
The help of 'hwut.labels.remove', EXIT STATUS 2.

[[comments-travel]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A hand comment glued to an entry of 'hwut-root.labels' is written back beside it when the file is rewritten and goes when the entry goes.
Services E-96, which supersedes E-19's COST.

[[list-counts]]
documents: services/lib/labels/list.py
'hwut.labels.list' prints every standing label with its number of members, sorted by name; a tree without labels prints nothing at all and answers EMPTY (3).
The help of 'hwut.labels.list'.

[[query-wishlist]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py
'hwut.labels.query <expr>' prints the runs the expression names as a wishlist, sorted and elided, which '--wishlist' reads back.
The help of 'hwut.labels.query'.

[[query-forms]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py
'--expand' prints one full target per line; '--labels' prints each run with the labels it carries.
The help of 'hwut.labels.query'.

[[query-expression]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
The expression composes labels with 'AND', 'OR', 'NOT', brackets and ',' for 'OR'; 'all' is every run, and a bare query is 'all AND NOT meta'.
The help of 'hwut.labels.query'; services E-19.

[[query-unknown-label-refused]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py
A label in an expression that does not stand is refused by name, never read as an empty set.
The help of 'hwut.labels.query'.

[[query-skips-orphans]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py
An entry naming a run the tree no longer offers is not printed by the query.
The help of 'hwut.labels.query', 'THE DOMAIN'.

[[broken-file-is-fault]]
documents: services/lib/labels/list.py, services/RATIONALE.txt
A labels file that cannot be read is named as a FAULT (1); a writing face then writes nothing, and the list prints nothing.
The five helps, EXIT STATUS 1; services E-20 ('LabelFileError').

[[unreadable-command-refused]]
documents: services/lib/labels/query.py, services/lib/labels/list.py
An argument or option a label face does not take, two labels and two expressions are refused by name with the usage line, status 2.
The five helps, EXIT STATUS 2.

[[take-replaces-target]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A take replaces the whole target range of GOOD by the taken OUTPUT lines, line-wise, the two counts free to differ.
README 'the keyed session'; RATIONALE E-61.

[[region-take]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'a' takes the region under the cursor into its associated region of GOOD.
README 'THREE TAKES'; RATIONALE E-61.

[[unaccepted-region-split]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A take inside an 'unaccepted' region splits it around what was taken, and a region emptied by takes is removed with its framing.
README 'the keyed session'; RATIONALE E-61, E-75.

[[other-regions-never-cut]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A take never cuts a region of another kind: it takes such a region whole, markers included, or is refused, and a take touching two regions is refused.
RATIONALE E-61.

[[filler-per-output-line]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
An unaccepted region holds one filler line for every OUTPUT line it stands for.
RATIONALE E-64 ('the fillers pair 1:1').

[[label-shares-first-row]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
On the screen an unaccepted region's label stands on the row of its first filler, which is not drawn; GOOD's text is unchanged.
RATIONALE E-64.

[[take-paired-in-place]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The taken lines are paired where they stand; every OUTPUT line is drawn once, and a GOOD line whose partner is already drawn stands alone until the next alignment.
RATIONALE E-75.

[[local-reindex]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
After a take the pairing below is shifted and the taken stretch paired one to one, without inventing a tolerance or an analogy.
RATIONALE E-61 ('RE-ALIGNMENT IS EXPLICIT').

[[spent-lines]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
An OUTPUT line already copied is spent: no cursor stands on it and no take covers it again, until its copy is removed from GOOD.
RATIONALE E-63, E-86.

[[target-follows-cursor]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The target range in GOOD follows the OUTPUT cursor while untouched; entering the pane does not fix it, modifying it does.
README 'THE TARGET'; RATIONALE E-61.

[[closing-token]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The closing token '<hwut-end>' can be taken, and a take holding it cuts GOOD right after the taken lines.
RATIONALE E-77.

[[remove-from-good]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'd' removes from GOOD the marked lines, the whole region the cursor stands in, or the cursor's line -- never the closing token -- and splits no region.
RATIONALE E-86.

[[undo-redo-reset]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, services/README.txt
'u' restores the whole state before an act, a bulk take being one act; 'r' redoes until a new act forks the history; 'R' resets to how GOOD stood when the screen opened; 'c-z' and 'c-y' serve beside 'u' and 'r'.
RATIONALE E-89, E-117.

[[keymap-table]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
One table binds the keys to the acts, and the help screen is that table.
README 'THE SEAM'; RATIONALE E-61, E-92.

[[screen-titles]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The screen has no header row: OUTPUT stands left under 'OUTPUT of: <test> <choice>', GOOD right under 'GOOD', and the driven pane's title is marked.
RATIONALE E-90, E-95.

[[differ-count-and-jump]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
GOOD's title counts the OUTPUT lines still unpaired or differing, a gutter numbers OUTPUT's lines, and '<number>g' moves to that line.
RATIONALE E-91.

[[element-colours]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Both panes draw each line in the colours of the elements compare read, a differing piece marked, the cursor line included.
RATIONALE E-87, E-92, E-94.

[[element-status]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'w' and 'b' step through the cursor line's elements, and the foot says what the element stepped on is -- for a number, the interval compare tolerates.
RATIONALE E-87, E-90.

[[unpaired-line-marked]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A line without a partner stands on the 'unpaired' ground against a note on the other side; a marker's, a filler's and the token's rows keep their empty side.
RATIONALE E-142, superseding E-93 for a line with no partner.

[[foot-keys]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
The foot is left-aligned with the function keys first and whole hints dropped from the right; undo and redo are lit where their stack holds something and grey where not, and each window shows its own keys.
README 'THE FOOT'; RATIONALE E-121.

[[stderr-warning]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Where the case's stderr spoke and the test does not declare it ignored, the foot says so before the merge begins.
RATIONALE E-91, E-136.

[[tolerance-report]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
't' opens a report of what the tolerance in force lets pass or fails -- analogies, patterns that matched, constraints -- row by 'OUTPUT:GOOD' line pair; it scrolls over its rows and <enter> jumps to the row's line.
RATIONALE E-91 to E-95.

[[round-pairs]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The report and the count hold the pairs of the current alignment round only.
RATIONALE E-117 ('A ROUND'S PAIRS ARE THAT ROUND'S').

[[realign-on-change-only]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A round that changed nothing -- 'z' before a take, an edit or an editor that changed nothing or failed -- starts no new alignment and stays on the screen.
README 'THE TARGET'; RATIONALE E-117.

[[viewing-screen]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The screen for looking ('hwut.diff') shows the same panes with moving, search and help, and no key that changes GOOD or opens an editing pane acts.
RATIONALE E-79, E-118 (COST).

[[good-edited-in-place]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
'e' makes GOOD an editable text and <F5> makes the changed text the nominal and realigns; <F6> or 'c-e' edits it in $EDITOR; 'c-c' inside the edit means nothing.
README 'THREE TAKES'; RATIONALE E-117.

[[edit-undo-apart]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
In an editing pane 'c-z' and 'c-y' undo and redo the typed text on a stack of their own, apart from the merge's.
README 'THE TOLERANCE PANE'; RATIONALE E-117.

[[tolerance-pane]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
<F5> opens, from the merge or from the report, the tolerance held in memory as text: every key, '# default' where compare's own, the proposals commented below each key; <F5> again tries a changed text on the next round; <F6> edits it in $EDITOR.
README 'THE TOLERANCE PANE'; RATIONALE E-118.

[[pane-lower-line-stands]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
In the tolerance pane the lower of two lines of one key stands, so an uncommented proposal replaces the key above it.
README 'THE TOLERANCE PANE'; RATIONALE E-118.

[[pane-fault-kept-open]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
A tolerance text the header's reader refuses keeps the pane open with the fault named in the foot.
README 'THE TOLERANCE PANE'; RATIONALE E-118.

[[pane-discard]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
<Esc> closes an editing pane discarding what was typed: no round, nothing kept.
README; RATIONALE E-117, E-118.

[[leave-asks]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
's' asks whether to save GOOD and leave, 'q' whether to leave without saving, and only 'y' lets the asked act run; 'S' and 'Q' act unasked; a quit writes nothing.
RATIONALE E-89, AMENDED 2026-10-09 (r-11c).

[[propose-numeric-ratio]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
From the differing numbers of a round a 'numeric_ratio' is proposed: the largest relative deviation plus 10 %, where it exceeds the ratio in force and stays at most 1.
RATIONALE E-120.

[[propose-eq-pattern]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt
For words that differ two 'eq_pattern' alternatives are proposed, one by character class and one by edit operations, each shown only if it matches both words whole.
README 'THE TOLERANCE PANE'; RATIONALE E-120.

[[propose-whitespace]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Where lines differ in blanks only and 'whitespace' is off, 'whitespace = true' is proposed.
RATIONALE E-120.

[[propose-nothing-pattern]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
For a word standing on one side only a 'nothing' pattern of the word and the blanks after it is proposed.
RATIONALE E-120.

[[proposals-only-news]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
Nothing is proposed where nothing differs, and what is in force is not proposed again but kept in the proposed line.
RATIONALE E-120 ('each eq_pattern line holds the patterns in force first').

[[proposals-reach]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
With every proposal applied, compare finds the lines of the measured case equivalent, by class and by edit operations.
RATIONALE E-120 ('MEASURED on test-proposal.py applied').

[[run-disk-room]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/RATIONALE.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A case starts only where its disk has room for twice its 'disk_mb' above the root's 'free_disk_min_mb'; it waits for a case running on that disk, else fails 'no-disk', not run, its numbers in HINTS; the floor in a directory's own 'hwut.conf' is a fault.
engine/orchestrator/scheduler RATIONALE S-5; exploration R-99.

[[cross-directory-link:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
A test links a feature of another directory by its internal id '<directory id>:<name>': the leaf lists the run, and the matrix names it by external id; an external id, an unknown name or a retired leaf is said OUTSIDE; repoint, fold, rename and a move rewrite such links, and a move links by id instead of copying a feature still linked in the source.
engine/orchestrator/exploration RATIONALE R-101.

[[book-discrepancy-said-alike]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A test or choice the book records and the tree no longer declares is said alike by 'hwut.run' and 'hwut.report', with or without '--fail', in every format: the directory named from the root, one order by test then choice, the directory's date, the same word, category and phrase from one table.
RATIONALE E-160.

[[script-helpers-supervised]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'on_before_test' and 'on_after_test' run around each test run, told by 'HWUT_TEST' and 'HWUT_CHOICE', no verdict, an end other than exit 0 said; every script of a directory -- frame, helpers, the targets of 'hwut.execute' -- runs under the procsitter with the caps of 'script_caps { }'.
RATIONALE E-161.

[[book-defects-ch1-5]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The reader's D-27 to D-31 hold: play marks the reading under NO_COLOR, accept.apply names the directory from the root, a region's analogies stay private in the diff, a constraint broken by the output is the behaviour's failure, and 'hwut.diff | head' ends quietly.
RATIONALE E-162.

[[book-defects-round-2]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
The defects of the book writers' round 2 and of the agent swarm stand fixed: a signed binding binds, a goto in <entry> switches before the next line, a refused acceptance exits 1, a proposal names what it could not propose, a test application that cannot be called is named as such.
RATIONALE E-166.

[[accept-names-recording]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.accept' names what it blesses -- which face recorded it and when -- hints where the test's source changed since, and refuses a case with no recording.
RATIONALE E-165.

[[play-runs-handlers]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.play' runs the directory's 'on_entry', 'on_before_test', 'on_after_test' and 'on_exit' around the case it plays, told as 'hwut.run' tells them, and says an end other than exit 0 in the run's words.
RATIONALE E-207.

[[good-link-read]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A '.link' in GOOD/ holds its case against the '.txt' it names: 'hwut.good.link' writes it and the book entry, and the run judges the case against that text.
RATIONALE E-209; bookkeeper B-32.

[[good-link-writers]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
No writer writes through a link: 'hwut.accept' refuses a linked case by name, '--unlink' gives it its own '.txt'; 'hwut.merge' opens no session over it; the text named stays untouched.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[good-link-faces]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.good.link' reads as 'cp': text to case, texts to a test directory, directory to directory; a standing GOOD is listed and kept unless '--force'; a link as source, a link onto its own case, a target without its test and a target outside the link's bubble (a way out) are refused; 'hwut.good.link.show' says who links what.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[good-link-isolate]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.good.isolate' gives a linked case its own copy -- of a link, or of every link of a text; 'hwut.good.rebase' points every link of one text at another.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[good-link-remove]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
A '.txt' other cases link is not removed: 'hwut.remove' names the links and the three ways out; a linked case's removal takes its link and leaves the text.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[good-link-move]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.move' carries links: a moved linked case names its text from its new place, the links of a moved text follow it, and a move that would make a link reach out of its bubble is refused before anything moves; into a bubble below, the link follows.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[good-link-sanitize]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt
'hwut.sanitize.propose --links' finds a link that names nothing it may and a link beside its case's own '.txt'; 'unlink' heals both and refuses a sound link.
RATIONALE E-209.

[[platform-words-taken]]
'-p' and '--exclude-local' are taken by 'hwut.run' and 'hwut.play', and by 'hwut.sanitize.propose', which runs nothing; a '-p' no bubble requires is a NOTE, and '--exclude-local' alone is refused.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 2; intend/42 rulings 9, 12.

[[platform-words-refused]]
Every other face that reads the whole wish refuses '-p' and '--exclude-local' by name, naming the faces that take them.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 2.

[[console-bubble-not-available]]
A bubble whose required platforms none is specified stands in the final tree where the walk meets it, '[PLATFORM not available]', the reason beside; counted nowhere.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 6; intend/42 ruling 21.

[[console-bubble-not-reachable]]
A bubble whose one platform cannot be reached stands in the final tree, '[PLATFORM not reachable]', the reason beside; counted nowhere.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 6; intend/42 ruling 21.

[[console-bubble-measured-there]]
A directory measured on a platform names it once in the final tree, '(linux)' after its name; its verdicts count in RESULTS as local ones do.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 6.

[[good-link-inward]]
A link may name a text in a bubble below its own -- no way out, every way in: written, read by the run, named by 'hwut.good.link.show' and 'hwut.remove' of that text; a link inside a bubble naming a text outside it stays refused.
bookkeeper RATIONALE B-32, amended 2026-11-03 (plan/42 P-8).

[[book-defects-round-3]]
The author's findings of 2026-11-04 stand fixed: a trailing '/' on '--dir' is punctuation, a directory the wish never asked about is not explored and says nothing, and a silent run's stdout carries events alone.
services RATIONALE E-221.

[[pipe-filter]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
A pype script runs as a filter in an operating-system pipe -- named as argument or called as a she-bang script -- and turns lines arriving in any order into one deterministic text.
README PURPOSE, INVOCATION; MANUAL 1, 3.

[[input-file-arguments]]
Input files named after the script are read in order as one stream, and the trace names file and line of each.
Stated in no document; shown by the 'pipe' run only.

[[pattern-tokens]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A pattern is a sequence of constant strings, numbers, integers and globs, found anywhere in the line with any whitespace between tokens; a named token binds its value as float, int or str.
README PATTERN TOKENS; MANUAL 5.

[[glob-classes]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A glob covers one token and never crosses whitespace; it reads '*', '?', '[seq]', '[!seq]' and ranges, a leading ']' and an unclosed '[' being literal.
MANUAL 5.

[[anchors]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'<bol>' and '<eol>' demand the line's begin and end at their place in the pattern.
README PATTERN TOKENS; MANUAL 5 ANCHORS.

[[repeat-groups]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A repeat group matches its sub-pattern one or more times, counts the repetitions and collects indexed bindings into a dictionary, the index admitting '+'/'-' offsets; nesting and an index outside a group are refused.
README PATTERN TOKENS; MANUAL 5 REPEAT GROUPS.

[[one-handler-per-line]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
Per input line the handlers of the active mode are tried in definition order, the first whose cause holds fires alone, and its effects, 'and:' continuations included, run in definition order.
README DISPATCH; MANUAL 2, 4, 6.

[[else-handler]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The '<else>' handler fires when every match handler of the active mode failed.
README SCRIPT GRAMMAR, DISPATCH; MANUAL 6.

[[mandatory-else]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A mode that can be activated without an '<else>' in its effective list is refused -- at parse time, or at activation where only 'pype.goto()'/'pype.push()' reaches it; a pure mixin is exempt.
README DISPATCH; MANUAL 6 MANDATORY <else>.

[[flush-and-ignore]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'flush' emits the input line unchanged, also chained after a block, and 'ignore' consumes it; '<else> => flush;' makes a pass-through filter.
README KEYWORDS; MANUAL 6.

[[if-cause]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A handler may fire on a Python condition over the shared namespace instead of a pattern, in definition order and exempt from the disjointness law; a mode holding one cannot be inherited from.
README DISPATCH, INHERITANCE; MANUAL 6, 8.

[[shared-namespace]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
All Python blocks of all handlers and modes run in one namespace: bindings and assignments persist across lines and mode switches.
README NAMESPACE; MANUAL 6.

[[pype-line-information]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The 'pype' object tells a block the current line, its 1-based number, the seconds since reading began and the bindings of the current line.
README DISPATCH; MANUAL 6 THE 'pype' OBJECT (line, line_n, time, bindings).

[[pype-mode-information]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The 'pype' object tells a block the script's file name, the name of the active mode and the depth of the mode stack.
MANUAL 6 THE 'pype' OBJECT (file_name, mode_name, stack_depth).

[[mode-switch]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'goto' as effect or 'pype.goto()' in a block switches the active mode; the old mode's '<exit>' handlers fire, then the new mode's '<entry>' handlers.
README MODES; MANUAL 7 SWITCHING, <entry> / <exit>.

[[switch-request-order]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A switch request is applied after the handler's effects have completed, the last request wins, and a switch to the active mode does nothing.
README MODES; MANUAL 7 SWITCHING.

[[default-mode]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
Bare handlers form the nameless default mode, which is the start mode wherever it stands in the file and cannot be switched into once left; without it the first-defined mode starts.
README MODES; MANUAL 7 DEFAULT MODE.

[[push-pop]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'push' stacks the active mode and switches, 'pop' returns to it -- also into the default mode -- with '<exit>' and '<entry>' firing as on a plain switch; 'pop' on an empty stack is an error.
README MODES; MANUAL 7 PUSH / POP.

[[file-boundaries]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'<bof>' and '<eof>' handlers of the then-active mode fire once each; a run nests as '<entry>', '<bof>', the lines, '<eof>', '<exit>'.
README MODES; MANUAL 7 <bof> / <eof>.

[[terminal-token]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A script's '<eof>' handler states the completeness of the stream by printing '<hwut-end>'; a script without it ends the stream bare.
MANUAL 1b.

[[no-reserved-mode-names]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
No word is reserved as a mode name: effect and cause words are lawful mode names, and an effect lacking its ';' or its argument is refused.
README KEYWORDS; MANUAL 4.

[[inheritance]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'is:' gives a mode every handler of its bases, transitively and from several bases; a handler arriving twice over a diamond is entered once, and the default mode may inherit.
README INHERITANCE; MANUAL 8.

[[shadowing-forbidden]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
Two pattern handlers of one effective list whose languages intersect are refused at parse time wherever they come from, decided exactly on the patterns; the error names the mode, both defining modes and both locations.
README INHERITANCE; MANUAL 8 SHADOWING IS FORBIDDEN; top README, pype section.

[[else-precedence]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
Of several '<else>' handlers the first of the effective list fires: own before inherited, bases in declaration order.
README INHERITANCE; MANUAL 8 THE <else> EXCEPTION.

[[inheritance-faults]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
An inheritance cycle and an unknown base are parse errors.
README INHERITANCE; MANUAL 8, 11.

[[imports]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'import:' merges another pype file: quoted path with environment variables expanded, searched beside the importer and then in the '--pype-dir' directories; an unquoted path, a missing file and a cycle are refused.
README INVOCATION, SCRIPT GRAMMAR; MANUAL 3, 4.

[[import-once]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A file imported more than once is entered once.
README SCRIPT GRAMMAR ('idempotent'); MANUAL 4.

[[script-faults-located]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A faulty script is refused with 'file:line' and the reason on stderr; an unknown mode name is an error.
README ERRORS; MANUAL 3, 11.

[[pype-exit-status]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The filter exits 0 after the whole stream and 1 on a script error, with nothing on stdout but the filtered stream.
README ERRORS; MANUAL 3, 6 <exit> STATUS.

[[exit-from-block]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'sys.exit(n)' in a block ends the run at once with status n.
README DISPATCH; MANUAL 1b, 6 <exit> STATUS.

[[block-exception-traceback]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
A Python exception inside a block ends the run with a traceback that points into the pype script.
README ERRORS; MANUAL 3.

[[dry-run]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
'--dry-run' parses and checks the script, reads no input, and reports 'dry run ok' with mode and handler counts, or the error.
README INVOCATION; MANUAL 3.

[[trace]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
'--trace' writes to stderr each input line against the handler it fired, the effects and the executed block lines as 'file:line:' -- '--trace-plain' with a plain starter, mode names restricting it -- while stdout stays the filter stream.
README INVOCATION; MANUAL 3.

[[example-input]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
'--example' prints an input with one sample line per pattern handler, walking the modes along static 'goto'/'push' effects; fed back, it drives the script.
README INVOCATION; MANUAL 3.

[[shipped-examples]]
documents: services/lib/pype/README.txt, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
Each of the five example scripts produces its recorded output from its input file.
README FILES; MANUAL 9.

[[wait-guard]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
An 'if' cause on 'pype.time()' fires when a wait exceeds its limit.
MANUAL 1, 9 (05-service-discovery); top README, pype section.

[[python-version-independence]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The interpreter's source uses no syntax that narrows the range of supported Python 3 versions.
MANUAL 10.

[[runs-under-pypy3]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
The interpreter runs unmodified under pypy3.
MANUAL 10 PYPY3.

[[hocon-separators]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
Blanks, newlines and commas separate entries and list items alike; a comma is required nowhere, so several entries may share one line.
Section 1.

[[hocon-objects-and-lists]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
Nested objects, lists and objects inside lists are read; an entry binds with '=' or ':', and a brace directly after a key binds without a sign.
Section 1.

[[hocon-keys]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
A dot inside a key is a literal character, never a path, and a key holding a blank is written in quotes.
Sections 1, 2.

[[hocon-strings-quoted]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
A string value carries double quotes, inside which blanks, comment markers and binders are content and the escapes are resolved; an unquoted word is a fault that shows the word in the quotes it wants.
Section 2.

[[hocon-text-of-several-lines]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
A text between three double quotes is taken as written, its common indentation and the blank lines at both ends removed; one that never closes is a fault.
Section 2.

[[hocon-numbers]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
Numbers are read in decimal, hexadecimal, binary, octal and strict roman spelling, with '_' between digits entering no value and a sign that may stand apart; nothing is computed, and what spells no number is a string that wants quotes.
Section 3.

[[hocon-booleans-and-nothing]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'true'/'yes' and 'false'/'no' are booleans; 'null', 'nil', 'none', 'nihil' and a value left empty all read as nothing.
Section 4.

[[hocon-comments]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'#' and '//' begin a comment to the end of the line, also after a value; a comment never becomes content.
Section 5.

[[hocon-refused-by-name]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'include', a '${...}' substitution and '+=' are each refused with a message of their own that says what does not exist.
Section 6.

[[hocon-duplicate-key]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
A duplicate key is a fault: the first stands, the second is refused.
Section 6.

[[hocon-faults-are-values]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt, doc/HOWTO-HWUT-BRAINSTORM.txt
The parser never raises and never hangs: it collects the faults, recovers and completes, so a text with several mistakes reports them all.
README head, section 7; HOWTO-HWUT-BRAINSTORM 9 (a parser is fuzzed).

[[hocon-positions]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
Every key, value, block and fault carries its line and column, file-relative also where the text stands inside a comment block of another language.
README head, sections 7, 8.

[[runner-command-line]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
A test application with the HwutRunner front end answers '--hwut-info' with its info block, runs the choice named as argument, and without argument lists the choices and reads an index.
Section 1.

[[runner-info-block]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
The info block holds the title, CHOICES, the HAPPY patterns, 'SAME;' where asked, and always 'INTERACTIVE;'.
Section 1.

[[runner-coroutine-choices]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
A choice's test function may be a plain function or a coroutine function; the key 'None' names the single choice-less test.
README head.

[[runner-interactive-session]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt
With '--interactive' the application stays loaded and runs choices on 'run <choice> <sink-out> <sink-err>' from stdin, answering 'done', 'fail', 'info:' and 'bye' on its original stdout.
README section 2; RATIONALE entries 1, 2.

[[runner-interactive-byte-exact]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
A choice run interactively writes byte for byte what it writes in normal mode, so one set of GOOD files serves both.
RATIONALE entry 1; README section 5.

[[runner-two-sinks]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt
Per choice stdout and stderr go to two separate files named by the framework, and processes the choice spawns inherit them.
README section 2; RATIONALE entries 2, 3.

[[runner-stray-output]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt
Output written outside a running choice lands in a stray sink and never in the protocol channel.
README section 2; RATIONALE entry 4.

[[runner-done-status]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
'done' carries the choice's status: 0 on return, n on 'SystemExit(n)', 1 on an exception with its traceback in the error sink.
README section 2.

[[runner-done-barrier]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt
When 'done' is readable every write of the application to the sinks is flushed, the sinks are closed and descriptors 1 and 2 are restored.
README section 4; RATIONALE entry 6.

[[runner-process-scheme]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt, support/test_writing/python/RATIONALE-hwut_runner.txt
With 'parallel="process"' each 'run' forks a child, choices run concurrently, 'done' arrives in completion order and each choice starts from the state at session entry.
README sections 3, 5; RATIONALE entry 5.

[[runner-session-end]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_runner.txt
'quit' and the end of stdin both drain the outstanding children and then answer 'bye'.
README section 2.

[[vterm-screen-text]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
Bytes fed to a Screen are read back as the rows' text and the cursor's place: UTF-8, a wide character over two columns, line wrap, cursor addressing and erasing.
README head, section 1.

[[vterm-cell-attributes]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt, doc/HOWTO-HWUT-BRAINSTORM.txt
A cell's attributes are read by name and print the same on every libvterm version, because their bits are measured and not copied; an attribute the library does not know reads False.
README sections 1, 3; HOWTO-HWUT-BRAINSTORM 9.

[[vterm-cell-colours]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
A cell's colours are handed out as red, green, blue -- an indexed colour resolved -- or as None for the terminal's default.
README section 1.

[[vterm-application-unaware]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
An application run in a Terminal finds a real terminal: three streams, a controlling terminal, the Terminal's size whatever LINES and COLUMNS said, TERM, and an answer to its cursor query; its exit status comes back.
README section 2, THE APPLICATION CANNOT TELL.

[[vterm-alternate-screen]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
A full-screen application's picture on the alternate screen does not mix with the lines printed before it.
README section 2.

[[vterm-keys]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
Text, named keys, function keys and keys with modifiers reach the application as a keyboard sends them.
README section 2, KEYS.

[[vterm-regions]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
Of the declared regions only one whose text changed is told, once per pause, in declaration order; a region's rows can also be asked directly.
README section 2, on_change, region_text.

[[vterm-raw-events]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
The raw layer reports damage, cursor, property and bell events as they happen, and the terminal's answer while nothing runs.
README section 2, on_event, answer.

[[vterm-expect]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'expect' waits until a text stands on the screen and raises 'TimeoutError', carrying the screen, where it never does.
README section 2.

[[vterm-wait-kills]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'wait' kills an application that does not end and raises 'TimeoutError'.
README section 2.

[[vterm-library-missing]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
A libvterm that cannot be loaded raises 'LibraryMissing', whose text carries the hint how to install or name the library.
README section 4.

[[vterm-library-probe]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'python3 hwut_vterm.py' says whether the library is found: its name and exit 0, or the detection message and exit 1.
README section 4.

[[random-stream-reproducible]]
The random stream yields the same integers, floats and coin flips for the same seed on every machine, over any range including a degenerate one.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[random-gauss]]
Gaussian draws, clamped draws and integer Gaussian draws are reproducible up to the last bits of the mathematics library.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[random-sampling]]
Sampling and stateless selection from a pool give the same elements for the same stream state.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[random-spread-selection]]
Selection of the not-yet-chosen spreads over a pool reproducibly; the memory is keyed on the candidate, not on its container.
Stated in no document; docstring of 'SelectionMarker' only.

[[timeline-schedule]]
A trigger fires at the ticks its drawn timeline names; empty and blank timelines and a dispatcher without triggers run without failing.
Stated in no document; module docstring of 'deterministic_timeline.py' only.

[[timeline-order-in-tick]]
Within one tick the lower digit fires first and equal digits fire in registration order.
Stated in no document; module docstring of 'deterministic_timeline.py' only.

[[timeline-termination]]
Every trigger is told the end of the timeline with its length as end tick, also when it never fired and also when the run was stopped early.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[timeline-yields]]
The dispatcher yields between firings, so a consumer acts on each item before the next tick.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[timeline-cancel-silent]]
Cancelling the task that carries the run announces no termination.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[timeline-trigger-contract]]
A trigger must provide 'fire'; 'on_termination' is optional and silent by default.
Stated in no document; shown by the test only.

[[fsm-text-model]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
A state machine is written as text -- 'A ---( event )---> B', chained, ':' for the state that began the line above, 'initial:', 'forbidden:', 'final:' -- read and written back alike; a model that cannot be meant is refused with its line.
Sections 1, 2.

[[fsm-breach-line]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
An event the model does not allow where the machine stands, and a forbidden state entered, each print one BREACH line naming the place, the event and what was allowed; the machine stays, or enters the forbidden state.
Section 3.

[[fsm-set-of-states]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
With 'DFA=False' an event may lead to several states and the machine stands in all of them at once.
Section 3.

[[fsm-model-questions]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
The model alone answers which states cannot be reached, which are dead ends, which forbidden states it lets the system reach, and the shortest event sequence into a state.
Section 4.

[[fsm-coverage-report]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
At the end a sorted report says how many states were visited and transitions taken, each transition taken or not, the states never entered, where the machine ended, and a breach where it ended outside the final states.
Section 5.

[[fsm-changing-law]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
While the machine runs, events may be disallowed and allowed -- in every state or one, for a block -- and states forbidden and permitted; a refused event says since when, forbidding the present state is a breach at once, the model's questions answer under the masks unless asked as written, and the report names what still stands.
Section 6.

[[space-product]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/space_iterator.py
'space_iterator.do' yields every combination of a basis -- one value of each dimension, in order -- the rightmost dimension changing fastest, as many points as the product of the dimensions' lengths.
The module's docstring.

[[space-edges]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/space_iterator.py
An empty basis yields one point, the empty one; a basis with an empty dimension yields nothing; a single dimension yields its values one by one.
The module's docstring, NOTES.

[[perl-random]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt
'Hwut::DeterministicStream' and 'Hwut::SelectionMarker' draw the stream 'deterministic_random.py' draws, seed for seed: the Perl test prints the Python test's GOOD files byte for byte, which it is held against by link.
README.txt, section 2; Hwut/DeterministicRandom.pm.

[[perl-timeline]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt
'Hwut::TriggerDispatcher' fires, orders and announces as 'deterministic_timeline.py' does, and its run -- a task stepped from yield to yield -- interleaves with a consumer and is cancelled as an asyncio task is: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; Hwut/DeterministicTimeline.pm.

[[perl-space]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt
'Hwut::SpaceIterator' yields the cartesian product 'space_iterator.py' yields, the rightmost dimension fastest, and its two edges alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; Hwut/SpaceIterator.pm.

[[perl-fsm]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
'Hwut::Fsm' reads the model text 'hwut_fsm.py' reads, refuses what it refuses with the same words, and prints the same breach lines, model findings and coverage report: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_fsm.txt.

[[perl-hocon]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'Hwut::Hocon' parses the HOCON subset into the tree 'hwut_hocon.py' builds -- positions, kinds, faults -- and gives the same verdict on 3000 mutated headers from the same seed: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_hocon.txt.

[[perl-vterm]]
documents: support/test_writing/perl/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'Hwut::Vterm' reads libvterm's screen, cells and events through FFI::Platypus as 'hwut_vterm.py' does through ctypes, runs an application on a pseudo-terminal it cannot tell from a person's, and names a missing library alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, sections 2, 3; ../python/README-hwut_vterm.txt.

[[c-random]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt
'DeterministicStream' and 'SelectionMarker' in C draw the stream 'deterministic_random.py' draws, seed for seed: the C test prints the Python test's GOOD files byte for byte, which it is held against by link.
README.txt, section 2; deterministic_random.h.

[[c-timeline]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt
'TriggerDispatcher' in C fires, orders and announces as 'deterministic_timeline.py' does, and its run -- a task stepped from step to step -- interleaves with a consumer and is cancelled as an asyncio task is: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; deterministic_timeline.h.

[[c-space]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt
'SpaceIterator' in C yields the indices of the cartesian product 'space_iterator.py' yields, the rightmost dimension fastest, and its two edges alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; space_iterator.h.

[[c-fsm]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
'FsmMachine' in C reads the model text 'hwut_fsm.py' reads, refuses what it refuses with the same words, and prints the same breach lines, model findings and coverage report: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_fsm.txt.

[[c-hocon]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'Hocon_parse' in C parses the HOCON subset into the tree 'hwut_hocon.py' builds -- positions, kinds, faults -- and gives the same verdict on 3000 mutated headers from the same seed: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_hocon.txt.

[[c-vterm]]
documents: support/test_writing/c/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'VtermScreen' and 'VtermTerminal' in C read libvterm's screen, cells and events through dlopen -- no header needed -- as 'hwut_vterm.py' does through ctypes, run an application on a pseudo-terminal it cannot tell from a person's, and name a missing library alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, sections 2, 3; ../python/README-hwut_vterm.txt.

[[cpp-random]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt
'hwut::DeterministicStream' and 'hwut::SelectionMarker' in C++ draw the stream 'deterministic_random.py' draws, seed for seed -- over the C core: the C++ test prints the Python test's GOOD files byte for byte, which it is held against by link.
README.txt, section 2; deterministic_random.hpp.

[[cpp-timeline]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt
'hwut::TriggerDispatcher' in C++ fires, orders and announces as 'deterministic_timeline.py' does, triggers being classes that override 'fire'; its run -- a 'hwut::Task' stepped from step to step -- interleaves with a consumer and is cancelled as an asyncio task is: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; deterministic_timeline.hpp.

[[cpp-space]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt
'hwut::space_iterator::do_' in C++ yields the points of the cartesian product 'space_iterator.py' yields, the rightmost dimension fastest, and its two edges alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; space_iterator.hpp.

[[cpp-fsm]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
'hwut::fsm::Machine' in C++ reads the model text 'hwut_fsm.py' reads, throws 'hwut::FsmError' where Python raises, with the same words, and prints the same breach lines, model findings and coverage report: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_fsm.txt.

[[cpp-hocon]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_hocon.txt
'hwut::hocon::parse' in C++ parses the HOCON subset into the tree 'hwut_hocon.py' builds -- positions, kinds, faults -- and gives the same verdict on 3000 mutated headers from the same seed: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_hocon.txt.

[[cpp-vterm]]
documents: support/test_writing/cpp/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'hwut::vterm::Screen' and 'hwut::vterm::Terminal' in C++ read libvterm's screen, cells and events -- loaded at run time, no header needed -- as 'hwut_vterm.py' does through ctypes, run an application on a pseudo-terminal it cannot tell from a person's, observe it by lambdas, and throw 'LibraryMissing' alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, sections 2, 3; ../python/README-hwut_vterm.txt.

[[java-random]]
documents: support/test_writing/java/README.txt
'DeterministicStream' and 'SelectionMarker' in Java draw the stream 'deterministic_random.py' draws, seed for seed: the Java test prints the Python test's GOOD files byte for byte, which it is held against by link.
