README.txt, section 2; ../python/README-hwut_hocon.txt.

[[fortran-vterm]]
documents: support/test_writing/fortran/README.txt, support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_vterm.txt
'Screen' and 'Terminal' in Fortran read libvterm's screen, cells and events through ISO_C_BINDING into the C port -- no header needed -- run an application on a pseudo-terminal it cannot tell from a person's, and name a missing library alike: held against the Python test's GOOD files by link.
README.txt, sections 2, 3; ../python/README-hwut_vterm.txt.

[[support-written]]
documents: services/lib/support/core.py
'hwut.support <language> <dir>' writes '<dir>/<language>' (the language's test writing support, its own TEST left out), '<dir>/TEST' (the demonstration in that language, every '.link' resolved into the nominal it names) and '<dir>/hwut-root.conf' (the template), from 'support/' in a clone or from the archive an installed hwut carries; and the written demonstration runs green as it stands.
The face's docstring; services RATIONALE E-217.

[[support-importable]]
documents: services/lib/support/core.py, support/test_writing/LANGUAGES.conf
The face ends with MAKE IT IMPORTABLE, the lines that make '<dir>/<language>' reach a test of one's own, and the line such a test writes; with the demonstration's marked 'HWUT-SUPPORT-PATH' line pointing nowhere and only those lines applied, the demonstration still runs green.
The face's docstring; 'support/test_writing/LANGUAGES.conf'; services RATIONALE E-217.

[[support-refused]]
documents: services/lib/support/core.py, services/lib/demo/python.py
A directory that is not empty, one word or three, a language that is none, and the retired 'hwut.demo.python' are refused by name, the last with its remedy 'hwut.support python <dir>'.
The face's docstring; services RATIONALE E-217.

[[support-faces]]
documents: services/lib/support/core.py
'hwut.support' offers exactly the languages of 'LANGUAGES.conf', each with a title and a demonstration ('support/demo/<language>/TEST'), and a word that names none is refused with the list.
services RATIONALE E-217.

[[platform-availability]]
A platform says whether it can run a bubble now: 'local' always; a 'container' where its engine stands on PATH and its image stands, or a recipe to build it from -- else one line naming what is missing.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 rulings 14, 16, 17.

[[platform-image-build]]
A container image that does not stand is built from the recipe at start, said once; a failed build is an ERROR naming the image and the recipe, with the build's last five lines, and the platform is not reachable.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 ruling 17.

[[platform-container-call]]
A bubble is run in the container by one engine call: the tree's root mounted at its own path, the bubble the working directory, the user kept, the far hwut.run silent with its events on stdout.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 rulings 20, 22.

[[platform-kind-keys]]
The helper names the kinds a conf may give and, per kind, the keys it knows beside 'name' and 'kind'; an unknown kind has none.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, sections 1 and 4; intend/42 rulings 14, 15, 16.

[[engine-imports-no-face]]
The platforms helper imports only the engine and the standard library -- nothing of the faces.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; adm/LAYERING.txt.

[[platform-stream-translated]]
The far run's events come back as near ones as they happen: tree-begun and tree-done dropped, each directory below the bubble, 'platform' added, the time kept; a line that is no event and a far status other than 0 or 1 each a fault.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 5; intend/42 ruling 20.

[[platform-stop-ends-run]]
'stop' ends a far run still standing when its reader stops, and the reading returns.
adm/WORK/spec/42-platforms.txt, section 4; intend/42 ruling 14; services E-55.

[[deploy-node]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/plan/RATIONALE.txt
An application stating 'deploy' gets one DEPLOY node: supported by the build actions building it, supporting every test of its file, covered by the exclusion sets covering that file.
README section 7; RATIONALE P-20; intend/26 o-47.

[[supports-transitive]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/scheduler/README.txt
A supporter ending bad leaves unsupported what it supports and what that supports in turn: a build broken leaves its deploy and the deploy's tests unsupported; a deploy broken leaves its tests.
README section 1; intend/26 o-47.

[[+cover]]
What a demonstration shows: one feature of hwut each, demonstrated from the port's language.
intend/44 P-a..P-g; T-j, T-k.

[[+cover-compare]]
The comparison: what a nominal tolerates.

[[+cover-region]]
The regions of an output and how each is compared.

[[+cover-pype]]
The pype: laws over the stream.

[[+cover-conf]]
The organisation of tests: headers, confs, choices, spaces.

[[+cover-screen]]
The terminal screen as seen.

[[cover-order-free-block]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
The lines between '##! potpourri' and '####' may arrive in any order; the same lines must stand on both sides.
README, Order, tamed: potpourri.

[[cover-fsm-law-broken]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
A door that breaks the model prints one 'BREACH' line per surprise -- an event not allowed where it stands, a forbidden state entered, an end outside the final states -- and the GOOD file holds them.
door.py; support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt, section 3.

[[cover-fsm-law-kept]]
documents: support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt
'door.pype' fires every 'door:' event of the log into the door's state machine; a door that keeps the model prints no breach, and the report names each transition taken or not and where the door ended.
door.py; support/test_writing/python/README-hwut_fsm.txt, sections 3, 5.

[[cover-law-breach-verdict]]
documents: ../README.md
'protocol.pype' on a stream that breaks the law names each breach -- answered twice, not answered, a response without request, a request after shutdown -- and prints 'law: BROKEN'.
README, Order, tamed: pype -- the 'printf ... | hwut.pype protocol.pype' example.

[[cover-order-law-by-pype]]
documents: ../README.md, services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt
'protocol.pype' between five concurrent clients and the comparison prints one verdict per exchange, sorted, and 'law: kept', whatever order the lines arrived in.
README, Order, tamed: pype, the law as states.

[[cover-pype-owns-terminal-token]]
documents: services/lib/pype/MANUAL.txt, ../README.md
The producer that feeds the pype prints no '<hwut-end>'; the script's '<eof>' handler closes the stream.
pype MANUAL 1b; README, output of 'protocol.pype'.

[[cover-analogies]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
A handle in '((...))' may take any value as long as it is the same value wherever the handle appears.
README, table of the GOOD file; demo.py, 'analogies'.

[[cover-constraints]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
A value bound as '((name: value))' is judged by the stated constraint expressions, over one value or over two, and not by the GOOD file's value.
README, table of the GOOD file; demo.py, 'constraints'.

[[cover-equivalence-pattern]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
Any text an 'eq_pattern' names is equivalent to any other text it names.
README, table of the GOOD file; demo.py, 'tolerance'.

[[cover-exact-comparison]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
Where no tolerance is stated the output must equal the GOOD file line by line.
README, Describing behaviour the way it happens; demo.py, 'plain'.

[[cover-numeric-ratio]]
documents: ../README.md, engine/compare/MANUAL.txt
A number passes when it lies within the stated ratio of the GOOD file's number.
README, table of the GOOD file; demo.py, 'tolerance'.

[[relation-judged:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
The 'childs' of a component's file and the map are judged against where the directories stand, each disagreement by kind: no-file, unlisted, moved, dangling, twin, no-map, unmapped, map-gone, map-twin, map-clash; a tree of TEST files only is not judged but for its map.
README section 13 (table of kinds); RATIONALE R-84.

[[relation-adapted:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt, doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
One adaption makes the files agree with the directories -- a file written, the 'childs' entry above entered, carried with its key after a move by hand or dropped, the map completed or repointed, a published id retired -- leaving comments and layout byte for byte.
README section 13; RATIONALE R-84; HOWTO-FEATURES section 5.

[[feature-wish-selects:retired]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt, engine/orchestrator/exploration/RATIONALE.txt
'-F' and '-k' choose the runs linked to the features an id glob names and whose effective keys satisfy an expression; a published id resolves through the map, a retired id or a key no feature carries is refused by name.
README section 13 ('feature_wish.py'); RATIONALE R-95.

[[report-features-components:retired]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
A component is printed with its id, title, what it does, how it serves its parent and what its parent says of it, its counts summed from below.
HOWTO-FEATURES sections 3, 4; spec 23 sections 3, 4.

[[report-features-alone-complete:retired]]
documents: services/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
Feature files alone are complete: ids and undescribed components are judged only where the tree relates by id.
RATIONALE E-144; spec 23 section 5.

[[sanitize-relations:retired]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, adm/WORK/spec/23-features.txt
'--relations' proposes one 'relate' per directory whose statement file disagrees with where it stands -- a move by hand noted 'moved from' -- and one 'unrelate' per dead 'childs' entry; 'relate' and 'unrelate' adapt the files and leave every other byte.
HOWTO-FEATURES section 5; RATIONALE E-140; spec 23 sections 6, 7.

[[accept-partial-first:retired]]
documents: services/README.txt, services/RATIONALE.txt, ../README.md
A first acceptance without '--force' records the candidate's shape with nothing decided -- one 'unaccepted' region per chunk -- and reports 'undecided'.
README 'ACCEPT IS PARTIAL BY DEFAULT'; RATIONALE E-60; README.md 'Accept'.

[[forest-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
'hwut-feature-forest.csv' is read line by line, '<id> : <child>, ...': comments, blanks and whitespace dropped; the roots are what stands as no child, in file order; a feature id is '[A-Za-z0-9_-]+', case kept and compared.

[[forest-faults-named]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A forest line of no shape, one naming no child, an id that is no id, a left side twice, a child under two parents and a circle are each a FAULT naming file and line; a forest that cannot be read stops every face of the features.

[[forest-prototypes-expand]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A line '+<prototype> : <member>, +<prototype>, ...' declares a prototype; a feature aggregating it, '<feature> : +<prototype>', holds its instance '<feature>+<prototype>' with each member as '<feature>+<member>' below it -- two-part names, the place from the prototypes; what a prototype may not do is a FAULT.

[[doc-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
'hwut-feature-doc.dat' is read in blocks headed '[[<id>]]': an optional 'documents:' line, then the prose -- its first line the title, the rest the doc; '[[<id>:retired]]' keeps a retired claim as it was.

[[doc-audiences-fold]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
'[[CUSTOMERS]]' declares the audiences; 'to' adds one to a feature and everything below it in the forest, 'not' takes an inherited one off -- documentation only, no face selects by an audience.

[[doc-faults-named]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
Text before the first heading, a heading of no shape, 'CUSTOMERS' as an id, an id documented twice, a retired id in a forest line and an audience not declared are each a FAULT naming file and line, an unknown name with 'did you mean'.

[[doc-prototype-blocks]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A member's block documents it in every instance, '[[+<prototype>]]' every instance of the prototype, '[[<owner>+<id>]]' one instance and wins; a block naming no prototype or no instance is a FAULT.

[[external-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
'hwut-feature-external-map.csv' maps our feature id to the outside's names, '<id> ; <their name>', several per id; their name is a free string, never checked.

[[external-faults-named]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A map line with no ';' or an empty side, and an id the forest does not hold, are a FAULT naming file and line, the id with 'did you mean'.

[[links-read]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
The relation holds every link of every run of a TEST directory, whatever id it names; the runs linking nothing; whether the directory takes part; the runs of each id over the whole tree with the verdict of 'GOOD/book.csv'.

[[view-states-fold]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A feature folds its own runs and its children's: IMPLEMENTED where it has either and no child is OPEN, its verdict the worst below it; one with neither is OPEN untested; the unplaced, the retired and the links outside are kept apart, whichever directory asks.

[[view-cut-by-id]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
The view kept to the subtree of an id, the features above it as headers folded and counted anew; several ids a union; no retired, no outside.

[[view-refused-named]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
No forest at the top refuses the view with '=> hwut.features.init'; a fault of the forest, the doc file or the map stops it, named.

[[feature-label-runs]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/plan/label.py
'f/<id>' holds the runs linked to the feature and to every feature below it in the forest; a glob holds those of every feature it matches, and is refused where it matches none; a retired id, a member and a prototype are refused, an unknown id with 'did you mean' -- or, not strict, holds no run.

[[detector-notes]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
The one detector notes a documented feature in no forest line, an undocumented root, a link to an id that stands nowhere or is retired -- with 'did you mean' where a known id is near -- and a prototype no feature aggregates; a non-root without a block is silent.

[[detector-names-past-files]]
documents: engine/orchestrator/exploration/README.txt
A TEST directory's 'hwut-features.conf', a 'hwut-root-features.conf' and a 'hwut-feature-map.conf' are named as files of the past, read by nothing; a bubble with a forest of its own is not walked.

[[register-notes]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
'hwut.features.register' names what the three feature files and the links disagree about, one NOTE each, and adds nothing; a tree that agrees: 'nothing disagrees'; a fault of a file is FAULT.

[[rename-follows]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
'hwut.features.rename <old> <new>' writes the id anew in one step -- a feature's, a member's or a prototype's: both sides of the forest's lines, every instance, the doc headings, the map's left column, every 'features = [...]' of the test headers and the confs; the outside's names are untouched; nothing is written where anything is refused.

[[forest-spans-bubbles]]
documents: doc/HOWTO-FEATURES.txt
A bubble without a forest of its own is the forest's above, called inside it too; a bubble with one begins its own features, is not walked from above and is NOTEd.

[[report-features-cut-by-id]]
documents: services/lib/report/features.py
A bare feature id cuts the page of 'hwut.report.features' to its subtree, the features above as headers; several are a union; an unknown or retired id is refused, '-F', '-e' and '-k' are gone.
